/* Career research: a deeper look at each career, written by Claude from cited sources and
   reviewed by Bix (pull request) before it goes live. Keyed by career id from careers.js or
   careers-bls.js. Unlike careers-bls.js, the monthly data rebuild never touches this file.
   Add or refresh careers with the weekly research task; see "Career research" in README.md.
   Check with: node tools/research.mjs check

   Fields (plain text, a few sentences each, no HTML):
     checked    date researched, YYYY-MM-DD
     pay        pay right now: BLS figures plus what job postings offer, with years
     growth     outlook: BLS projection and whether recent hiring news agrees
     ai         {rating: Low | Low–Medium | Medium | Medium–High | High, text: what AI does and doesn't do here}
     sentiment  what people in the job say (forums, social media, news), in our own words
     path       realistic ways in, usual steps and years, and what comes next
     notes      licenses, physical demands, unions, regional differences, red flags
     sources    [["Title", "https://..."], ...] at least 3 */
"use strict";
const CAREER_RESEARCH={
 "se":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $124,900 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $73,870 and the top 10% over $195,270. Pay is usually salary plus commission or bonus. Enterprise software roles pay well above the BLS median: a 2026 recruiter benchmark puts base at about $130k early on to $200–225k senior, plus $35–80k variable (roughly a 75/25 split).",
  growth:"BLS projects 3% growth from 2025 to 2035 (about average), with about 3,800 openings a year in a small field of roughly 51,600 jobs. Recruiters report demand is strongest in AI, cloud infrastructure, and cybersecurity, where experienced sales engineers are scarce.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI is taking over the prep work: researching prospects, drafting RFP answers and demo scripts, call notes, and simple self-guided demos. The human core stays: reading what a buyer really needs, live demos and workshops, handling technical objections, and earning trust over a long sales cycle. Expect fewer people doing routine demos and more value on deep product and industry knowledge."},
  sentiment:"People in the role like the pay, the mix of technical and people work, and being seen as a key part of closing deals. The most common complaint is workload and burnout, especially early and mid-career, followed by friction with account executives (worse at small companies) and keeping up with fast-changing AI products. Since pay depends partly on sales results, income can swing with the market.",
  path:"Most people come in from technical support, implementation, consulting, or software and systems roles at a company that sells technical products, usually after 1–3 years. A bachelor's degree (engineering, computer science, or business) is typical but not strictly required if you have sales and technical experience. Next steps are senior or principal sales engineer, solutions architect, or managing a sales engineering team.",
  notes:"Expect travel (sometimes several days a month) and some weeks over 40 hours. Product knowledge matters more than credentials; vendor certifications (for example cloud certifications) help, and NAASE offers a Certified Sales Engineer credential. Part of pay is tied to quota, so check how the variable pay is earned before accepting an offer.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Sales Engineers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/sales/sales-engineers.htm"],["Betts Recruiting: Enterprise sales engineer compensation trends for 2026","https://bettsrecruiting.com/blog/top-enterprise-sales-engineer-compensation-trends-for-2026/"],["Demoboost (vendor blog): How AI is changing presales and the solution engineer role in 2026","https://demoboost.com/blog/presales-solution-engineer-ai-era"],["NAASE: What sales engineers struggle with most today (Nov 2025 poll)","https://sales-engineering.org/what-sales-engineers-are-really-struggling-with-right-now/"]]},
 "impl":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS has no separate category for implementation consultants. The closest, Management Analysts, has a median of $101,860 (May 2025), with the lowest 10% under $60,640 and the top 10% over $171,640. Salary sites for the role itself vary: a 2026 SaaS benchmark puts implementation specialists at a $75,000 median base (middle half about $69k to $85k), while Indeed's average across implementation consultant postings is about $101,000 (2026). Enterprise platform consultants earn more.",
  growth:"Using Management Analysts as a stand-in, BLS projects 10.1% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), with about 94,100 openings a year. Hiring for the role itself depends on software sales: a 2025 survey of 560+ venture-backed software companies found 17% had cut professional services staff in the past year, fewer than cut sales roles. Data specific to implementation jobs is thin, so treat this as uncertain.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"AI is starting to suggest configurations, map and clean customer data, write test cases, draft training guides, and answer routine setup questions, so simple self-serve onboarding needs fewer people. The human core stays: learning how a customer's business really works, deciding how to configure the system, managing timelines and stakeholders, and calming nervous clients at go-live. Industry surveys report most onboarding teams plan to adopt AI tools."},
  sentiment:"Many people like the variety, solving real business problems, and seeing a customer go live on something they set up. Common complaints are juggling several projects at once, tight go-live deadlines set by sales promises, scope creep, and frustrated customers when things slip. Siloed tools and handoffs are a frequent pain point in surveys of onboarding teams. Evidence on mood is mostly anecdotal, so treat this as a general picture.",
  path:"People usually come from technical support, customer success, business analyst, or operations jobs, or from a job in the customer's own industry (for example payroll, HR, accounting, or healthcare) where they used the software. Some enter as associate or junior consultants after college. Typical steps are junior (0–2 years), consultant, senior, then lead or principal, implementation project manager, solutions consultant, or customer success leadership.",
  notes:"No license is needed. Vendor certifications (for example Salesforce, Workday, or NetSuite) can raise pay and are sometimes required by consulting partners. Some roles involve travel to customer sites, though much work is now remote. Hours can spike near go-live dates. Ask how projects are scoped and how many you will carry at once before accepting an offer.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Management Analysts","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/management-analysts.htm"],["Founderpath: Implementation Specialist SaaS salary benchmarks (Sep 2026)","https://founderpath.com/salary-benchmarks/saas/implementation-specialist"],["Indeed: Implementation consultant salaries in the United States","https://www.indeed.com/salaries/implementation-consultant-Salaries"],["Payscale: Implementation Consultant with SaaS skills salary (Jan 2026)","https://www.payscale.com/research/US/Job=Implementation_Consultant/Salary/d28a35ef/Software-as-a-Service-SaaS"],["Rocketlane (vendor report): The State of Customer Onboarding 2025","https://www.rocketlane.com/resources/state-of-customer-onboarding-2025"],["SaaStr: The Great SDR Downsizing (Emergence Capital survey of 560+ B2B software companies, 2025)","https://saastr.com/the-great-sdr-downsizing"]]},
 "csm":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS has no separate category for customer success managers. The closest fit is a mix of sales and account roles; Sales Representatives of Services had a median of $69,990 (May 2025), 10th percentile $37,980, 90th $148,840. SaaS-specific data runs higher: RepVue reports a median CSM base of $95,000 and on-target earnings (base plus bonus) of $130,000 (2025), and the Customer Success Collective puts the US median at $88,500 (2025). Senior and enterprise CSMs and managers earn more.",
  growth:"Using Sales Representatives of Services as a stand-in, BLS projects 2.7% growth from 2025 to 2035 (slower than average) but about 115,700 openings a year, mostly from turnover. In software, industry voices in 2026 expect fewer CSMs per customer as AI frees up time, and more pressure for CS to own renewals and upsell revenue. Hiring news is mixed, not booming.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"AI already drafts outreach and follow-ups, summarizes calls, flags at-risk accounts from usage data, and builds value reports, and AI support agents handle many basic how-to questions. What stays human: building trust with key contacts, handling escalations and tough renewal talks, advising customers on strategy, and negotiating expansions. Experts expect each CSM to cover more accounts, so lower-touch roles serving many small customers are most at risk."},
  sentiment:"In a 2025 industry survey, a little over half of CSMs said they were happy in their roles, and most enjoyed helping customers and thinking strategically. Common complaints: unclear career paths (only about a quarter saw one at their company), feeling that the company values sales more, heavy account loads, and being blamed for churn they cannot control. Many said they would leave for the right role.",
  path:"Common ways in include customer support, account management, teaching, hospitality, or other client-facing jobs, plus some new grads in associate CSM roles. A bachelor's is common but not always required. Steps run from associate CSM to CSM to senior or enterprise CSM (about 3–5 years), then CS manager, director, or a move into account management, sales, implementation, or RevOps.",
  notes:"No license needed. Optional certificates exist (for example from SuccessCOACHING or Gainsight), but experience and results matter more. Many roles now carry renewal or expansion targets, so part of pay may depend on numbers; ask how the bonus works. Some roles are remote. Skills employers say CSMs lack most are sales, negotiation, and analysis.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Customer Service Representatives","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/customer-service-representatives.htm"],["RepVue: Customer Success Manager salaries","https://repvue.com/salaries/customer-success-manager"],["Customer Success Collective: How much do CSMs earn in the United States?","https://www.customersuccesscollective.com/customer-success-manager-salary-united-states/"],["ChurnZero (vendor): CSM Confidential 2025 careers research","https://churnzero.com/press-release/csm-careers-research-confidential-2025/"],["ChurnZero (vendor blog): 2026 customer success trends from experts","https://churnzero.com/blog/2026-customer-success-trends-experts/"]]},
 "sales":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $104,920 for technical and scientific product sales reps (41-4011) and $69,990 for services sales reps (41-3091) (May 2025). Ranges: $52,600 to $200,440 and $37,980 to $148,840 (10th to 90th percentile). In software, a 2025 Bridge Group study puts median SDR on-target earnings at $80,000 ($55k base plus $25k variable), and a 2026 guide puts account executive base around $104,000 with $180,000–$220,000+ on-target earnings. Top enterprise sellers can earn much more.",
  growth:"BLS projects slow growth from 2025 to 2035: 1.2% for technical sales reps (about 23,600 openings a year) and 2.7% for services sales reps (about 115,700 a year), mostly from turnover. Recent news is weaker for entry-level software sales: a 2025 survey found 36% of B2B software companies cut SDR teams and 25% cut account executives, with AI tools taking over much prospecting.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"Microsoft's 2025 study ranks sales reps among the jobs where AI applies most. AI already researches prospects, builds lead lists, writes and sends outreach emails, books meetings, takes call notes, and updates the CRM, which hits SDR jobs hardest. What stays human: discovery conversations, negotiation, multi-person enterprise deals, and relationships. Fewer entry seats and a harder SDR-to-AE path are likely."},
  sentiment:"People like the uncapped earning potential, independence, and competition. Complaints center on quota pressure and stress: a 2025 survey of 830 B2B sellers found only 39% consistently hit quota, about 40% reported job stress, and 70% struggled with work-life balance. Bad managers are a top reason reps leave. SDRs report repetitive cold outreach and fewer promotions to AE than a few years ago.",
  path:"No degree is strictly required, and many come from retail, hospitality, recruiting, or other customer-facing jobs. In software, the classic route is SDR or BDR for about 1–2 years (average tenure 1.9 years), then account executive (small business, then mid-market, then enterprise), then senior AE, sales manager, or sales engineering. Promotions from SDR to AE have become rarer, so some now enter directly as junior AEs.",
  notes:"Pay is heavily tied to results, so income can swing year to year; always ask what share of reps hit quota. Technical sales (41-4011) often prefers a bachelor's in the product's field. Some roles involve travel. No license needed for most software sales, but some fields (insurance, securities, real estate) require one. Turnover is high and layoffs hit sales teams early in downturns.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Wholesale and Manufacturing Sales Representatives","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/sales/wholesale-and-manufacturing-sales-representatives.htm"],["The Bridge Group: 2025 SDR Models and Metrics Report","https://www.bridgegroupinc.com/research/2025-sdr-models-metrics-report-the-bridge-group"],["Qobra (vendor blog): 2026 Software Sales Salary Guide","https://www.qobra.co/blog/average-salary-for-software-sales"],["SaaStr: The Great SDR Downsizing (Emergence Capital survey of 560+ B2B software companies, 2025)","https://saastr.com/the-great-sdr-downsizing"],["SalesFuel: 2025 Voice of the Sales Rep study","https://salesfuel.com/salesfuels-2025-voice-of-the-sales-rep-study-finds-leadership-credibility-is-the-1-factor-in-sales-rep-retention/"],["Headlight Data: Microsoft research on which occupations use AI and how successfully (Nov 2025)","https://headlightdata.com/microsoft-research-reveals-occupations-using-ai-and-how-successfully"]]},
 "revops":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS has no separate category for revenue or sales operations. The closest, Management Analysts, has a median of $101,860 (May 2025), with the lowest 10% under $60,640 and the top 10% over $171,640. A study of 2,211 US RevOps job postings from the first half of 2026 found median pay of $62,400 at entry level, about $97,000 for specialists, $122,500 for experts, and $175,000 for leaders. Sales operations titles had a median near $97,000.",
  growth:"Using Management Analysts as a stand-in, BLS projects 10.1% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), with about 94,100 openings a year. RevOps postings in early 2026 stayed steady month to month and were mostly full-time, as companies build the function in-house. Postings asking for AI and automation skills paid much more, and new hybrid titles like GTM engineer are growing.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"AI is taking over much analyst-level work: building reports and dashboards, cleaning CRM data, routing leads, writing forecasts, and setting up simple automations. A RevOps career guide expects this to shrink entry-level analyst work over the next 3–5 years. What stays human: designing the sales process, territory and compensation plans, deciding which data to trust, and getting sales, marketing, and finance to agree."},
  sentiment:"People in RevOps tend to like being at the center of how a company makes money and the mix of data, systems, and strategy. Common complaints are messy CRM data, endless ad-hoc report requests, being seen as the sales team's admin, and being blamed when forecasts miss. Being a small team at many companies can mean a heavy load. Evidence here is mostly from industry commentary, so treat it as a general picture.",
  path:"Most people move in from sales, sales development, marketing operations, customer success, finance, or data analysis rather than straight from school. A Salesforce Administrator certification is close to required for many entry jobs, along with strong Excel and basic SQL. Typical steps: analyst (1–2 years), specialist or senior analyst, manager (often 3–5 years in), then director or head of RevOps.",
  notes:"No license needed. Salesforce and HubSpot certifications are the most useful credentials. Mostly desk work, often remote or hybrid. Smaller companies may expect one person to cover systems, reporting, and planning. Pay rises sharply for people who can build automations and AI workflows, so technical skills matter for growth.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Management Analysts","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/management-analysts.htm"],["RevGuild Research: US Revenue Operations Salary 2026 (H1 2026 postings)","https://revguild.org/blog/us-revenue-operations-salary-2026/"],["Landbase (vendor blog): Choosing the right RevOps career path (Mar 2026)","https://www.landbase.com/blog/choosing-right-revops-career-path"],["Glassdoor: Revenue operations analyst salaries","https://www.glassdoor.com/Salaries/revenue-operations-analyst-salary-SRCH_KO0,26.htm"]]},
 "data":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS has no separate data or BI analyst category. The closest, Operations Research Analysts, has a median of $88,940 (May 2025), with the lowest 10% under $57,060 and the top 10% over $159,910; more technical roles can fall under Data Scientists (median $120,230). Salary surveys for the role vary: Payscale puts BI analysts at a $65,000 median base, roughly $48k–$94k (2026), while Glassdoor's average for data analysts is about $111,000 (cited 2026).",
  growth:"BLS projects Operations Research Analysts to grow 12% from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), with about 7,500 openings a year; Data Scientists are projected at 34.6%. Recent hiring is more cautious: early 2026 reports describe data analyst postings as stabilizing after a flat 2025, few postings aimed at beginners, and employers favoring analysts who turn findings into business decisions.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"Microsoft's 2025 study ranks data scientists and analysts among jobs with notable AI overlap. AI already writes SQL and Python, builds charts and dashboards, cleans data, and drafts summaries of results, so routine reporting needs fewer people. What stays human: asking the right business question, knowing the data's quirks, judging whether results make sense, and convincing leaders to act. Entry-level reporting jobs are most exposed."},
  sentiment:"Analysts often like solving puzzles, steady desk work, and seeing their findings used. Common complaints are endless requests for one-off reports and dashboards nobody reads, messy data, and a tough entry-level market with many applicants per opening. Many worry AI tools will shrink junior roles, though surveys of analysts also report AI makes them more effective. Tech-sector layoffs in 2025 did not spare data teams.",
  path:"Many enter with a bachelor's in business, economics, statistics, or computer science; others switch from finance, operations, marketing, or sales jobs by learning SQL, Excel, and a BI tool (Tableau or Power BI) and showing a portfolio. Typical steps: junior analyst, analyst, senior analyst (about 4–6 years), then analytics manager, analytics engineer, data scientist, or product or business roles.",
  notes:"No license needed. In a 2026 review of postings, about half asked for SQL, 41% Excel, 33% Python, and 25–28% Power BI or Tableau; 39% asked for a bachelor's and 36% a master's. Certificates (for example Google Data Analytics or Microsoft Power BI) help career changers but don't replace a portfolio. Mostly desk work, often hybrid or remote.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Operations Research Analysts","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Data Scientists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm"],["Payscale: Business Intelligence Analyst salary (2026)","https://www.payscale.com/research/US/Job=Business_Intelligence_Analyst/Salary"],["365 Data Science: Data Analyst Job Outlook 2026 (1,355 postings)","https://365datascience.com/career-advice/data-analyst-job-outlook-2025/"],["Interview Query: January 2026 Data Science Job Market Report","https://www.interviewquery.com/p/jan-data-science-job-market-report"],["Headlight Data: Microsoft research on which occupations use AI and how successfully (Nov 2025)","https://headlightdata.com/microsoft-research-reveals-occupations-using-ai-and-how-successfully"]]},
 "ds":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for data scientists is $120,230 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $67,240 and the top 10% over $199,130. Machine learning engineer roles pay more: 2026 figures compiled by a staffing firm put typical entry base in the low $120k range, mid-level around $150–160k, and senior around $190k+, and Levels.fyi shows an all-level median total pay near $262k for the ML engineer title, which leans toward big tech.",
  growth:"BLS projects 35% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), with about 24,800 openings a year. Recent hiring is mixed: a January 2026 market report found data science postings stabilizing but still slightly below a year earlier, while ML engineer postings grew fastest and AI-linked postings rose year over year. Dice reported AI/ML job title postings up 173% from early 2025 to early 2026.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"A Microsoft study of real AI use ranked data scientists among the 40 most AI-applicable jobs (2025). AI tools now write much of the routine code, cleaning scripts, queries, and first-pass charts. What stays human is framing the right question, judging whether a model or result makes sense, understanding the business, and explaining findings to decision makers. Demand is shifting toward people who can build and run AI systems in production."},
  sentiment:"The field feels crowded at the entry level, and online debate swings between calling data science dead and calling it booming because of AI. Practitioners say the title now covers many different jobs, from product analytics to ML engineering, which makes job hunting confusing. People like the problem solving and pay; complaints center on tough competition for junior roles, long interview processes, and tech layoffs in 2025.",
  path:"Most start with a bachelor's in statistics, math, computer science, or a related field; many employers prefer a master's, and research roles at big tech often want a PhD. Common routes in are data analyst, software engineer, or a quantitative field like economics or science, plus a strong project portfolio. After 3–5 years people move to senior or lead data scientist, ML engineer, or analytics manager.",
  notes:"No license is needed. Key skills are Python, SQL, statistics, and increasingly production tools (Git, Docker, MLOps) and basic LLM knowledge. Pay and openings concentrate in tech, finance, retail, and healthcare, and in large metro areas. Red flag: job titles vary a lot, so a data scientist posting may really be a reporting or dashboard job, or a heavy engineering job.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Data Scientists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm"],["Interview Query: January 2026 data science job market report","https://www.interviewquery.com/p/jan-data-science-job-market-report"],["KORE1 (staffing firm): ML engineer salary guide 2026","https://www.kore1.com/ml-engineer-salary-guide/"],["Microsoft Research: Working with AI, measuring the occupational implications of generative AI (2025)","https://arxiv.org/html/2507.07935v5"],["Towards Data Science: Data science in 2026, is it still worth it?","https://towardsdatascience.com/data-science-in-2026-is-it-still-worth-it/"],["CIO: IT hiring sees a boost as software development jobs slowly bounce back (2026)","https://www.cio.com/article/4195780/it-hiring-sees-a-boost-as-software-development-jobs-slowly-bounce-back.html"]]},
 "swe":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $135,980 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $82,460 and the top 10% over $214,670. Levels.fyi, which leans toward large tech firms, reports 2025 US median total pay (salary plus stock and bonus) of about $155k for entry-level engineers and $312k for senior engineers. Pay at non-tech employers and outside big metros is much closer to the BLS figures.",
  growth:"BLS projects 10% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), with about 95,300 openings a year. Recent hiring is weaker than that suggests: Indeed postings rose about 15% from early 2025 to mid-2026 but were still about 27.5% below pre-pandemic levels, and 71% of the rise was for senior roles. A Stanford payroll study found employment of software developers aged 22–25 fell sharply from late 2022 to mid-2025 while older workers held steady.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"AI coding tools are now used by about 80% of developers (2025) and can write, test, and fix routine code, especially front-end and simple app work. Anthropic data shows coding agents mostly automate tasks rather than assist. What stays human is deciding what to build, system design, reviewing and debugging AI output, security, and working with users. The biggest effect so far is on junior hiring, since AI does much of the work juniors used to learn on."},
  sentiment:"Mood is mixed. Experienced developers still find good pay and remote options, but new grads and career changers describe hundreds of applications, long searches, and fear that AI will shrink entry roles. Developers use AI daily but trust it less: in 2025 only about 29% trusted its accuracy and many said fixing almost-right AI code eats time. Only about a quarter called themselves happy at work, though layoffs in 2025 eased somewhat in 2026.",
  path:"The usual route is a bachelor's in computer science or a related field plus internships. Bootcamp and self-taught paths still work but are harder now that junior openings are scarce; a portfolio of real, deployed projects and referrals matter most. Many enter from QA, IT, data analysis, or another field by building tools at their current job. After 3–5 years: senior engineer, then staff engineer, architect, or engineering manager.",
  notes:"No license needed; certifications matter less than skills and projects, though cloud certifications can help. Interviews often include coding tests and several rounds. Pay varies widely by employer and city, with the highest pay at large tech firms in major hubs. Red flags: the entry-level market in 2024–2026 is the toughest in years, and the role keeps changing as AI tools improve, so ongoing learning is required.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Software Developers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/software-developers.htm"],["CIO: IT hiring sees a boost as software development jobs slowly bounce back (2026)","https://www.cio.com/article/4195780/it-hiring-sees-a-boost-as-software-development-jobs-slowly-bounce-back.html"],["LeadDev: Young devs see steepest losses in exposed roles, Stanford study finds (2025)","https://leaddev.com/hiring/young-devs-steepest-losses-exposed-roles-stanford-study-finds"],["Stack Overflow: 2025 Developer Survey results","https://stackoverflow.blog/2025/07/29/developers-remain-willing-but-reluctant-to-use-ai-the-2025-developer-survey-results-are-here"],["Anthropic (AI vendor research): Economic Index, AI's impact on software development (2025)","https://www.anthropic.com/news/impact-software-development"],["Levels.fyi: 2025 annual pay report","https://www.levels.fyi/2025/"]]},
 "cyber":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for information security analysts is $129,180 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $75,090 and the top 10% over $199,850. Entry SOC (security operations center) analyst jobs often start lower: training providers citing 2026 job data put Tier 1 SOC pay around $50–70k base, with night-shift differentials, and Tier 2 around $85–110k after 1–2 years. In-house teams usually pay more than outsourced security providers.",
  growth:"BLS projects 21% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), with about 14,100 openings a year. CyberSeek counted nearly 500,000 US cybersecurity job postings from mid-2024 to early 2025. But ISC2's 2025 survey found 39% of organizations had hiring freezes and 24% had layoffs, and employers now say they lack specific skills more than headcount, so entry roles are competitive.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI SOC agents are taking over much Tier 1 work: sorting alerts, gathering context, and writing incident summaries. Gartner (2025) describes humans staying in the loop to check AI conclusions, make judgment calls, respond to incidents, and own accountability, since AI can reach wrong conclusions. Expect fewer pure alert-watching jobs and more need for analysts who can investigate, hunt threats, and tune detections."},
  sentiment:"Most people in security are satisfied with their jobs (68% in ISC2's 2025 survey), and many like the mission and variety. Burnout is real: nearly half feel exhausted keeping up with threats and technology, and SOC staff often complain about alert fatigue, night and weekend shifts, and repetitive triage. Newcomers report that the promised talent shortage does not feel like it applies to entry-level applicants.",
  path:"BLS says most analysts have a bachelor's in IT or a related field, but some get in with a high school diploma plus certifications. Many come from IT help desk or systems administration after 1–3 years. Hands-on SIEM practice and certifications such as CompTIA Security+ help land a Tier 1 SOC job. Next steps are Tier 2 or 3 analyst, threat hunter, incident responder, detection engineer, or security engineer.",
  notes:"Certifications matter more here than in most tech fields: Security+ to start, then options like CySA+, GIAC, or CISSP later. SOC work often means 24/7 shifts and on-call duty. Government and defense jobs may require a security clearance. Red flag: the field is popular with career changers, so entry roles are crowded, and AI tools are shrinking pure Tier 1 alert work.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Information Security Analysts","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/information-security-analysts.htm"],["ISC2: 2025 Cybersecurity Workforce Study","https://www.isc2.org/insights/2025/12/2025-isc2-cybersecurity-workforce-study"],["CyberSeek: 2025 infographic (job postings, Lightcast data)","https://www.cyberseek.org/docs/CyberSeek_Infographic_2025.pdf"],["Dropzone AI (vendor blog): Inside Gartner's 2025 research on AI SOC agents","https://www.dropzone.ai/blog/inside-the-gartner-2025-research-the-rise-of-ai-soc-agents-in-modern-security-operations"],["TryHackMe (training vendor blog): Best entry-level cyber security jobs in 2026","https://tryhackme.com/resources/blog/best-entry-level-cyber-security-jobs-in-2026-what-they-pay-and-how-to-get-them"]]},
 "grc":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS has no separate GRC category; the closest is compliance officers, with a median of $80,730 a year (May 2025), the lowest 10% under $48,220 and the top 10% over $133,720. IT and cybersecurity GRC roles pay more: 2026 salary-site data puts the average GRC analyst around $101k (Salary.com) to $140k (Glassdoor), and practitioners cite entry pay near $45k rising past $100k with experience, though these estimates vary widely.",
  growth:"BLS projects 4% growth for compliance officers from 2025 to 2035 (about average), with about 32,700 openings a year. Cybersecurity GRC demand looks stronger: new rules, vendor-risk reviews, and AI governance (such as the EU AI Act and NIST AI framework) are adding work, though I found no hard 2025–26 hiring counts specific to GRC analysts.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"Compliance software already automates much of the routine work: collecting audit evidence, checking controls continuously, mapping one control to several frameworks, and answering security questionnaires with AI. Reports and dashboards are becoming commodity output. What stays human is judging risk, interpreting regulations, negotiating with business teams and auditors, and setting policy. Analysts who can also automate compliance checks appear better placed than those who mainly fill in spreadsheets."},
  sentiment:"People like that GRC is less technical and more stable than many security jobs, with regular hours and a clear link to business decisions. Complaints include repetitive evidence gathering and spreadsheet work, being seen as the department of no, and careers that plateau if the job stays mostly reporting. A 2025 survey of compliance officers found nearly half had struggled with anxiety in the past year, tied partly to workload and reporting lines.",
  path:"GRC is a common way into security for people from audit, accounting, legal, project management, or operations, and no technical degree is strictly needed, though many employers list a bachelor's. Learn frameworks like SOC 2, ISO 27001, and NIST, and get an entry certification such as Security+ or ISC2 CC. Typical steps: analyst for 1–2 years, senior analyst by 3–4, then GRC manager, director of risk, or CISO after 10+ years.",
  notes:"Useful certifications include CISA (audit), CRISC (risk), CISM (management), CGRC (federal), and CIPP (privacy); several require years of experience. Work is mostly desk-based with regular hours and often remote. Finance, healthcare, government, and SaaS companies hire the most. Red flag: salary figures for GRC vary a lot by source, and job titles range from entry admin work to senior risk roles.",
  sources:[["BLS OEWS: Compliance Officers (13-1041), May 2025","https://www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes131041.htm"],["QuickStart (training vendor blog): How to become a GRC analyst, 2026 career path","https://www.quickstart.com/blog/cyber-security/grc-analyst-career-path-2026/"],["Vanta (vendor blog): The future of GRC","https://www.vanta.com/resources/the-future-of-grc"],["Infosec Conferences: GRC salary data 2026","https://infosec-conferences.com/security-domains/grc/salary/"],["CIO: Breaking into cybersecurity without a technical degree (2025)","https://www.cio.com/article/4051515/breaking-into-cybersecurity-without-a-technical-degree-a-practical-guide.html"],["Corporate Compliance Insights: 2025 compliance officer stress and mental health report","https://www.corporatecomplianceinsights.com/2025-mental-health-stress-cci/"]]},
 "itsup":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for computer user support specialists is $61,860 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $40,980 and the top 10% over $100,540. For network and computer systems administrators, the median is $99,130, with the lowest 10% under $62,640 and the top 10% over $155,050. Help desk jobs usually start in the $40–60k range, and moving to sysadmin or cloud work is how pay rises.",
  growth:"BLS projects a 3.5% decline for user support jobs and a 4.1% decline for systems administrators from 2025 to 2035, but still about 39,700 and 13,400 openings a year, all from people retiring or changing jobs. Recent news agrees: CompTIA flags support roles as flat or declining, and entry-level IT applicants report long searches, while employers say they struggle to find people with 3–5 years of experience.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"Help desk is one of the jobs most exposed to AI. Chatbots, self-service portals, and automatic password resets handle simple tickets, and BLS cites chatbots as the reason user support jobs will shrink. Palo Alto Networks said in 2024 it cut its IT support staff by nearly half. Humans still handle hard or unusual problems, hardware and on-site work, upset users, and oversight of AI answers. Sysadmin work is shifting to cloud services and automation scripts."},
  sentiment:"Help desk is widely seen as a stepping stone rather than a long-term job. Common complaints are low pay for the stress, repetitive tickets, frustrated users, and shift work, plus worry about AI chatbots replacing tier 1. Sysadmins generally report better pay and more interesting work but complain about after-hours outages, being on call, and on-premises jobs disappearing into the cloud.",
  path:"One of the easiest ways into tech from an unrelated job: many start with a high school diploma or some college plus CompTIA A+ and land a help desk role. After 1–3 years, adding Network+, Linux, Windows Server, scripting (PowerShell), and cloud skills leads to desktop support, then systems administrator. From there people move to cloud, DevOps, network engineering, or cybersecurity.",
  notes:"Certifications carry real weight here: A+, Network+, Security+, Microsoft, and cloud certifications. Some jobs require lifting equipment and on-site work; support may run nights and weekends, and sysadmins often have on-call duty. Red flags: BLS projects both jobs to shrink, and pure on-premises sysadmin roles are fading, so plan to keep learning cloud and automation skills.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Computer Support Specialists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-support-specialists.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Network and Computer Systems Administrators","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/network-and-computer-systems-administrators.htm"],["CNBC via NBC New York: The tech support desk is one of the first jobs on AI's replacement ticket (2024)","https://www.nbcnewyork.com/news/business/money-report/the-tech-support-desk-at-work-is-one-of-the-first-jobs-on-ais-replacement-ticket/6029799/"],["IT Support Group (blog): IT job market 2026 reality check","https://thisisanitsupportgroup.com/blog/it-job-market-2026-reality-check"]]},
 "cloud":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS has no separate category for cloud or DevOps engineers. The closest, Computer Occupations All Other, had a median of $116,580 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $55,940 and the top 10% over $188,470. Role surveys run higher: a 2026 staffing-firm guide built from Glassdoor, PayScale and others puts DevOps base pay at about $81–95k entry level, $110–135k mid-level, $140–175k senior and $175–220k staff, and Motion Recruitment's 2026 guide shows about $129–168k for mid to senior.",
  growth:"BLS projects 5.1% growth for Computer Occupations All Other from 2025 to 2035 (a bit above average), with about 27,000 openings a year. The related network architect job is projected at +7.7%, while systems administrators are expected to shrink 4.1% as server work moves to the cloud. 2026 recruiter reports say demand for DevOps, platform and cloud security skills held up while the wider IT market softened, but junior openings are scarce.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"AI already drafts much of the routine work: infrastructure code, pipeline settings, scripts and first-pass fixes, and 2026 tools are moving toward agents that take actions on their own. Surveys show engineers want those agents only with human approval and rollback, and only about a third would trust AI with daily tasks unreviewed. System design, security and compliance, cost trade-offs and handling outages stay human. Expect less manual toil and more value on architecture and security."},
  sentiment:"People like the pay, solving hard technical puzzles, and being central to how software ships. The biggest complaints are on-call duty, late-night outages, and being stretched across too many tools; in one 2025 industry survey nearly half said DevOps overload drives burnout or frustration. Forum threads in 2026 say it is hard to break in without prior experience, since newcomers compete with developers and sysadmins moving over.",
  path:"Most people move in from system administration, help desk, software development or testing after 1–3 years, often by transferring inside their company. Typical study: Linux and networking, one cloud (AWS or Azure), Terraform, containers and Kubernetes, and CI/CD pipelines, plus a portfolio of projects; a focused switch from sysadmin takes about 6–12 months. A CS or IT degree helps but is not required. Next steps are senior, site reliability or platform engineer, cloud architect, or engineering manager.",
  notes:"Common certifications are AWS Solutions Architect Associate or Azure Administrator, HashiCorp Terraform Associate, and Certified Kubernetes Administrator; free training is on AWS Skill Builder and Microsoft Learn, though exams cost money. Expect an on-call rotation that can include nights and weekends. Titles vary (cloud, DevOps, platform, SRE), so read the duties. Security and compliance experience, especially in health care and finance, is reported to be in short supply.",
  sources:[["BLS OEWS: Computer Occupations, All Other (15-1299)","https://www.bls.gov/Oes/current/oes151299.htm"],["KORE1 (staffing firm): DevOps engineer salary guide 2026","https://www.kore1.com/devops-engineer-salary-guide/"],["Motion Recruitment: 2026 DevOps tech salary guide","https://motionrecruitment.com/it-salary/devops"],["GitLab (vendor) survey via Business Wire: The AI paradox, Nov 2025","https://businesswire.com/news/home/20251110342143/en/GitLab-Survey-Reveals-the-AI-Paradox-Faster-Coding-Creates-New-Bottlenecks-Requiring-Platform-Solutions"],["DuploCloud (vendor blog): 2026 AI + DevOps report","https://duplocloud.com/blog/ai-devops-report/"],["Adam the Automator: Transition from sysadmin to cloud engineer","https://adamtheautomator.com/transition-sysadmin-cloud-engineer/"],["Forum thread: Is it getting harder for juniors to break into DevOps in 2026?","https://yomotherboard.com/question/is-it-getting-harder-for-juniors-to-break-into-devops-in-2026/"]]},
 "ba":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for computer systems analysts is $105,850 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $67,340 and the top 10% over $167,710. Robert Half's salary guide (projected for 2027) puts IT business systems analysts at $93,250 to $140,000 nationally, midpoint $115,000; its St. Paul range is $77,490 to $108,270. IIBA's 2026 global survey reports CBAP-certified analysts earn about 19% more than uncertified peers.",
  growth:"BLS projects 7.9% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), with about 32,900 openings a year across roughly 544,400 jobs. Recent reporting is mixed: experienced analysts are still hired, but 2025 writing describes traditional junior BA roles as harder to find, with entry work relabeled as project coordinator, product analyst or QA analyst.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"AI already handles much junior analyst work: meeting notes, first drafts of requirements and user stories, documentation and dashboard updates. Microsoft's 2025 study ranks computer and math jobs second-highest of 22 groups for AI overlap. What stays human is getting stakeholders to agree on what they really need, spotting what they left out, weighing trade-offs, and checking that systems fit how people actually work. Expect fewer entry-level seats and more value on domain knowledge."},
  sentiment:"Most analysts in IIBA's 2026 survey said AI is good for their careers (69%, down from 74% a year earlier), and people value the mix of business and tech work. Common frustrations are unclear role definitions and being brought into projects too late. Newcomers describe a tough market, with entry postings asking for 2–3 years of experience and degrees and certificates often drawing little response.",
  path:"Many people come from inside a business, such as operations, finance, customer support or QA, after learning its systems, or from a business, MIS or CS degree. Useful skills are SQL and Excel, process mapping, user stories and Agile. Adjacent first jobs like project coordinator or product analyst are common stepping stones. After 3–5 years, paths lead to senior or lead analyst, product owner or product manager, solution architect, or IT consulting.",
  notes:"IIBA offers the ECBA (entry), CCBA and CBAP certifications; in its 2026 survey certified analysts earned 12% more on average, and CBAP holders 19% more. Domain knowledge (health care, finance, or ERP systems like SAP or Workday) often matters as much as the title. The work is desk-based with many meetings, often hybrid. Titles vary widely (business analyst, systems analyst, functional analyst), so read the duties.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Computer Systems Analysts","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm"],["Robert Half: IT business systems analyst salary (2027 guide)","https://www.roberthalf.com/us/en/job-details/it-business-systems-analyst"],["Robert Half: Business systems analyst salary, St. Paul MN","https://www.roberthalf.com/us/en/job-details/business-systems-analyst/st-paul-mn"],["IIBA: 2026 Global State of Business Analysis report","https://www.iiba.org/business-analysis-blogs/insight-enables-confidence-the-2026-global-state-of-business-analysis-report/"],["Modern Analyst: Breaking in as a business analyst in an AI-disrupted job market (Aug 2025)","https://www.modernanalyst.com/Resources/Articles/tabid/115/ID/7002/Breaking-In-as-a-Business-Analyst-Skills-Empathy-and-Adaptability-in-an-AI-Disrupted-Job-Market.aspx"],["Microsoft Research: Working with AI, measuring applicability of generative AI to occupations (2025)","https://arxiv.org/html/2507.07935v5"]]},
 "pm":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS has no separate category for product managers. The closest, Computer and Information Systems Managers, had a median of $175,140 (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $107,550 and the top 10% over $297,510. Marketing Managers' median was $166,790. A 2026 staffing-firm guide built from Levels.fyi, Glassdoor and others puts base pay at $80–110k for associate PMs, $120–160k for PMs and $150–195k for senior PMs; tech total pay with stock runs about $160–350k.",
  growth:"BLS projects 15.8% growth for Computer and Information Systems Managers from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), with 53,500 openings a year, and 6.9% for Marketing Managers. Hiring news is mixed: open PM roles at tech companies reached about 7,300 in March 2026, the most since 2022, but US tech PM headcount was still about 28% below its 2022 peak by mid-2026, and most openings are mid- or senior-level.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI now drafts specs, summarizes customer feedback and research, analyzes usage data, and lets PMs build quick prototypes themselves. The core stays human: deciding what to build and what to skip, balancing customers, engineers and executives, setting strategy, and owning results. Many 2026 layoff announcements cited AI, so expect leaner product teams with fewer layers and more pressure on PMs to be hands-on and technical."},
  sentiment:"PMs like the influence, variety and pay, and many enjoy working where business, design and tech meet. Common complaints are heavy responsibility without direct authority, constant meetings, and pressure to deliver; in an Atlassian survey of over 1,000 product people, 84% feared their products would fail. A 2026 tech-worker survey found burnout at a record high, and recent layoffs hit product managers and leaders hard.",
  path:"Most people become PMs through an internal transfer, after taking on PM-like work in another role such as engineering, design, analytics, support or consulting. Associate PM programs hire some new grads but are very competitive. Deep industry knowledge (for example health care or education) can be a shortcut. An MBA helps but is not required. Next come senior and group PM, director, VP of product and chief product officer.",
  notes:"No license or certification is required; shipped products and references matter most. The best pay and most openings are in tech hubs, and about a third of open AI-related roles are in the Bay Area. Titles vary (product owner, technical PM, AI PM) and differ in scope. Director and VP product roles were cut deepest since 2022, so check a company's stability before joining.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Computer and Information Systems Managers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/computer-and-information-systems-managers.htm"],["KORE1 (staffing firm): Product manager salary guide 2026","https://www.kore1.com/product-manager-salary-guide/"],["Lenny's Newsletter: State of the product job market in early 2026","https://www.lennysnewsletter.com/p/191595250?r=1o9eyi"],["Mind the Product: What the data tells us about tech and PM layoffs in 2026","https://www.mindtheproduct.com/what-the-data-tells-us-about-tech-and-pm-lay-offs-in-2026/"],["Atlassian (vendor): State of Product 2026","https://www.atlassian.com/software/jira/product-discovery/resources/state-of-product-2026"],["Jackie Bavaro: How to become a product manager","https://jackiebavaro.substack.com/p/how-to-become-a-product-manager"],["Lenny's Newsletter: How tech workers actually feel about AI in 2026","https://www.lennysnewsletter.com/p/how-tech-workers-actually-feel-about"]]},
 "proj":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for project management specialists is $102,320 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $61,580 and the top 10% over $167,970. PMI's 2025 salary survey found a US median of $135,000 for PMP holders versus $109,157 without it (about 24% more), rising to $173,000 for those certified over 10 years. Pharmaceuticals and aerospace paid best, at a $150,000 median.",
  growth:"BLS projects 6.7% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), with about 76,500 openings a year in a large field of 1.09 million jobs. PMI's 2025 talent gap report estimates up to 30 million more project professionals will be needed worldwide by 2035, led by construction, manufacturing, IT services and health care, though it expects the fastest growth outside North America.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"Gartner predicted in 2019 that AI would take over most of today's tracking, data collection and status reporting by 2030, and AI tools now draft schedules, meeting notes, risk logs and status updates. What stays human is leading people, negotiating scope and budgets with stakeholders, resolving conflicts, and making judgment calls when plans break. Expect less admin work and more weight on leadership and industry knowledge."},
  sentiment:"Project managers often like the variety, seeing work get finished, and pay that rises with certification and experience. Common complaints are deadline pressure, constant meetings, and being responsible for results without full control over the team. A UK survey of 1,000 practitioners found 76% said their main project caused them stress, citing supply problems and weak manager support (2021), with legal, health care and telecom projects the most stressful.",
  path:"Many people move into project management from another role, such as coordinator, analyst, engineer, nurse or tradesperson, by running projects in their field. BLS lists a bachelor's degree as typical. Common first steps are project coordinator or the entry-level CAPM (23 hours of training, no experience needed). The PMP needs 35 hours of training plus 36 months leading projects with a four-year degree, or 60 without. Next come senior PM, program or portfolio manager, and PMO director.",
  notes:"The PMP is the main credential and is widely requested; exams cost about $225–300 for CAPM and $405–675 for PMP depending on PMI membership, so ask an employer to pay. Agile credentials such as Scrum Master are common in software. Industry knowledge matters, since construction, IT and health care projects need different backgrounds. The work is mostly desk work and meetings, with site visits or travel in some fields.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Project Management Specialists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/project-management-specialists.htm"],["PMI: Earning Power salary survey, 14th edition (Nov 2025)","https://www.pmi.org/about/press-media/2025/pmp-certification-holders-build-career-momentum-and-experience-earning-advantage-pmi-survey-finds"],["TechRepublic: PMI project talent gap report to 2035 (May 2025)","https://www.techrepublic.com/article/news-project-talent-shortage-2035/"],["Gartner: 80% of today's project management tasks will be eliminated by 2030 (2019)","https://www.gartner.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2019-03-20-gartner-says-80-percent-of-today-s-project-management"],["APM: More project managers experiencing stress at work (2021)","https://www.apm.org.uk/news/more-project-managers-experiencing-stress-at-work-new-survey-by-apm-finds/"],["Coursera: CAPM vs. PMP","https://www.coursera.org/articles/capm-vs-pmp"]]},
 "ux":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for web and digital interface designers is $104,000 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $53,750 and the top 10% over $201,550. A 2026 staffing-firm guide using Glassdoor, ZipRecruiter and others puts UX base pay around $65–85k for true juniors, about $95–115k mid-level and $130k-plus senior, with product designer titles paying roughly $20–40k more; PayScale puts senior product designers near $176k.",
  growth:"BLS projects 6.0% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), with about 8,300 openings a year. Hiring news is weaker: open design roles at tech companies have been roughly flat since early 2023 at about 5,700 worldwide (March 2026), while PM and engineering openings grew. Nielsen Norman Group said in January 2026 that senior roles are recovering but entry-level jobs remain scarce.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"AI tools now produce much routine wireframing, mockups and standard interface screens, and Nielsen Norman Group warns that designers who only assemble design-system components are already replaceable. What stays valuable is user research and making sense of it, deciding which problem to solve, taste and judgment, and designing trustworthy AI-powered products. Expect fewer junior production jobs and more demand for research and strategy skills."},
  sentiment:"Designers enjoy solving problems for real people and the creative work, but the mood since 2023 has been rough. Common complaints are a crowded market where one entry-level posting can draw hundreds of applicants, layoffs, design being treated as decoration rather than strategy, and pressure to prove business value. Experienced designers with research skills report getting offers quickly, while newcomers and bootcamp grads face the hardest market.",
  path:"People come in through design, psychology, HCI or computer science degrees, or switch from graphic design, research, marketing, front-end development or support. Bootcamp certificates are now seen as baseline rather than a ticket in, so a portfolio of case studies showing the problem, your decisions and the outcome matters most, along with referrals. After 3–5 years, paths lead to senior, lead or principal designer, UX research, design management or product management.",
  notes:"No license is required. Get a portfolio review before paying for an expensive bootcamp (some cost around $15,000). The best-paid jobs cluster in tech hubs like San Francisco, and remote roles draw more applicants per opening. Titles vary (UX, UI, product, interaction designer), and product designer roles usually pay more. Figma and basic research methods are expected, and AI and design-system skills increasingly are.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Web Developers and Digital Designers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/web-developers.htm"],["KORE1 (staffing firm): UX designer salary guide 2026","https://www.kore1.com/ux-designer-salary-guide/"],["Nielsen Norman Group: State of UX 2026","https://nngroup.com/articles/state-of-ux-2026"],["Lenny's Newsletter: State of the product job market in early 2026","https://www.lennysnewsletter.com/p/191595250?r=1o9eyi"],["Center Centre (Jared Spool): Why is the UX job market such a mess right now? (Jan 2025)","https://articles.centercentre.com/?p=3694"],["MentorCruise (vendor blog): Entry-level UX designer jobs in 2026","https://mentorcruise.com/blog/entry-level-ux-designer-jobs-in-2026-what-actually-gets-you-hired/"]]},
 "mkt":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for market research analysts and marketing specialists is $78,760 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $43,390 and the top 10% over $155,480. Digital marketing roles track this: Robert Half's latest guide (labeled 2027) puts a digital marketing specialist at about $60,000 for newer hires, $70,500 mid-level and $84,500 for highly skilled people. Pay climbs faster in paid media, analytics and marketing automation than in social media posting.",
  growth:"BLS projects 7% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), with about 82,000 openings a year in a large field of about 952,700 jobs. Recent news is cooler at the entry level: a 2026 Content Marketing Institute study found one in three marketers say their company is cutting entry-level hiring, 75% say finding a marketing job is harder than two years ago, and average job searches stretched to 5.2 months.",
  ai:{rating:"High",text:"AI already drafts social posts, ad variations, emails and blog copy, does keyword research, builds reports and suggests audience segments. Those are exactly the junior tasks that used to train new marketers, so entry-level roles are shrinking. Microsoft's 2025 study listed market research analysts among the 40 jobs whose tasks overlap most with AI. Human work that holds up: strategy, judging what actually works, brand voice, budgets, and coordinating with sales and leadership."},
  sentiment:"Marketers like the creativity, variety and the chance to see results in data. Burnout is the big complaint: Marketing Week's 2026 survey of about 2,350 marketers found roughly two-thirds felt overwhelmed and over half emotionally exhausted in the past year, and many hide it out of fear for their jobs. Common gripes are constant pressure to prove marketing's value, too many tools, and a tougher job hunt after layoffs.",
  path:"BLS says a bachelor's degree (marketing, business, communications or similar) is typical, but digital marketing is one of the easier business fields to enter with a portfolio: running ads, SEO or social for a small business, nonprofit or your own project counts. Common starts are marketing coordinator or specialist roles, then senior specialist or channel lead in 3–5 years, then marketing manager. Many people move in from sales, customer service, writing or design.",
  notes:"No license is required. Free platform certificates (Google Ads, Google Analytics, HubSpot, Meta) are common and help show skills. Skills in paid media, analytics, CRM and marketing automation pay better than general social media work. Mostly office or remote work. Watch for coordinator jobs that bundle three roles into one at entry-level pay, and expect AI tool skills to be assumed in job posts.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Market Research Analysts","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/market-research-analysts.htm"],["Robert Half: Digital Marketing Specialist salary","https://roberthalf.com/us/en/job-details/digital-marketing-specialist"],["Content Marketing Institute: The missing generation, how AI is reshaping content and marketing roles in 2026","https://contentmarketinginstitute.com/content-operations/missing-generation-ai"],["Marketing Week: Most marketers feel overwhelmed, undervalued and emotionally exhausted (2026 Career & Salary Survey)","https://www.marketingweek.com/marketers-overwhelmed-emotionally-exhausted/"],["Fortune: Microsoft research on jobs most and least exposed to generative AI (2025)","https://fortune.com/2025/07/31/microsoft-research-generative-ai-occupational-impact-jobs-most-and-least-likely-to-impact-teaching-office-jobs-college-gen-z-grads"]]},
 "writer":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS medians (May 2025): writers and authors $76,910 (10th percentile $44,310, 90th $139,870); public relations specialists $74,750 (10th $44,110, 90th $135,150). Salaried jobs pay steadier than freelance work, and about 65% of writers and authors are self-employed. Robert Half's latest guide (labeled 2027) puts a staff copywriter at about $66,000 to $96,500, with a midpoint of $80,750.",
  growth:"BLS projects writers and authors to stay flat from 2025 to 2035 (about 0%, around 11,900 openings a year) and PR specialists to grow only 2% (about 23,000 openings a year), both below average. BLS itself cites AI writing as a reason for weak demand. Recent data agrees: The Drum reported in 2026 that writing roles fell from about 17% of ad agency job postings in late 2025 to 3% by July 2026, the lowest since 2022.",
  ai:{rating:"High",text:"Microsoft's 2025 study ranked writers and authors 5th and PR specialists 23rd among jobs most exposed to AI. AI now drafts blog posts, product copy, press releases and social posts, and edits and summarizes. A 2026 Muck Rack survey found 76% of PR pros use AI, mostly for editing, research and drafting. What stays human: knowing the audience, reporting and interviews, judgment in a crisis, media relationships, and taking responsibility for what gets published."},
  sentiment:"Many writers love the craft and the flexibility, but the mood in freelance copywriting is grim: published accounts from late 2025 describe clients replacing writers with AI or offering low-paid work fixing AI drafts. PR and in-house communications people are more upbeat, since AI speeds up their work, though about 61% worry clients will decide they no longer need content creators. Unstable income and constant hunting for work are common complaints.",
  path:"BLS says a bachelor's degree (English, communications, journalism) is generally needed for salaried jobs, and a portfolio matters most. People get in through student or local publications, internships, blogging, nonprofit work, or by writing for their current employer, such as newsletters or help docs. Common steps: content writer or communications coordinator, then senior writer, content strategist, or communications manager. Moving into technical writing or PR can be steadier.",
  notes:"No license required. Accreditation in Public Relations (APR) exists for PR people but is optional. Work is computer-based and often remote, with jobs concentrated in big media markets like New York, California, Texas and Washington, DC. Red flag: pure freelance blog and SEO writing is the most exposed to AI and shrinking. Writing tied to a specialty (health, finance, tech, law) or combined with strategy, editing or media relations holds up better.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Writers and Authors","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/media-and-communication/writers-and-authors.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Public Relations Specialists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/media-and-communication/public-relations-specialists.htm"],["Robert Half: Copywriter salary","https://roberthalf.com/us/en/job-details/copywriter"],["The Drum: As writing jobs go from 1 in 6 to 1 in 30 of agency postings, are copywriters in trouble? (2026)","https://www.thedrum.com/news/as-writing-jobs-go-from-1-6-to-1-30-of-agency-postings-are-copywriters-in-trouble"],["Matter Communications: Examining Muck Rack's State of AI in PR 2026 report","https://www.matternow.com/blog/examining-muck-racks-state-of-ai-in-pr-2026-report/"],["The Hustle: AI is coming for your online freelance jobs (Harvard, Imperial, DIW study)","https://thehustle.co/news/ai-is-coming-for-your-online-freelance-jobs"],["Simon Willison: Copywriters reveal how AI has decimated their industry (Dec 2025, links Blood in the Machine)","https://simonwillison.net/2025/Dec/14/copywriters-reveal-how-ai-has-decimated-their-industry/"]]},
 "media":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS medians (May 2025): graphic designers $62,960 (10th percentile $39,520, 90th $104,910); film and video editors $75,420 (10th $40,280, 90th $148,550); photographers $44,660 (10th $30,820, 90th $91,810). Robert Half's latest guide (labeled 2027) puts staff graphic designers at about $52,750 to $81,000 and video editors at about $60,750 to $90,250. Freelance income varies widely.",
  growth:"BLS projects 2025 to 2035: graphic designers down 2% (about 16,000 openings a year), photographers down about 1% (about 10,700 openings), and video editors up about 4% (about 3,100 openings). Openings come mostly from people leaving. BLS names AI design tools as a reason for weak designer demand, while demand for video content and effects supports editors. Studies found freelance graphic design jobs fell about 17% after ChatGPT launched.",
  ai:{rating:"High",text:"AI image and video tools now make stock images, simple graphics, social media visuals, headshots, background removal, rough cuts, captions and color fixes in minutes. Most new stock food photos are already AI-made, and AI headshots compete with portrait sessions. What stays human: brand identity and art direction, weddings and live events, real products and real people for major brands, news work (where AI images are often banned), and the taste to pick and refine."},
  sentiment:"People in these fields love making things and seeing their work out in the world. The big worries are AI-made images and cheap subscriptions undercutting freelance rates, and entry-level gigs drying up. A 2024 survey of 400 graphics professionals found nearly half expect manual graphic production to become obsolete within five years, and many reported stress and heavier workloads. Unsteady freelance income and client churn are long-standing complaints.",
  path:"BLS says designers and editors usually have a bachelor's degree, but a strong portfolio matters most and many people are partly self-taught; photographers often need only a portfolio and training. Common starts: in-house junior designer, production artist, assistant editor, second shooter, or freelancing for local businesses. Next steps are senior designer, art director, lead editor, or running your own studio. Mixing design, video and motion skills widens options.",
  notes:"No license is required. Software certificates (for example Adobe) are optional. Self-employment is common: about 20% of designers, 35% of editors and camera operators, and many photographers work for themselves, so you must find clients, price work and handle contracts. Photo and video work can mean lifting heavy gear, standing for hours, nights and weekends. Red flag: generic stock, simple logos and basic social graphics are the most exposed to AI.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Graphic Designers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/arts-and-design/graphic-designers.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Film and Video Editors and Camera Operators","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/media-and-communication/film-and-video-editors-and-camera-operators.htm"],["Robert Half: Graphic Designer salary","https://www.roberthalf.com/us/en/job-details/graphic-designer"],["Axios: How AI is disrupting the photography business (Sept 2025)","https://www.axios.com/2025/09/13/ai-photography-headshots-stock-images"],["The Hustle: AI is coming for your online freelance jobs (Harvard, Imperial, DIW study)","https://thehustle.co/news/ai-is-coming-for-your-online-freelance-jobs"],["TechRadar: Survey of creative professionals on AI and graphic design (CHILI publish, 2024)","https://www.techradar.com/pro/ai-could-terminate-graphic-designers-before-2030-survey-of-creative-professionals-finds"]]},
 "fin":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for financial and investment analysts is $102,740 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $63,720 and the top 10% over $180,860. Corporate FP&A pays a bit less than investment roles at the start: Robert Half's 2026 guide puts an FP&A analyst at about $71,250 to $88,000 (midpoint $80,500), with senior analyst and manager roles above that.",
  growth:"BLS projects 7% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), with about 24,700 openings a year in a field of about 377,200 jobs. Hiring news is mixed: Gartner's survey of 300+ CFOs (Oct 2025) found finance headcount growth slowing from 6% in 2025 to 2% in 2026 as budgets shift to AI, and big banks talked in 2026 about shrinking junior analyst classes.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"AI and finance software increasingly pull data, build first-draft models and variance reports, write commentary, and run forecasts, which is much of what junior analysts used to do by hand. Banks are testing AI on research and monitoring, and some executives openly plan smaller junior classes. Human work that holds up: explaining the numbers to managers, challenging assumptions, judging deals and strategy, and being accountable for the forecast."},
  sentiment:"FP&A analysts often describe the job as internal consulting and prefer it to accounting, liking the strategy projects and work with business leaders. Common complaints are repetitive budgeting and forecasting cycles, template-heavy work at some companies, and long hours during quarterly forecasts and the annual plan (one senior analyst described 60–70 hour weeks then). Investment and banking roles pay more but have much heavier hours.",
  path:"BLS says a bachelor's degree in finance, accounting, economics or business is typical, and internships help a lot. Common entries are financial analyst or FP&A analyst roles right after college, or moving over from accounting, banking or operations with strong Excel skills. Steps: analyst, senior analyst (2–4 years), FP&A manager, then director or CFO track. An MBA or CFA helps for investment roles and senior jobs.",
  notes:"Most corporate FP&A jobs need no license. Investment roles may need FINRA licenses, which the employer usually sponsors after hiring. CFA (investment) and FPAC (FP&A, from AFP) are optional credentials. Office work with crunch periods around month-end, quarterly forecasts and annual planning. Excel, financial modeling and increasingly SQL, BI and AI tool skills are expected. Investment roles cluster in big financial centers.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Financial Analysts","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/financial-analysts.htm"],["Robert Half: FP&A Analyst salary (2026)","https://www.roberthalf.com/us/en/job-details/fpa-analyst"],["CPA Practice Advisor: CFOs to prioritize growth functions, technology and AI in 2026 (Gartner)","https://www.cpapracticeadvisor.com/2026/02/10/cfos-budget-plans-prioritize-growth-functions-technology-and-ai-in-2026/177774"],["Fortune: Banks lay groundwork for workforce cuts as AI takes on junior analyst work (June 2026)","https://fortune.com/2026/06/07/banks-mass-workforce-cuts-ai-entry-level-jobs-junior-analysts/"],["Fishbowl: What's FP&A like? Work-life balance discussion","https://www.fishbowlapp.com/post/whats-fpa-like-do-you-like-it-whats-the-work-life-balance-life"]]},
 "acct":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for accountants and auditors is $83,680 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $56,020 and the top 10% over $144,090. Robert Half's 2026 Salary Guide puts the senior accountant midpoint at $94,750, and starting pay in tax, audit and assurance was rising faster (about 3.7% a year) than in accounting overall (about 2.1%). A CPA license usually raises pay and is needed for many senior public accounting roles.",
  growth:"BLS projects 5% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), with about 115,300 openings a year in a very large field of about 1.6 million jobs. Firms still report a strong hiring outlook: in AICPA's 2025 Trends report, about three in four firms that hired expected to hire as many or more. The talent pipeline is recovering: four-year accounting enrollment rose 8.9% in spring 2026, the third straight yearly increase.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"AI and automation already handle data entry, invoice processing, reconciliations, first-pass tax prep and research, and routine report building; Thomson Reuters found firm use of generative AI rose from 8% in 2024 to 21% in 2025. Big Four firms in the UK cut graduate intakes in 2023 partly as AI takes junior tasks. BLS expects this to shift work toward advisory and analysis rather than cut demand. Human work: judgment, audit sign-off, advising clients, and complex tax."},
  sentiment:"Accountants value job security, clear career steps and transferable skills. Public accounting gets the most complaints: a 2025 survey of audit and tax staff found nearly 80% worked over 51 hours a week in busy season, and seniors rated work-life balance worst. Another 2025 survey found poor firm support, health strain and dull, repetitive work drive people out more than hours alone. Industry and government jobs are usually calmer.",
  path:"BLS says a bachelor's in accounting or business is typical; bookkeepers and associate-degree holders can move up into junior roles. Classic path: public accounting staff, senior (2–3 years), manager, then partner, or moving to industry as senior accountant, controller and CFO. The CPA path changed in 2025–26: a model law update now allows a bachelor's plus two years of experience instead of 150 credit hours, and 42 states had adopted it by August 2026.",
  notes:"A CPA license (exam plus state rules) is needed to sign audits and for many senior roles; Minnesota's alternative path took effect Jan. 1, 2026, but a few states (Maine, North Dakota, Wyoming) still required 150 hours as of Aug 2026, so check your state board. Other credentials: CMA, CIA, EA for tax. Busy seasons (Jan–April for tax and audit) mean long hours. The long-feared CPA shortage is easing as enrollment and exam numbers rise.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Accountants and Auditors","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/accountants-and-auditors.htm"],["Robert Half: What's the typical senior accountant salary? (2026)","https://www.roberthalf.com/us/en/insights/career-development/whats-the-typical-senior-accountant-salary"],["CFO Brew: AICPA and NASBA approve alternatives to the 150-hour rule (May 2025)","https://www.cfobrew.com/stories/2025/05/15/aicpa-nasba-approve-alternatives-to-the-150-hour-rule"],["UWorld (CPA review vendor): Which states allow the 120-hour CPA pathway","https://accounting.uworld.com/?p=187121"],["Journal of Accountancy: Student enrollment in accounting continues to rise (June 2026)","https://www.journalofaccountancy.com/news/2026/jun/student-enrollment-in-accounting-continues-to-rise.html"],["CPA Practice Advisor: Senior accountants crushed by busy season stress (Aug 2025)","https://www.cpapracticeadvisor.com/2025/08/26/senior-accountants-crushed-by-busy-season-stress/167916/"],["Thomson Reuters (vendor blog): How will AI affect accounting jobs?","https://tax.thomsonreuters.com/blog/how-will-ai-affect-accounting-jobs/"]]},
 "advisor":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for personal financial advisors is $105,070 a year (May 2025). The spread is huge: the lowest 10% earn under $50,190 and the top 10% over $357,020, because pay often rides on fees or commissions tied to the assets you manage. Trainees start much lower and usually take years to build a book of clients; self-employed advisors earn through flat fees or commissions. Bank-branch roles (personal banker) sit at the low end of this range (not separately researched here).",
  growth:"BLS projects only 1% growth from 2025 to 2035 (slower than the 3% average), but about 17,100 openings a year, mostly from replacing people who retire or leave. Industry research agrees there is a retirement wave: Cerulli counted about 106,000 advisors (around 37%) planning to retire within 10 years. A July 2026 Wealth Management column argues the shortage is overstated, since AI tools and later retirements let each advisor serve more clients.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI already writes meeting notes, updates client records, drafts routine messages, and helps with planning models; one advisor-tech survey says this saves about a day a week. BLS notes AI advice tools may slow demand somewhat. What stays human: earning trust, coaching people through money fears and life changes, and complex planning. In a 2026 survey, 93% of advisors wanted final say over AI output and few would let it trade without review. Expect fewer support staff per advisor, not fewer advisors."},
  sentiment:"Established advisors tend to like the income, independence, and long client relationships. The hard part is the start: Cerulli found close to 75% of rookie advisors fail or leave within about three years, and most new advisors must build their own client list from scratch, which means a lot of prospecting and rejection. Juniors often get stuck serving small accounts for senior advisors. Fee-based planning firms with salaried roles are growing and are seen as a gentler entry than commission-only sales.",
  path:"Common starts are trainee programs at big firms, personal banker or insurance roles, or a paraplanner or associate job at a planning firm; people also switch in from sales, teaching, or accounting. Expect on-the-job training under a senior advisor for more than a year, plus securities or insurance licenses for the products you sell. BLS lists a bachelor's degree as typical. Later steps: lead advisor, team partner, then your own practice or buying a retiring advisor's book. The CFP adds credibility after about 2–3 years of experience.",
  notes:"Licenses depend on what you sell or advise on; firms managing money register with state regulators or the SEC. The CFP credential is optional but respected and needs coursework, an exam, ethics standards, and 6,000 hours of experience (4,000 via an apprenticeship pathway). Expect evening or weekend client meetings and some 40+ hour weeks. Red flag: commission-only offers with a low draw and big asset targets; ask how pay works in years 1–3 and what the trainee success rate is.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Personal Financial Advisors","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/personal-financial-advisors.htm"],["Institutional Investor: Wealth management's double attrition problem (Cerulli data, 2023)","https://www.institutionalinvestor.com/article/2bujzkg6xea9hjpl5jshs/ria-intel/wealth-managements-double-attrition-problem"],["Wealth Management: Why the advisor shortage narrative falls short (July 2026)","https://www.wealthmanagement.com/professional-development/why-the-advisor-shortage-narrative-falls-short"],["InvestmentNews: Advisors see AI as a growth partner, not a replacement (Advisor360 vendor survey, 2026)","https://www.investmentnews.com/goria/technology/advisors-see-ai-as-a-growth-partner-not-a-replacement/265543"],["CFP Board: Experience requirement","https://www.cfp.net/certification-process/experience-requirement"]]},
 "ops":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"For logisticians, BLS median pay is $82,320 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $50,890 and the top 10% over $133,160. Moving up into running operations pays more: general and operations managers have a median of $105,770 (May 2025, BLS data via a third-party site), from about $50k to over $250k. ASCM's 2026 salary survey puts the U.S. median at $98,500 base, with analysts near $88k, buyers near $65k, managers about $103k, and directors about $154k.",
  growth:"BLS projects logistician jobs to grow 18% from 2025 to 2035, much faster than average, with about 26,600 openings a year. Recent news is more mixed: in an ASCM and CNBC survey published January 2026, 32% of supply chain managers reported layoffs, often blamed on tariff costs, and warehouse job cuts jumped as automation spread. A late-2025 industry survey found 36% of companies added staff while 32% froze hiring, so smaller teams often carry the same work.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"AI and planning software increasingly handle routine calls like replenishment, order priority, demand forecasts, and tracking shipments, and robots are cutting warehouse jobs. But Gartner predicted in September 2026 that by 2030 only 5% of companies using planning automation will let it make even 10% of planning decisions on its own, citing messy data and the need for oversight. Humans keep network design, inventory policy, supplier and crisis decisions, and leading frontline teams."},
  sentiment:"People in the field say they are proud of the work: in ASCM's 2026 survey 89% said so, and senior people are the most likely to recommend it. Common complaints are pressure and unpredictability: tariff rule changes, cost spikes, and disruptions mean firefighting, and lean teams after hiring freezes. A 2026 executive survey ranked supply chain problems the top business risk, and many leaders said they are asking staff for more hours. Pay growth and clear promotion paths are seen as decent.",
  path:"Many start as an operations or logistics coordinator, buyer, analyst, dispatcher, or shift supervisor; warehouse workers, military logistics staff, and retail managers often move in. BLS lists a bachelor's degree as typical (supply chain or business), but experience can substitute and ASCM reports a $78k median even for people with only a high school diploma. Usual steps: coordinator or analyst (0–3 years), then planner or supervisor, then supply chain or operations manager, director, and VP of operations.",
  notes:"Certifications are optional but pay off: ASCM reports CLTD, CSCP, and CPIM holders earn about 15–30% more than uncertified peers. Defense jobs need specific federal acquisition training. Expect some evenings, weekends, or overtime, especially in manufacturing, and travel to plants or warehouses; site-based roles mean time on plant or warehouse floors. Manufacturing, the federal government, and transportation and warehousing are big employers. Tariffs and automation make some sectors volatile right now.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Logisticians","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/logisticians.htm"],["ASCM Supply Chain Salary and Career Report 2026","https://www.ascm.org/globalassets/ascm_website_assets/docs/salary/2026-ascmsalaryandcareerreport.pdf"],["Supply Chain 24/7: 32% of supply chain managers say tariffs are driving layoffs (Jan 2026)","https://www.supplychain247.com/article/32-of-supply-chain-managers-say-tariffs-are-driving-layoffs"],["Supply Chain 24/7: 2026 Supply Chain and Logistics Hiring Outlook","https://www.supplychain247.com/article/2026-supply-chain-and-logistics-hiring-outlook/supply_chain_management"],["Gartner: Only 5% of organizations will make 10% of planning decisions autonomously by 2030 (Sept 2026)","https://www.gartner.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2026-09-24-gartner-predicts-only-5-percent-of-organizations-will-make-at-least-10-percent-of-supply-chain-planning-decisions-autonomously-by-2030"],["Wagedex: General and Operations Managers (BLS OEWS May 2025 data)","https://wagedex.com/jobs/general-and-operations-managers"]]},
 "hr":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median pay for human resources specialists (which includes recruiters) is $75,940 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $47,180 and the top 10% over $128,720. HR managers, the usual next step, have a median of $149,280, from under $88,200 to over $267,810 (May 2025). Agency and tech recruiters may earn part of their pay as commission or bonus, so income can swing with hiring demand (pay mix not separately sourced here).",
  growth:"BLS projects 6% growth for HR specialists from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), with about 73,700 openings a year, and 6% for HR managers with about 16,800 openings a year. BLS expects outside HR service firms to grow faster than in-house HR teams. Recruiting is very cyclical: when tech hiring froze in 2022, companies that cut jobs cut recruiters by about half on average, and 2025 postings were fewer and more tightly justified, so recruiter demand rises and falls with the economy.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"Recruiting is one of the most AI-touched office jobs. In a 2025 poll, 57% of companies used AI in hiring, mainly to review resumes, check assessments, research candidates, and message applicants; some use it to schedule, transcribe, or even run first interviews. That squeezes coordinator and sourcing work. HR roles that stay human: employee relations and investigations, coaching managers, handling layoffs and sensitive cases, negotiating offers, and judging fit. Checking AI output for bias and errors is new work."},
  sentiment:"People like helping others and seeing the inside of a business, and many HR generalists report steady hours. Complaints: being caught between staff and leadership, taking the blame during layoffs and restructuring, and rising workloads; a 2026 HR Reporter piece found AI shifted work to checking its output rather than cutting it, and many fear being replaced by tools they help set up. Recruiters describe boom-and-bust cycles and huge application volumes from AI-written resumes and easy-apply tools.",
  path:"Common starts are recruiting coordinator, HR assistant, or agency recruiter; people often move in from customer service, retail management, sales, teaching, or office admin. BLS lists a bachelor's degree as typical (HR, business, communications), but agency recruiting often hires on drive and sales skills. Usual steps: coordinator or assistant (1–2 years), then recruiter or HR generalist, then senior or HR business partner (about 5+ years), HR manager, and head of people.",
  notes:"No license is required. Certifications help, especially for generalists: the aPHR needs only a high school diploma and no HR experience, the SHRM-CP has no set experience or education requirement, and the PHR needs 1–4 years of HR experience depending on your degree. Mostly office or remote work during business hours; recruiters may travel to job fairs and campuses. Big employers include professional services, staffing firms, healthcare, and government. Red flag: commission-heavy agency roles with no base pay.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Human Resources Specialists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/human-resources-specialists.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Human Resources Managers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/human-resources-managers.htm"],["HRD America: AI could run hiring processes by end of 2026, poll suggests (Resume.org poll, 2025)","https://www.hcamag.com/us/specialization/hr-technology/ai-could-run-hiring-processes-by-end-of-2026-poll-suggests/547332"],["Canadian HR Reporter: Is HR at risk of burnout amid surge in AI? (Sept 2026)","https://www.hrreporter.com/focus-areas/automation-ai/is-hr-at-risk-of-burnout-amid-surge-in-ai/394863"],["Recruiting News Network: Tech layoffs, recruiters don't feel so lucky (2022)","https://www.recruitingnewsnetwork.com/posts/tech-layoffs-recruiters-dont-feel-so-lucky"],["CareerXroads: How talent teams quietly recalibrated hiring in 2025 (Feb 2026)","https://community.cxr.works/blogs/shannon-nattar/2026/02/12/how-talent-teams-quietly-recalibrated-hiring-in-20"],["EANE: HR Certification Guide (aPHR, PHR, SHRM-CP eligibility)","https://www.eane.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/HR-Certification-Guide_.pdf"]]},
 "teach":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median pay is $63,820 for kindergarten and elementary teachers and $72,040 for high school teachers (May 2025). The lowest 10% earn about $47,000–49,000 and the top 10% about $102,000–108,000. NEA reports an average starting salary of $48,112 and an average teacher salary of $74,495 (2024–25), with big state gaps: average pay runs from about $55,000 in Mississippi to about $104,000 in California. NEA estimates teacher pay is down nearly 5% since 2017 after inflation.",
  growth:"BLS projects little or no change in jobs from 2025 to 2035 for elementary and high school teachers, but many openings from retirements and people leaving: about 99,400 a year for kindergarten and elementary and 63,500 for high school. Falling student enrollment and tight budgets have brought layoffs and hiring freezes in some districts, while special education and some regions still struggle to fill jobs. Where you live and what you teach matter a lot.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is becoming a planning helper: in a Gallup and Walton Family Foundation survey of the 2024–25 school year, about 6 in 10 teachers used AI and weekly users saved nearly six hours a week, mostly on lesson prep, worksheets, adapting materials, assessments, and paperwork. Grading and data analysis are less common uses, and AI grading has shown bias. The core stays human: managing a room, building relationships, motivating kids, spotting struggles, and working with families."},
  sentiment:"Teachers often love the kids and the sense of purpose. Stress has eased since the pandemic but is still high: a 2026 RAND survey found 55% of teachers report frequent job stress versus 34% of similar workers, and 57% report burnout. Fewer plan to quit (18%, down from 23% in 2022–23). Common complaints: pay that trails similar college-educated workers, buying supplies with their own money (94% do), side jobs (30% have one), and heavy special education caseloads.",
  path:"The classic route is a bachelor's degree with a teacher-prep program and student teaching, then a state license. Career changers can use alternative or residency routes: many states let you teach on a temporary license while finishing coursework, and in some states over half of candidates now come through non-university programs. Subbing or working as a paraprofessional is a good way to test the job first. Later options: lead or mentor teacher, instructional coach, then assistant principal and principal, which usually requires a master's degree.",
  notes:"All states require public school teachers to be licensed for their grade and subject, usually with a degree, student teaching, a background check, and exams; private schools often don't. Some states require a master's or ongoing training to keep the license. Most states have tenure protections after several years. Pay and demand vary hugely by state and subject; special education and some subjects are hardest to fill. New federal loan limits may make some certification programs harder to pay for.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: High School Teachers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/education-training-and-library/high-school-teachers.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Kindergarten and Elementary School Teachers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/education-training-and-library/kindergarten-and-elementary-school-teachers.htm"],["KPBS/NPR: Inflation is sucking the life out of teacher pay raises, report says (NEA data, Apr 2026)","https://www.kpbs.org/news/education/2026/04/27/inflation-is-sucking-the-life-out-of-teacher-pay-raises-report-says"],["K-12 Dive: Signs of stabilization as stress and exit plans drop for teachers (RAND, June 2026)","https://www.k12dive.com/news/signs-of-stabilization-stress-exit-plans-down-for-teacher-workforce/822932/"],["The 74: Survey finds 60% of teachers used AI and saved up to 6 hours a week (Gallup, 2025)","https://the74million.org/article/survey-60-of-teachers-used-ai-this-year-and-saved-up-to-6-hours-of-work-a-week/"],["Kelly Services (staffing firm): What the 2026 education workforce data reveals","https://kellyservices.com/insights/fewer-students-tighter-budgets-greater-needs-what-the-2026-education-workforce-data-reveals"],["WFAE: Three years into WS/FCS teacher residency program (Feb 2026)","https://www.wfae.org/2026-02-26/three-years-into-ws-fcs-teacher-residency-program-retention-and-pass-rates-exceed-state-averages"]]},
 "idtrain":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS (May 2025): training and development specialists earn a median $69,280, with the lowest 10% under $38,760 and the top 10% over $123,250. Instructional coordinators, the school-side version, earn a median $77,440 (10% under $47,980, top 10% over $121,670). Corporate instructional designers with strong e-learning and AI-tool skills tend to sit toward the upper end, but no reliable current ID-only salary survey turned up, so treat posting figures with caution.",
  growth:"For 2025 to 2035, BLS projects 11% growth for training and development specialists (much faster than average), about 46,000 openings a year from a base of 472,500 jobs. Instructional coordinators grow only 2%, with about 23,100 openings a year. ATD found over a third of organizations added talent development roles in 2024, but 2026 hiring commentary describes a tougher, more competitive market with AI-screened applications, so getting hired takes a strong portfolio.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"AI already drafts much of the production work: topic research, course outlines, storyboards and scripts, quiz questions, slides, captions and alt text. That means fewer hours per course and pressure on people who only build modules. What stays human is figuring out whether training is even the answer, choosing the learning strategy, managing stakeholders and subject experts, live facilitation, and proving the training changed results. In one hiring-manager poll most expected AI to change their team but not shrink it."},
  sentiment:"People like the creative mix of writing, design and teaching, the chance to work remotely, and seeing learners improve. Common complaints are being treated as order-takers who just make slides, tight deadlines from subject experts, and a crowded entry market full of career-changing teachers. In 2026 many worry about AI-driven layoffs and AI resume screening; others say learning AI tools has made them more valuable. Training budgets are often among the first cut in downturns.",
  path:"Most come in sideways: teachers, nurses, HR staff, or anyone who trains coworkers. A bachelor's degree is typical (business, education, communications, psychology); a master's in instructional design helps but is not required. The usual step is building a small portfolio (one e-learning module, a job aid, a short video) with tools like Articulate or Camtasia, then landing a trainer or junior ID role within 6 to 18 months. Later steps are senior ID, learning experience designer, L&D manager, and director of learning.",
  notes:"No license is required for corporate roles; certificates such as ATD's CPTD or APTD are optional. School-based instructional coordinator jobs often need a master's degree, teaching experience and sometimes a state license. Work is mostly desk-based with some presenting; trainers may travel to sites. Red flags: roles that are really slide production with no input on design, and contract gigs with no benefits. Remote jobs draw many applicants nationwide.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Training and Development Specialists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/training-and-development-specialists.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Instructional Coordinators","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/education-training-and-library/instructional-coordinators.htm"],["ATD: Benchmarks and trends from the 2025 State of the Industry report","https://www.td.org/content/atd-blog/benchmarks-and-trends-from-the-2025-state-of-the-industry-report"],["Devlin Peck (ID training seller blog): Will AI replace instructional designers?","https://www.devlinpeck.com/content/will-ai-replace-instructional-designers"],["eLearning Industry (event listing): How instructional designers get hired in 2026's tough job market","https://elearningindustry.com/elearning-events/how-instructional-designers-get-hired-in-2026s-tough-job-market-sept-4-2026"]]},
 "nurse":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median pay is $97,550 a year ($46.90 an hour) in May 2025; the lowest 10% earn under $68,940 and the top 10% over $137,470. Pay varies a lot by state and setting, and overtime, night and weekend differentials add to it. A 2026 Nurse.org reader survey (not nationally representative) put its median respondent in the $70k to $80k range and found a quarter say their income barely covers basics, so many nurses earn below the BLS median.",
  growth:"BLS projects 6% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), with about 180,800 openings a year in a field of about 3.47 million jobs, mostly from people retiring or leaving. Hospitals in 2026 are still adding clinical staff while cutting administrative roles, which fits the outlook.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is starting to help with charting: ambient listening tools that draft notes, early-warning alerts that flag patients who may be getting worse, and help with scheduling and staffing. Hands-on care, assessment, giving medications, judgment at the bedside, and talking with patients and families stay human, and licensing rules require a nurse to be responsible for care. In a 2026 survey only a quarter of nurses had used AI at work, and few trusted it yet for safe patient care."},
  sentiment:"Most nurses say the purpose of the work is why they stay, and many are glad they chose it. But burnout is high: a 2026 Cross Country and FAU survey found 67% report burnout, up from 39% in 2022, with short staffing and pay worries rising. A 2026 Nurse.org survey found job satisfaction falling and over 40% likely to leave the bedside within a year. Common complaints are patient loads, mandatory overtime, workplace violence and feeling undervalued.",
  path:"The usual route is an associate degree in nursing (ADN, about 2 to 3 years) or a bachelor's (BSN, 4 years, or 12 to 18 months accelerated if you already have a degree), then passing the NCLEX-RN licensing exam. Working as a nursing assistant (CNA, a few weeks of training) first is a cheap way to test the fit. Many start in hospitals, then move to specialties, clinics, case management or travel nursing. Further steps include nurse practitioner, nurse anesthetist, educator or nurse manager, which need graduate school.",
  notes:"A state license is required; the multistate Nurse Licensure Compact lets nurses in member states work across state lines. Many hospitals prefer or require a BSN. The work is physical: 12-hour shifts, lots of standing, lifting patients, nights, weekends and holidays. Unions are common in some states (Minnesota, California, New York) and less so elsewhere. Pay and staffing laws differ widely by state. Note that the burnout survey was co-run by a staffing firm.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Registered Nurses","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/registered-nurses.htm"],["Cross Country Healthcare and FAU: 2026 State of Nursing report","https://www.fau.edu/newsdesk/articles/state-of-nursing-survey-2026"],["Nurse.org: 2026 State of Nursing Survey","https://nurse.org/articles/state-of-nursing-survey-2026"],["Nursing Standard (RCNI): Effect of AI on nursing documentation and patient safety (2026)","https://journals.rcni.com/nursing-standard/evidence-and-practice/effect-of-artificial-intelligence-on-nursing-documentation-and-patient-safety-ns.2026.e12724/abs"],["StaffDNA (repost of Modern Healthcare): Hospitals keep hiring but nonclinical jobs face cuts","https://staffdna.com/news/hospitals-keep-hiring-but-nonclinical-jobs-face-cuts/"]]},
 "allied":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS medians, May 2025: dental hygienists $98,100, diagnostic medical sonographers $96,590, respiratory therapists $82,280, radiologic technologists $80,110, surgical technologists $64,650, and clinical lab technologists and technicians $62,930. The lowest 10% earn roughly $39k to $75k depending on the job, and the top 10% roughly $97k to $129k. Shortages have pushed sign-on bonuses and travel contracts in imaging, but no solid current figures were found for those.",
  growth:"BLS projections for 2025 to 2035: sonographers 14% (about 6,000 openings a year), respiratory therapists 9% (8,600), dental hygienists 8% (15,000), radiologic technologists 5% (about 15,300 a year counting MRI techs), surgical technologists 5%, and lab technologists and technicians 3% (20,800). Most beat the 3% average. Imaging groups reported record vacancy rates (18% for radiography in 2023), so hiring news agrees demand is strong.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is entering these jobs mostly as a helper: scanners that suggest positioning and protocols, faster image reconstruction, automated lab analyzers, and software that flags abnormal results. The core stays human: positioning and calming patients, running equipment safely, starting IVs, assisting in surgery, managing ventilators, and cleaning teeth. Routine lab bench work is the most exposed. Imaging experts expect routine scans to get more automated but see the tech role growing, not disappearing."},
  sentiment:"Techs and therapists like the steady demand, solid pay for a two-year program, and direct patient contact without the full weight of nursing. Complaints center on short staffing, burnout and feeling underappreciated: in a 2023 imaging workforce survey about half felt emotionally exhausted at least a few times a month and most saw coworkers leave in the past year. Others mention limited career ladders, on-call shifts, and wear on the body from lifting and standing.",
  path:"Most roles take a one- to three-year certificate or associate program at a community college or hospital, including clinical rotations, then a national certification exam (ARRT for radiography, ARDMS for sonography, NBRC for respiratory, NBSTSA for surgical techs). Competitive programs may have waitlists and prerequisite science classes. Career changers from CNA, medical assistant or phlebotomy jobs are common. Later steps are advanced modalities (CT, MRI), lead tech, educator, or department manager, often with a bachelor's.",
  notes:"Most states license or require certification for radiography, respiratory therapy and dental hygiene; rules for sonography and surgical techs vary by state. Choose an accredited program, or you may not be able to sit for the exam. Expect standing, lifting and moving patients, exposure to illness, and in imaging, radiation safety rules. Hospitals often need nights, weekends and on-call. Dental hygiene is mostly daytime clinic work. Some hospital techs are unionized, varying by region.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Radiologic and MRI Technologists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/radiologic-technologists.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Respiratory Therapists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/respiratory-therapists.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Diagnostic Medical Sonographers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/diagnostic-medical-sonographers.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Surgical Assistants and Technologists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/surgical-technologists.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Clinical Laboratory Technologists and Technicians","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/clinical-laboratory-technologists-and-technicians.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Dental Hygienists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/dental-hygienists.htm"],["Radiology Business: Rad tech groups propose new entry-level role to relieve staffing shortages (2024)","https://radiologybusiness.com/topics/healthcare-management/healthcare-staffing/rad-tech-groups-propose-creating-new-entry-level-role-relieve-radiology-staffing-shortages"],["AuntMinnie: Does AI technology pose a threat to radiologic technologists' jobs? (2022)","https://www.auntminnie.com/clinical-news/mri/article/15631035/does-ai-technology-pose-a-threat-to-radiologic-technologists-jobs"]]},
 "hadmin":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS (May 2025): medical and health services managers earn a median $123,860, with the lowest 10% under $73,390 and the top 10% over $224,340. Entry roles pay much less: medical records specialists, a common starting job in health information, earn a median $51,140 (10% under $37,000, top 10% over $81,150). Front-desk, patient access and coordinator jobs usually start in that lower range, so expect a big jump only once you reach management.",
  growth:"BLS projects 24% growth for medical and health services managers from 2025 to 2035, one of the fastest of any management job, with about 62,300 openings a year. Medical records specialists grow 8% (about 14,000 openings a year). Recent news is more mixed: in 2026 hospitals kept hiring clinical staff but cut thousands of administrative and support roles to save money, including Kaiser, HCA and Wellstar, so back-office jobs feel less secure than the projection suggests.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"AI is moving fast into the paperwork side of healthcare: insurance eligibility checks, prior authorizations, medical coding, predicting claim denials, scheduling, patient billing reminders, and drafting reports. BLS itself notes AI coding tools may reduce demand for records workers. Most exposed are routine coding, billing and data-entry jobs. What stays human is managing staff, handling exceptions and appeals, compliance judgment, budgets, working with doctors and nurses, and leading change, including choosing and rolling out these AI tools."},
  sentiment:"People like the mission of helping a hospital or clinic run, regular hours compared with clinical work, and a clear ladder into management. Complaints include being squeezed between executives and clinical staff, heavy regulation and insurance hassles, understaffed front desks, and middle-manager burnout. In 2026 many admin and IT staff are uneasy because cost-cutting layoffs have targeted nonclinical roles, while clinical jobs were spared.",
  path:"Common doors in are patient access, front desk, scheduling, medical records, billing or clinic coordinator jobs, often open with a high school diploma or certificate. A bachelor's in health administration, business, health information management or nursing is typical for management, and many directors hold a master's (MHA or MBA). Clinicians such as nurses also move into management. Expect several years from entry job to practice manager or department manager, and more to director or executive roles.",
  notes:"Nursing home administrators need a state license. Health information roles often ask for AHIMA credentials (RHIT with an associate degree, RHIA with a bachelor's) or coding certificates. Knowing the hospital's electronic records system (like Epic) and privacy law (HIPAA) helps a lot; Epic certifications are usually employer-sponsored. Work is mostly office-based with some on-site meetings. Watch out for roles in billing and coding most exposed to AI and outsourcing.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Medical and Health Services Managers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/medical-and-health-services-managers.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Medical Records Specialists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/medical-records-and-health-information-technicians.htm"],["Becker's Hospital Review: 4 systems cutting administrative, not clinical, roles (Oct 2026)","https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/finance/4-systems-cutting-administrative-not-clinical-roles/"],["StaffDNA (repost of Modern Healthcare): Hospitals keep hiring but nonclinical jobs face cuts","https://staffdna.com/news/hospitals-keep-hiring-but-nonclinical-jobs-face-cuts/"],["Health IT Answers (vendor executive predictions): Will AI continue to improve healthcare RCM in 2026?","https://www.healthitanswers.net/will-we-continue-to-see-ai-improve-healthcare-rcm-in-2026"]]},
 "counsel":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS medians for May 2025: social workers $61,780 (lowest 10% under $42,790, top 10% over $100,090); substance abuse, behavioral and mental health counselors $59,350 (10% under $38,940, 10% over $97,590); school and career counselors $64,330. Setting matters a lot: hospital social workers have a median of $77,440, while individual and family service agencies pay about $51,980. Licensed therapists in private practice can earn more, but that depends on caseload and insurance rates.",
  growth:"For 2025 to 2035 BLS projects 18% growth for mental health and substance abuse counselors (about 50,500 openings a year), 6% for social workers (about 69,100 openings a year), and 3% for school and career counselors (about 27,800 a year). Demand for care is real, but funding is shaky: in 2026 several behavioral health agencies cut staff, some citing Medicaid changes or lost federal crisis-line contracts. Expect plenty of openings, but check how secure an employer's funding is.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is mostly helping with paperwork: drafting progress notes, dictation, emails, and summaries. In a 2025 APA survey of psychologists, 29% used AI at least monthly (up from 11% in 2024), mostly for writing and notes, and 38% worried AI could make some of their duties obsolete. The core work stays human: building trust, crisis response, home visits, judgment calls on safety, and advocating for clients. Some states are drawing lines: Illinois in 2025 banned AI from making therapy decisions while allowing it for admin support."},
  sentiment:"People in these jobs often say the work is meaningful and that they would choose it again for the purpose. The steady complaints are large caseloads, heavy documentation, pay that feels low for a master's degree, and emotional exhaustion, especially in child welfare and community agencies. BLS itself notes understaffing and big caseloads make the work stressful. Recent news adds worry about funding cuts and layoffs at some agencies. Private practice and hospital roles tend to get better reviews than underfunded nonprofits.",
  path:"A common way in is a case aide, peer support, or human services assistant job, which needs a high school diploma or a bachelor's. A BSW opens many entry social work jobs. Clinical work (therapist, clinical social worker, licensed counselor) needs a master's (about 2 years, often 1 with a BSW), then roughly 2 years of supervised practice and a state exam. Career changers often go straight to an MSW or counseling master's. Later steps: clinical supervisor, program director, private practice, or school counselor.",
  notes:"Licensing is state by state: clinical social workers and mental health counselors must be licensed everywhere, which means a master's, supervised hours, and an exam. Public school counselors need a state credential and a background check. Some jobs involve evenings, weekends, on-call shifts, home visits, and crisis situations. Many jobs are in government or nonprofits, so pay and job security track public budgets. Public service loan forgiveness can matter for people with grad school debt.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Social Workers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/community-and-social-service/social-workers.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Substance Abuse, Behavioral Disorder, and Mental Health Counselors","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/community-and-social-service/substance-abuse-behavioral-disorder-and-mental-health-counselors.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: School and Career Counselors and Advisors","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/community-and-social-service/school-and-career-counselors.htm"],["APA Monitor: How AI is reshaping therapy (2025 Practitioner Pulse Survey), March 2026","https://www.apa.org/monitor/2026/03/ai-reshaping-therapy"],["Illinois IDFPR: Gov. Pritzker signs legislation prohibiting AI therapy (Aug 2025)","https://idfpr.illinois.gov/news/2025/gov-pritzker-signs-state-leg-prohibiting-ai-therapy-in-il.html"],["Becker's Behavioral Health: Behavioral health layoffs to know, 2026","https://www.beckersbehavioralhealth.com/workforce/6-behavioral-health-layoffs-to-know-2026/"]]},
 "trades":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS medians for May 2025: electricians $63,190 (lowest 10% under $42,640, top 10% over $108,510); plumbers, pipefitters and steamfitters $63,800 (10% under $44,150, 10% over $108,420); HVAC mechanics $61,010 (10% under $40,050, 10% over $95,210). Union work in big metro areas pays far more: a 2026 Fortune report cites an IBEW local near Washington, D.C. starting apprentices around $26 an hour and journeymen around $59.50 an hour plus benefits. Overtime is common and can add a lot.",
  growth:"BLS projects strong growth for 2025 to 2035: electricians 9% (about 72,700 openings a year), HVAC mechanics 11% (about 40,600 a year), and plumbers 7% (about 42,000 a year), all faster than average. News agrees for now: AI data centers, grid work, and retirements are driving demand for electricians, and NECA says commercial apprenticeship applications rose from about 70,000 to 120,000 between 2022 and 2024. Construction is cyclical, though, and some 2026 reporting warns the data center boom may cool before today's apprentices finish.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"The work is physical, done in a different building every time, and has to meet code and pass inspection, so AI has little direct reach. A 2025 Microsoft study of AI applicability put roofers among the least exposed jobs, and other hands-on trades sit near them. AI and software already help with estimating, scheduling, load calculations, diagnostics on smart equipment, and customer quotes. Prefabrication and robotics may trim some tasks. Troubleshooting, installing, and working safely around live systems stay human."},
  sentiment:"Tradespeople like earning while they learn, avoiding student debt, solid pay once licensed, and seeing real results at the end of the day. Common complaints are wear on the body, early starts, commuting to job sites, weather, and layoffs when construction slows. Some surveys rank trades low on happiness despite good pay. Young people are moving in: enrollment at trade-focused community colleges rose almost 20% from spring 2020 to spring 2025. Union members tend to report better pay, benefits, and training than non-union shops.",
  path:"Most people start as an apprentice, applying to a union program (such as IBEW or the pipefitters) or a contractor program, usually with a high school diploma and some algebra. Apprenticeships last 4 to 5 years with about 2,000 paid hours a year plus classes. A trade school certificate can shorten the wait or help HVAC entry. Veterans and people with construction experience may get credit. After journeyman licensing come foreman, estimator, master license, inspector, or running your own contracting business.",
  notes:"Most states license electricians and plumbers through exams on codes; licenses often need renewal and continuing education. HVAC techs need EPA refrigerant certification. The work involves heights, cramped spaces, heat and cold, lifting, and real risks such as shocks, burns, and falls. Union access and pay vary a lot by region; big metros and data center areas pay the most. Good apprenticeship slots can be competitive. Watch for shops that underpay unlicensed helpers with no real path to a license.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Electricians","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/construction-and-extraction/electricians.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Plumbers, Pipefitters, and Steamfitters","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/construction-and-extraction/plumbers-pipefitters-and-steamfitters.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Heating, Air Conditioning, and Refrigeration Mechanics and Installers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/heating-air-conditioning-and-refrigeration-mechanics-and-installers.htm"],["Fortune: AI data centers face an electrician shortage (March 2026)","https://fortune.com/2026/03/02/ai-data-centers-electrician-shortage-gen-z-training-careers/"],["Quartz: Electricians are training for a data center boom that may be over by graduation (July 2026)","https://qz.com/apprentice-electrician-data-center-timing-gap-071026"],["National Student Clearinghouse: Vocational-focused public two-years lead enrollment rise (May 2025)","https://www.studentclearinghouse.org/nscblog/vocational-focused-public-2-years-lead-enrollment-rise/"],["Fortune: Jobs least likely to be affected by AI, per Microsoft research (July 2025)","https://fortune.com/2025/07/31/jobs-careers-unlikely-to-be-impacted-by-ai-microsoft"]]},
 "eng":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS medians for May 2025: civil engineers $100,840 (lowest 10% under $68,240, top 10% over $163,220); mechanical engineers $104,110 (10% under $73,990, 10% over $164,340); electrical engineers $120,630 (10% under $76,550, 10% over $184,300). NACE projects an average starting salary of about $81,200 for 2026 engineering bachelor's grads. ASCE's 2025 salary report puts the median entry civil salary at $77,100 and says a PE license adds about $40,000 a year.",
  growth:"BLS projects for 2025 to 2035: mechanical engineers 11% (about 17,800 openings a year), electrical engineers 10% (electrical and electronics together about 16,300 openings a year), and civil engineers 6% (about 22,700 a year). Staffing firms in 2026 report more open roles than qualified candidates, especially civil and project engineers for infrastructure and electrical engineers for data centers. NACE expects flat overall new-grad hiring for 2026, but mechanical engineering is among the most sought majors.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI and software already speed up drafting, generative design options, simulation setup, code and spec lookups, report writing, and data analysis. An ACEC Research Institute study (2025) found most engineering firms still piloting AI, with liability and data quality as barriers, and firms see it as boosting licensed engineers rather than replacing them. Judgment on safety, signing off designs under a PE stamp, site visits, testing physical prototypes, and coordinating with clients and contractors stay human. Routine CAD and calc work may need fewer junior hours."},
  sentiment:"Engineers generally report good job security and satisfaction: in ASCE's 2025 survey, about 86% of civil engineers were satisfied with their jobs. Common gripes include pay that lags software jobs for similar effort, deadline crunches, and slow promotion at large firms or utilities. Mechanical and electrical engineers in manufacturing can face layoffs when a sector slows. Many say the first years of grunt work, like drafting and checking calculations, are the hardest part.",
  path:"The standard route is a 4-year ABET-accredited engineering degree, ideally with internships or co-ops. Take the FE exam near graduation, work about 4 years under a licensed engineer, then pass the PE exam (most important for civil). Career changers often start as an engineering technician or CAD drafter with a 2-year degree, then finish a bachelor's part-time. Later steps: senior or project engineer, project manager, principal, or moving into management, sales engineering, or consulting.",
  notes:"A PE license is needed to sign off designs and serve the public directly, especially in civil work; each state issues its own and some require continuing education. Civil and field roles mean site visits in all weather; mechanical and electrical roles may be in plants or labs. Some defense and utility jobs need U.S. citizenship or a security clearance. Pay and demand are strongest in growing metros and around data centers, energy, and infrastructure projects. Public sector jobs pay less but offer stability and pensions.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Civil Engineers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/architecture-and-engineering/civil-engineers.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Mechanical Engineers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/architecture-and-engineering/mechanical-engineers.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Electrical and Electronics Engineers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/architecture-and-engineering/electrical-and-electronics-engineers.htm"],["NACE: Salaries expected to climb for Class of 2026 grads despite flat hiring","https://naceweb.org/about-us/press/2026/salaries-expected-to-climb-for-class-of-2026-grads-despite-flat-hiring"],["ASCE: 2025 Civil Engineering Salary Report news","https://www.asce.org/publications-and-news/civil-engineering-source/society-news/article/2025/10/14/civil-engineering-salary-growth-outpaces-overall-workforce-according-to-new-report"],["ACEC Research Institute: The role of AI in the engineering industry (2025)","https://www.acec.org/resource/the-role-of-ai-in-the-engineering-industry/"],["Addison Group (staffing firm): Engineering hiring trends 2026","https://addisongroup.com/insights/engineering-hiring-trends-workforce-planning-guide-2026/"]]},
 "sci":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS medians for May 2025 vary widely by field: environmental scientists $82,220 (lowest 10% under $52,520, top 10% over $140,010); chemists $91,240 (10% under $58,460, 10% over $160,830); medical scientists $103,410 (10% under $64,800, 10% over $177,780); biochemists and biophysicists $127,410 (10% under $74,290, 10% over $201,110). NACE projects about $74,200 average starting pay for 2026 math and science bachelor's grads. Industry (pharma, biotech) usually pays more than universities or government.",
  growth:"BLS projects for 2025 to 2035: medical scientists 13% (about 10,500 openings a year), biochemists 12% (about 2,900 a year), environmental scientists 6% (about 7,300 a year), and chemists 6%. Recent news is much weaker than these projections: in 2025 federal grant cuts and agency layoffs hit research jobs, and Indeed data showed research postings down 18% from January to May 2025. Congress fully funded NIH in early 2026, but many labs and PhD programs shrank. Treat the BLS numbers as uncertain for now.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI already helps with literature searches, data analysis, coding, image analysis, protein and molecule design, and drafting papers and grants. Automated robot labs, like those profiled by NPR in 2026, now run repetitive pipetting and testing overnight and can even propose experiments. That shifts work toward planning experiments and checking results, and may mean fewer routine lab tech hours. Asking good questions, designing studies, judging whether results are real, field work, and leading a research program stay human."},
  sentiment:"Scientists love the curiosity and discovery, but many describe a tough market. Since 2025, US academic and government researchers report grant cuts, hiring freezes, rescinded offers, and lab layoffs; a 2026 Boston Globe survey of NIH-funded scientists found most delayed hiring and over two-thirds advise students to consider careers outside academia. Long postdoc years on modest pay and few faculty jobs are long-running complaints. Industry roles get better reviews for pay but can see biotech layoffs.",
  path:"A science bachelor's (biology, chemistry, environmental science, physics) leads to lab technician, research assistant, or environmental field jobs. Leading independent research usually needs a PhD (about 5 to 6 years, often paid by stipend) and often 2 to 5 years as a postdoc. A master's can open industry, government, and environmental consulting roles faster. Career changers often start as lab techs or data analysts. Later steps: senior or principal scientist, lab manager, faculty, or moving into regulatory, data science, or product roles.",
  notes:"Lab work can mean chemicals, biohazards, long hours on your feet, and odd hours for experiments; field scientists face weather and travel. Funding drives everything: academic and government jobs depend on grants and budgets, which became unstable in 2025. Jobs cluster in research hubs like Boston, the Bay Area, San Diego, and around federal labs. Licensing is rarely needed, though some environmental roles want certifications. A red flag is endless low-paid postdoc work with no path forward.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Medical Scientists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/life-physical-and-social-science/medical-scientists.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Environmental Scientists and Specialists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/life-physical-and-social-science/environmental-scientists-and-specialists.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Chemists and Materials Scientists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/life-physical-and-social-science/chemists-and-materials-scientists.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Biochemists and Biophysicists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/life-physical-and-social-science/biochemists-and-biophysicists.htm"],["Becker's: Scientific research job postings down 18% since January (May 2025)","https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/workforce/scientific-research-job-postings-down-18-since-january-report/"],["Boston Globe: Brain drain imperils Massachusetts biomedical future (March 2026)","https://www.boston.com/news/the-boston-globe/2026/03/06/my-scientific-career-is-essentially-over-a-brain-drain-imperils-massachusetts-biomedical-future/"],["NPR via WMFE: Researchers are building AI-powered robot labs (May 2026)","https://www.cfpublic.org/2026-05-26/researchers-are-building-ai-powered-robot-labs-what-does-this-mean-for-science"]]},
 "consult":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS counts consultants as management analysts: median $101,860 a year (May 2025), with the lowest 10% under $60,640 and the top 10% over $171,640. Top firms pay far more. In 2025 McKinsey, BCG, and Bain paid new undergrad hires about $110–112k base plus up to $18–22k bonus (about $137–140k total), Big 4 strategy arms about $90–112k base, and MBA hires about $190–192k base. These starting figures have been flat for about three years. Partners at big firms can earn $1M or more (industry estimate).",
  growth:"BLS projects 10% growth from 2025 to 2035, much faster than average, with about 94,100 openings a year in a field of about 1.08 million jobs. Hiring news is mixed: McKinsey, Bain, Deloitte, KPMG, and Accenture cut staff in 2025 (Deloitte US largely from lost federal contracts), and UK Big 4 graduate postings fell 44% in 2025. Yet McKinsey says it will grow North American headcount 12% in 2026, with junior ranks up as much as 20%. Experienced hires reportedly face the toughest entry in a decade.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"AI already does much of the junior grunt work: market research, data synthesis, first-draft slides, and summaries. McKinsey says it now runs about 20,000 AI agents next to roughly 40,000 people, and tests some candidates on working with its AI tool. What stays human: framing the client's real problem, judgment on messy tradeoffs, persuading executives, and leading change inside a client company. Expect fewer hours per project and more demand for people who can direct and check AI output."},
  sentiment:"People like the pay, fast learning, variety of industries, and strong exit options; per one 2025 analysis of MBB leavers, about 31% went to corporate strategy and 14% to finance. The big complaints are 55–65 hour weeks with spikes to 70–80, Monday to Thursday travel, little control over staffing, and up-or-out reviews. Work-life balance is the top reason people leave, and roughly 20% of big-firm staff leave each year. Recent layoffs and AI have added anxiety, especially for mid-level staff.",
  path:"The classic entry is an analyst or associate consultant job straight from a bachelor's degree, hired each fall after case interviews, or a consultant job after an MBA. Others move in from industry with deep expertise (IT, healthcare, supply chain) into tech or specialist consulting, which is the more realistic route from an unrelated job. Promotions come every 2–3 years for strong performers; partner usually takes 8–10+ years and few make it. Most leave within 3–5 years for corporate strategy, tech, finance, or startups.",
  notes:"No license is required; an MBA helps at top firms, and certifications like PMP or vendor tech certs help in IT consulting. About 14% of management analysts are self-employed. Expect heavy travel (often 50–80% on client projects) and long hours. Federal government is a big employer (18%), so budget cuts hit that segment hard. Top-firm hiring is extremely selective (under 1% of applicants at MBB), so practice case interviews early.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Management Analysts","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/management-analysts.htm"],["Poets&Quants: Consulting pay, what business graduates earned in 2025","https://poetsandquantsforundergrads.com/students/consulting-pay-what-business-graduates-earned-in-2025/"],["StrategyCase (case-prep firm blog): The consulting job market in 2026","https://strategycase.com/consulting-job-market/"],["People Matters: Big Four cut entry-level jobs amid AI and cost pressures (July 2025)","https://www.peoplematters.in/news/recruiting-and-onboarding/big-four-cut-entry-level-jobs-amid-ai-and-cost-pressures-42164"],["Fortune: How to get hired at McKinsey in the AI era (Jan 2026)","https://fortune.com/2026/01/14/how-to-get-hired-at-mckinsey-ai-tools-liberal-arts-creativity"],["Hacking the Case Interview (case-prep site): Pros and cons of consulting","https://www.hackingthecaseinterview.com/pages/pros-and-cons-of-consulting"]]},
 "entre":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"Owner income varies more than any job here, and BLS does not track business owners as one occupation. Payroll data from Gusto (a payroll vendor) shows the typical small business owner paid themselves about $4,800 a month (about $57,600 a year) in 2025, roughly 1.4 times their employees' pay, ranging from about $2,000 a month in food service to $7,400 in information businesses; this excludes profit draws. For comparison, BLS general and operations managers earned a median $105,770 (May 2025). Many owners earn little or nothing in the early years.",
  growth:"There is no BLS projection for owners; the closest, top executives, is projected to grow 5% from 2025 to 2035. Starting a business remains popular: Census counted about 535,000 new business applications in September 2026 alone. Survival is the hard part: BLS found about 80% of businesses born in 2013 survived one year, about 51% made it five years, and about 35% were still open after ten. The NFIB optimism index was 97.9 in September 2026, with finding good workers the top problem.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI cannot replace the owner, but it is changing the work. In a 2026 Goldman Sachs 10,000 Small Businesses survey, 76% of owners said they use AI, mostly for efficiency, though only 14% have built it into core operations. AI now helps with marketing copy, social posts, bookkeeping, customer emails, and research, which lowers the cost of starting. What stays human: picking the opportunity, selling, hiring and leading people, taking the risk, and building customer trust. AI also makes it easier for competitors to start."},
  sentiment:"Owners like the freedom, control, and building something of their own; about 31% in one 2026 survey said they love it and could not imagine anything else. The downside is heavy stress: in a 2026 survey by Patriot Software (a payroll vendor), about 48% had skipped or delayed their own paycheck, more than half lose sleep over the business at least a few times a week, and 84% said they had sacrificed health, relationships, or wellbeing. Rising costs, inflation, and hiring problems are common complaints.",
  path:"Anyone can start, with no degree needed. The most common realistic path is to learn an industry as an employee for a few years, then start a side business or freelance work, and go full time once it has steady paying customers. Others buy an existing business or a franchise. Free help is available from SBA resources, SCORE mentors, and Small Business Development Centers. Expect 2–5 years before income is stable. Success can lead to hiring a manager, opening more locations, or selling the business.",
  notes:"Licenses depend on the business and state (food, trades, child care, and health often need permits). You carry the risk: savings, personal guarantees on loans, and no employer health insurance or retirement plan. Hours are long, especially early. Watch out for franchise and coaching offers that promise easy income, and for business-in-a-box schemes. Keep personal and business money separate and set aside money for taxes. Survival rates differ a lot by industry and local market.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Top Executives","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/top-executives.htm"],["BLS TED: 34.7 percent of business establishments born in 2013 were still operating in 2023","https://www.bls.gov/opub/ted/2024/34-7-percent-of-business-establishments-born-in-2013-were-still-operating-in-2023.htm"],["Gusto (payroll vendor data): Small business owners earn 1.4x more than employees","https://gusto.com/resources/gusto-insights/small-business-owner-pay"],["Census Bureau: Business Formation Statistics","https://census.gov/econ/bfs/current/index.html"],["Goldman Sachs 10,000 Small Businesses Voices: AI presents a major opportunity for small businesses (2026)","https://www.goldmansachs.com/community-impact/10000-small-businesses-voices/insights/ai-presents-a-major-opportunity-for-small-businesses"],["Patriot Software (payroll vendor survey): Small business owner burnout survey 2026","https://www.patriotsoftware.com/blog/accounting/small-business-owner-burnout-survey/"],["TD Economics: U.S. NFIB Small Business Optimism Index","https://economics.td.com/us-nfib-small-business-optimism"]]},
 "sportsbiz":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"Entry pay is low and often commission-based. A 2026 NBA team posting for an account executive (ticket sales) offered a $30,000 base plus overtime and commission. Senior roles pay much more: BLS puts the median for marketing managers at $166,790 and for advertising and promotions managers at $133,660 (May 2025). Agents and business managers of artists and athletes had a median of $84,900 (May 2023, the newest figure I could open), with a wide range from about $47,100 to $239,200 or more. Big-market teams and leagues pay more than minor-league teams.",
  growth:"BLS has no single sports business occupation. For 2025 to 2035 it projects marketing managers to grow 7%, advertising and promotions managers to shrink 4%, and event planners to grow 6%. The industry itself is growing: Deloitte's 2026 outlook cites rising media rights and franchise values and double-digit growth in women's sports revenue, and NBA team staffs grew more than 250% from 2012 to 2022. Still, jobs are few compared with the number of applicants, so competition is intense (uncertain how much this has changed recently).",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"Teams are starting with back-office uses: finance reconciliations, season-ticket renewal outreach, dynamic ticket pricing, personalized offers, and AI-generated highlight clips (per Deloitte, a consulting firm with sports clients). AI agents may take over routine ticket purchases and fan service questions. What stays human: selling sponsorships and premium seats face to face, building relationships with fans and partners, running game-day events, and negotiating deals. Entry-level call-heavy sales roles are the most exposed."},
  sentiment:"People love working in sports and the game-day energy, but common complaints are low entry pay, long hours with nights, weekends, and holidays, and limited job security. Front-office staff describe days from before 9 a.m. to after 10 p.m. on home game days, small raises despite record team profits, mostly at-will jobs, and heavy layoffs and furloughs during COVID; some MLB staff have discussed unionizing. Burnout is a recognized industry issue. The passion for sports is often used to justify lower pay.",
  path:"The classic way in is an inside ticket sales job (often a 6–12 month class with 100 calls a day) or game-day event staff, internships, or volunteer roles while in school. A sports management or business degree helps but is not required; sales results matter most. From ticket sales, people move to account executive, premium or group sales, sponsorship and partnerships, marketing, or operations, then manager and director roles in 5–10 years. Coming from sales in another industry is a realistic way in.",
  notes:"Most front-office jobs require a bachelor's degree or at least an associate degree, and many postings ask for nights, weekends, and every home game. Pay is often base plus commission, so ask about the realistic total. Jobs cluster in cities with pro teams, colleges, and big venues, and openings are few, so moving for a promotion is common. Some league rules limit how teams can recruit staff from other teams. Watch for unpaid or very low paid internships.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Advertising, Promotions, and Marketing Managers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/advertising-promotions-and-marketing-managers.htm"],["BLS OEWS May 2023: Agents and Business Managers of Artists, Performers, and Athletes","https://www.bls.gov/oes/2023/May/oes131011.htm"],["TeamWork Online: Cleveland Cavaliers Account Executive, January 2027 class (job posting)","https://www.teamworkonline.com/basketball-jobs/cleveland-cavaliers/cleveland-cavaliers-28520/account-executive-january-2027-class-2186650"],["Deloitte (consulting firm): 2026 sports industry outlook","https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/insights/industry/technology/technology-media-telecom-outlooks/sports-industry-outlook.html"],["CBS Sports: Why baseball's next unionization effort could come from MLB front offices (2023)","https://cbssports.com/mlb/news/why-baseballs-next-unionization-effort-could-come-from-mlb-front-offices-were-not-protected-at-all"],["TurnkeyZRG (sports recruiting firm): What changed? Burnout in sports","https://www.turnkeyzrg.com/news/kellys-culture-what-changed-burnout-in-sports"]]},
 "event":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for meeting, convention, and event planners is $61,160 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $36,830 and the top 10% over $101,700. Pay is lower in arts and entertainment ($51,220 median) than in admin services or professional associations (about $63,000). PCMA's 2025 salary survey of about 275 mostly experienced planners found averages of about $67k for 1–3 years of experience, $95k for managers, $133k for directors, and $132k for those holding the CMP certification.",
  growth:"BLS projects 6% growth from 2025 to 2035, faster than average, with about 16,800 openings a year in a field of about 172,100 jobs. Industry mood is fairly upbeat: in American Express GBT's 2026 meetings forecast, 85% of planners felt optimistic, though cost and economic uncertainty were the top worries. Staffing has reportedly not recovered since COVID, which raises workloads more than it creates easy openings.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"Planners use AI mostly for writing and summarizing (46% in a small 2025 PCMA poll), data and reporting, and marketing; half plan to use it for agendas and attendee communications in 2026. AI-powered event apps, chatbots, and registration tools are spreading. What stays human: negotiating with venues and vendors, solving problems on site when things go wrong, managing staff and clients, and designing the experience. The work is physical and in-person, which limits automation."},
  sentiment:"Planners often love the work, variety, and the rush of a successful event, but burnout is a constant theme. A 2026 Skift Meetings report describes long hours without overtime, thin staffing since COVID, pay held down by passion for the job, and more than a third reporting anxiety or exhaustion. PCMA's 2025 survey found most work 41–60 hours a week, rated workload about 8 out of 10, and over a third felt underpaid. Some leave corporate jobs to work as independent planners.",
  path:"Common starts are event coordinator or assistant roles at hotels, venues, convention centers, associations, or agencies, or catering, hospitality, and AV jobs; volunteering to run events also counts. BLS lists a bachelor's degree (business, communications, hospitality) as typical, but experience matters a lot and people often switch in from hospitality, admin, or teaching. After 3–5 years many earn the CMP and become planners or managers, then directors of events, producers, or independent business owners.",
  notes:"The Certified Meeting Professional (CMP) credential needs work experience and an exam, and was linked to higher pay in PCMA's survey. Expect travel, weekends, and very long days on site, often on your feet. Many work as independent contractors (about 10% are self-employed per BLS). Demand drops in recessions, when companies cut meeting budgets first. Jobs cluster where hotels, convention centers, and associations are, so local demand varies.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Meeting, Convention, and Event Planners","https://www.bls.gov/OOH/business-and-financial/meeting-convention-and-event-planners.htm"],["PCMA Convene: Salary Survey 2025, Payment Due?","https://www.pcma.org/convene-salary-survey-2025-payment-due/"],["Skift Meetings: Why event planners are walking away from jobs they love (Aug 2026)","https://meetings.skift.com/2026/08/13/why-event-planners-are-walking-away-from-jobs-they-love/"],["PCMA Convene: How event planners use gen AI (Mar 2025)","https://pcma.org/event-planners-gen-ai"],["Associations Now: Meeting planners upbeat entering 2026","https://associationsnow.com/2026/01/meeting-planners-upbeat-entering-2026/"]]},
 "realestate":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS medians for May 2025: real estate sales agents $52,830 (lowest 10% under $32,970, top 10% over $123,590), brokers $73,220 ($37,110 to $143,300), and property managers $69,990 ($41,010 to $139,680). Agent pay is almost all commission, so income is irregular and the first year is often thin. NAR's 2025 member survey put the typical Realtor's gross income at $58,100 for 2024 on about 10 deals, before fees, dues, and marketing costs. Property managers more often earn a steady salary.",
  growth:"BLS projects 2% growth for agents and brokers from 2025 to 2035 (slower than average), with about 40,400 openings a year, and 4% for property managers, with about 36,900 openings a year. Recent news is weaker for sales: NAR membership fell from a 1.6 million peak in 2022 to about 1.4 million in 2025, with 1.2 million projected for 2026, as a slow housing market and the 2024 commission settlement squeeze part-time agents. Property management demand is steadier because rentals need managing in any market.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI already drafts listing descriptions and social posts, answers and sorts incoming leads, schedules showings, runs follow-up emails, makes market reports, and does virtual staging. Tech platforms may also pull some buyers and sellers away from agents. What stays human is the core of the job: earning a client's trust, showing homes, reading a local market, negotiating, handling problems during inspections and closing, and, for property managers, dealing with tenants, repairs, and owners in person."},
  sentiment:"Many in the field like the independence, the people side, and the upside when deals close. The common complaints are irregular income, paying fees and dues up front, constant prospecting, evening and weekend work, and a slow housing market that has pushed many agents to take side jobs or leave. Industry voices expect full-time agents to hold up better than part-timers, with business consolidating toward top producers. Property managers more often mention tenant conflict and on-call stress than income swings.",
  path:"For sales, take your state's prelicensing course (often a few weeks to a few months), pass the exam, and join a brokerage; many people switch in from retail, customer service, or other sales. Expect a slow first year or two while you build a client base. After about two years as an agent, most states let you qualify for a broker license, which lets you run your own office. Property managers often start as leasing consultants or assistant managers and move up to managing larger portfolios or regional roles.",
  notes:"Every state requires a license, with renewal every 2 to 4 years and continuing education; licenses usually do not transfer between states. Many property managers also need a license, often a broker or property management license, depending on the state. The 2024 NAR commission settlement changed how buyer agents get paid, and its full effect is still playing out. Budget for desk fees, dues, and marketing before your first sale. Work is local, with lots of driving.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Real Estate Brokers and Sales Agents","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/sales/real-estate-brokers-and-sales-agents.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Property, Real Estate, and Community Association Managers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/property-real-estate-and-community-association-managers.htm"],["NAR: Realtors show strong commitment to profession amid market headwinds (2025 Member Profile)","https://www.nar.realtor/newsroom/realtors-show-strong-commitment-to-profession-amid-market-headwinds-new-nar-report-finds"],["HousingWire: Business practices changed but real estate agents remained active in 2025","https://www.housingwire.com/articles/business-practices-changed-but-real-estate-agents-remained-active-in-2025/"],["Propmodo: Housing market stagnation drives mass exodus from brokerage ranks","https://propmodo.com/housing-market-stagnation-drives-mass-exodus-from-brokerage-ranks/"],["Luxury Presence (vendor blog): Ways to use AI in real estate in 2026","https://www.luxurypresence.com/blogs/ai-in-real-estate/"]]},
 "legal":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median pay for paralegals and legal assistants is $62,890 a year (May 2025). The lowest 10% earn under $44,740 and the top 10% over $101,500. Pay is higher in big-city law firms, federal government, and specialties such as litigation, intellectual property, and corporate work, and lower at small local firms. Many paralegals are paid hourly and can earn overtime during trials and deadlines.",
  growth:"BLS projects little or no change (0%) in jobs from 2025 to 2035, but about 40,800 openings a year because people retire or move to other jobs. BLS says technology, including AI, is the main brake, since it makes research and document work faster. A 2026 legal industry survey (vendor-sponsored) found 39% of respondents expect fewer paralegal or support roles, while 22% expect those roles to grow or change.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"Much of a paralegal's routine work is exactly what legal AI tools target: first drafts of standard documents, summarizing records and depositions, document review in discovery, basic legal research, intake, and cite checking. Firms are already trimming manual, repetitive tasks. What stays human is organizing complex cases, working with clients and witnesses, managing deadlines and court filings, trial support, and checking AI output, which matters because AI tools still invent case citations."},
  sentiment:"People like the steady demand, the variety of cases, and getting real legal work without law school. Common complaints are tight deadlines, heavy caseloads without added help, repetitive tasks, and feeling underpaid or undervalued compared with attorneys. In one 2025 survey, about 30% of paralegals said they feel burned out often and another 44% sometimes. Some worry about AI shrinking the role, while others see learning legal tech as a way to stand out.",
  path:"The usual way in is an associate degree or a paralegal certificate; career changers with a bachelor's degree can often finish a certificate in under a year. Some people start as legal secretaries or file clerks and are trained on the job. After a few years, picking a specialty (such as litigation, real estate, or IP) raises pay. Next steps are senior paralegal, paralegal manager, litigation support or legal tech roles, compliance work, or law school.",
  notes:"No license is needed in most states, but voluntary certification from a national paralegal association is preferred by some employers and can help with hiring and pay. Paralegals cannot give legal advice or represent clients, and work under an attorney. Expect long hours around trials and filing deadlines, mostly at a desk.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Paralegals and Legal Assistants","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/legal/paralegals-and-legal-assistants.htm"],["Texas Bar Blog (sponsored by 8am, a legal software vendor): What AI means for law firm hiring, staffing and career paths","https://blog.texasbar.com/2026/05/articles/sponsored-content/sponsored-content-what-ai-means-for-law-firm-hiring-staffing-and-career-paths/"],["Rev (vendor blog): How to overcome paralegal burnout","https://www.rev.com/blog/paralegal-burnout"],["ABA GPSolo: Suspect hallucination when generative AI finds the perfect case","https://www.americanbar.org/groups/gpsolo/resources/magazine/2026-may-jun/suspect-hallucination-when-generative-ai-finds-perfect-case"]]},
 "attorney":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median pay for lawyers is $159,670 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $78,360 and the top 10% over $351,600. Pay is split sharply by job type. The median starting salary for the Class of 2024 was about $95,000 (NALP), but large firms paid most first-year associates $200,000 to $225,000 in 2025, while small firms, government, and public interest jobs often start well under $100,000. Federal government lawyers had a median of $178,380.",
  growth:"BLS projects 5% growth from 2025 to 2035, faster than average, with about 28,700 openings a year. Recent hiring agrees for now: the Class of 2024 had the highest employment rate NALP has ever recorded, at 93.4%, with 84% in jobs requiring bar passage. NALP warned that federal hiring freezes and cuts to public interest grants could slow government and nonprofit hiring.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"AI tools now draft first versions of contracts and briefs, summarize documents, speed up legal research, and handle much of document review, the work junior associates used to do for hours. That may mean fewer entry-level jobs over time and pressure on hourly billing. The human core stays: legal judgment, advising clients, negotiating, arguing in court, and taking responsibility for every filing. Courts have found well over 1,000 U.S. filings with AI-invented citations, so checking AI work is now part of the job."},
  sentiment:"Lawyers often like the intellectual challenge, the pay in larger firms, and helping clients through hard problems. Heavy complaints center on billable-hour targets, always being on call, and stress: in a 2025 survey of more than 3,100 mostly big-firm lawyers and staff, about 66% said billable-hour pressure hurts their mental health and about 69% reported anxiety. Stress and hours vary a lot by employer and practice area.",
  path:"The standard route is a four-year bachelor's degree in any major, the LSAT, three years of law school for a J.D., and then the bar exam: about seven years after high school. Working as a paralegal or in another job first is common and can help admissions. Most start as associates at firms, as government attorneys such as prosecutors or public defenders, or as judicial clerks. Later steps include partner, in-house counsel, general counsel, judge, or running your own practice.",
  notes:"You need a license from each state where you practice, which usually means passing that state's bar exam and a character and fitness review, plus continuing education. The bar exam is changing: the new NextGen exam starts in many states, including New York, in 2028. Law school is expensive, so compare debt with likely pay before enrolling. Hours vary widely by practice area, and big-firm jobs are concentrated in large cities.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Lawyers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/legal/lawyers.htm"],["NALP: 2025 Associate Salary Survey research","https://www.nalp.org/0625research"],["ABA (2Civility): 2024 law school graduates report highest employment rate ever","https://www.2civility.org/2024-law-school-graduates-report-highest-employment-rate-ever/"],["ABA Journal: More lawyers report billable hour pressures are affecting their mental health","https://www.abajournal.com/news/article/more-lawyers-report-billable-hour-pressures-are-affecting-their-mental-health-survey-finds"],["ABA GPSolo: Suspect hallucination when generative AI finds the perfect case","https://www.americanbar.org/groups/gpsolo/resources/magazine/2026-may-jun/suspect-hallucination-when-generative-ai-finds-perfect-case"],["NCBE: New York to administer NextGen bar exam beginning July 2028","https://www.ncbex.org/news-resources/new-york-administer-nextgen-bar-exam-beginning-july-2028"]]},
 "ea":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median pay for executive secretaries and executive assistants is $76,590 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn about $50,560 and the top 10% about $109,850. General administrative assistants earn less, with a median of $48,310. Senior EAs supporting top leaders at finance, tech, or professional-services firms can top $100,000 with bonuses. A 2026 survey of experienced EAs (vendor-run) found the typical respondent earning $75,000 to $99,000, with the highest pay in New York and the Bay Area.",
  growth:"BLS projects no growth (0%) for executive assistants from 2025 to 2035 and a 2% decline for secretaries and administrative assistants overall, though turnover still creates about 314,400 openings a year across the whole group. BLS cites AI and digital tools, and managers doing more of their own scheduling and documents. Recent news fits: in 2026 PwC laid off about 600 US executive assistants and other support staff, and McKinsey also cut or moved support roles.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"AI already handles a lot of routine EA work: scheduling and calendar juggling, drafting emails and agendas, meeting notes, expense reports, and booking travel. That is why firms are cutting assistant headcount and having one EA support more leaders. What stays human is judgment and trust: guarding a leader's time, handling sensitive information, smoothing relationships, running projects and events, solving last-minute problems, and keeping an office running in person. Strategic, chief-of-staff-style EAs are safer than pure schedulers."},
  sentiment:"People who like the job enjoy variety, being at the center of things, and the trust that builds with one leader. Common complaints are being always on call, unclear boundaries, feeling undervalued, and pay that depends heavily on the industry; in one 2026 EA survey, few law and media EAs felt fairly paid while most finance EAs did. Most surveyed EAs expect AI to enhance their role rather than replace it, but recent layoffs at big firms have made job security a bigger worry.",
  path:"Many people start as receptionists, administrative assistants, or office coordinators with a high school diploma or some college, and move up to executive assistant after a few years of proving they are reliable. Strong skills in Microsoft Office or Google Workspace, calendar and travel management, and writing matter more than a degree. After several years supporting senior leaders, next steps include senior EA to a CEO, office manager, chief of staff, operations, or project management.",
  notes:"No license is required; employers typically ask for a high school diploma and some related experience. The work is mostly at a desk and often on site, though remote EA jobs exist. Expect some after-hours messages, especially when supporting executives. Pay is highest in big metro areas and in finance, tech, and consulting. Office managers are counted by BLS under office supervisors, a separate group not covered here.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Secretaries and Administrative Assistants","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/office-and-administrative-support/secretaries-and-administrative-assistants.htm"],["WageDex (BLS OEWS May 2025 data): Executive Secretaries and Executive Administrative Assistants","https://wagedex.com/jobs/executive-secretaries-and-executive-administrative-assistants"],["Bloomberg Government: Executive assistants making $100,000 a year lose jobs to AI (May 2026)","https://news.bgov.com/artificial-intelligence/executive-assistants-making-100-000-a-year-lose-jobs-to-ai"],["Vimcal (vendor report): 2026 Executive Assistant Report","https://www.vimcal.com/ea/2026-report"]]},
 "contracts":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS has no separate contracts manager occupation. The closest, buyers and purchasing agents, had a median of $77,710 in May 2025 (10th percentile under $48,380, 90th over $128,870); purchasing managers had a median of $148,080. Job-posting and salary sites put contracts managers higher than buyers: Indeed shows an average base of about $115,800 (Oct 2026, about 3,000 salaries) and Salary.com about $134,600 (Oct 2026). Associate contract administrators average about $83,500 on Salary.com. Defense, tech, and construction contracts tend to pay at the top of the range.",
  growth:"Using purchasing as the closest match, BLS projects 6% growth from 2025 to 2035, faster than the 3% average, with about 53,600 openings a year across 604,400 jobs. There is no BLS projection for contracts managers specifically, so treat this as a rough guide. Federal contracting is a weak spot: 2025 hiring freezes and buyouts cut the government acquisition workforce, which law firm reporting says raised workloads and delayed contract actions. That same reporting suggests contractors may pick up more work, and private-sector demand looks steadier, though this is uncertain.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"Contract software with AI already drafts from approved clause libraries, flags risky or missing terms, routes approvals, and tracks renewal dates and obligations after signing. Industry figures, mostly from vendors, claim big cuts in drafting and review time, and some legal teams report small headcount reductions. What stays human is negotiating with the other side, judging which risks the business can accept, handling exceptions, and keeping relationships with sales, finance, and suppliers. Expect fewer people doing routine administration and more value on negotiation and judgment."},
  sentiment:"Good public data on how contracts managers feel is thin, so treat this as uncertain. A 2025 PayScale survey cited by Vault found most contract administrators were highly satisfied with their choice, and the work is usually a regular office schedule. Common pressure points are being squeezed between sales teams who want deals fast and lawyers who want less risk, and heavy workloads in federal contracting after the 2025 staff cuts. Many in the field are watching AI tools closely, with mixed feelings about whether they help or shrink entry-level roles.",
  path:"Common entry points are contracts administrator, procurement or purchasing roles, paralegal or legal assistant work, and sales operations. People from unrelated jobs often move in by handling contracts informally first, then taking a formal administrator title. A bachelor's degree in business, supply management, or a related field is typical, and BLS notes purchasing managers usually need several years of experience. A common arc is administrator for 2–4 years, then contracts manager, then senior manager or director of contracts. Some later go to law school or move into procurement leadership.",
  notes:"No license is required. NCMA offers certifications: CFCM for federal work (focused on the Federal Acquisition Regulation), CCCM for commercial work, CPCM for broad mastery, and an entry-level CCMA. Federal contracting officers need agency warrants and specific training. Government contracting jobs cluster near Washington, D.C. and defense hubs, and some need a security clearance. The work is desk-based with deadline crunches at quarter and fiscal-year end. Be careful with salary sites: small samples give very different numbers.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Purchasing Managers, Buyers, and Purchasing Agents","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/purchasing-managers-buyers-and-purchasing-agents.htm"],["Indeed: Contract manager salary","https://www.indeed.com/career/contract-manager"],["Salary.com: Contract associate manager salary (with related titles)","https://www.salary.com/research/salary/position/contract-associate-manager-salary"],["NCMA: Certification","https://www.ncmahq.org/certification"],["Inside Government Contracts (Covington): DoD is shrinking its civilian workforce","https://www.insidegovernmentcontracts.com/2025/04/dod-is-shrinking-its-civilian-workforce-what-contractors-should-know-now/"],["ALA Legal Management (by a contract software CEO): AI and contract management","https://www.alanet.org/legal-management/2025/may/departments/legal-administrators-ai-revolution-contract-management"],["Vault: Contract administrators work environment","https://legacy.vault.com/industries-professions/professions/c/contract-administrators/work-environment"]]},
 "legalops":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS has no separate occupation for this; paralegals and legal assistants are the closest, with a median of $62,890 in May 2025 (10th percentile under $44,740, 90th over $101,500). A legal staffing firm's 2026 guide puts litigation support specialists at about $55,000 entry, $72,000 mid, and $95,000 senior, with Manhattan near $104,000. Legal operations roles inside companies pay more: a 2024 Brightflag survey found heads of legal operations averaged about $192,000, and manager pay ranged widely from about $52,000 to $237,000. PayScale data for e-discovery specialists (median about $55,000) is from 2023 and may be stale.",
  growth:"BLS projects 0% growth for paralegals from 2025 to 2035, still with about 40,800 openings a year from turnover, and says AI may cut demand for research and document work. There is no BLS projection for e-discovery or legal ops. Industry reporting is mixed: corporate legal departments say budgets and headcount are flat while demand rises, which pushes more work to legal ops, but first-pass document review jobs at outside vendors look at risk from AI. Recent hiring data from staffing firms was mostly 2022–2024 and described strong demand for project managers; newer figures were not found.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"Generative AI is now used for first-pass document review, sorting documents by relevance and privilege, summaries, and building timelines. Some vendor-sponsored writers say outsourced human review may soon disappear; law firm reporting is more cautious and says retrained reviewers are still needed to check and validate AI output. Legal ops work like choosing tools, managing outside law firm spend, setting up workflows, and governing AI use is more secure and may grow. Entry-level review and data processing roles face the most pressure."},
  sentiment:"People in e-discovery often describe deadline-driven work with late nights before productions, and an industry trade group headline in 2024 noted continued burnout despite strong hiring. Many roles have been remote or hybrid since the pandemic. Legal ops staff tend to like the mix of law, tech, and business, but say the role is still poorly understood and title alone does not predict pay. The big worry right now is AI shrinking review and junior roles, while those with tool and project skills feel in demand.",
  path:"Many start as paralegals, legal assistants, document reviewers, or IT staff at a law firm, then move to litigation support or e-discovery analyst. People from unrelated jobs can enter through a paralegal certificate or by earning a Relativity certification and taking a review or processing role. Typical steps: analyst 1–3 years, project manager, then senior manager. Legal ops at a company often follows experience in a legal department, operations, or analytics, leading to manager, director, or head of legal operations. A law degree is not required.",
  notes:"No license is required. Relativity certifications are widely asked for: Pro levels need little study, while Certified Administrator needs about 40 hours of study and six months of hands-on use. ACEDS offers a broader e-discovery credential, and CLOC is the main legal ops community. Jobs cluster in big legal markets like New York, Washington, D.C., Chicago, and California. Pay gaps by gender were reported in the 2024 Brightflag survey. Watch for contract-only review gigs with no benefits.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Paralegals and Legal Assistants","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/legal/paralegals-and-legal-assistants.htm"],["Careerscape (staffing firm): Litigation support specialist salary guide","https://cs-recruiters.com/salary-guide/litigation-support-specialist/"],["Legal Dive: Brightflag 2024 legal operations compensation survey","https://legaldive.com/news/legal-operations-compensation-survey-2024-brightflag-legal-operators/710739"],["eDiscovery Today (sponsored by an AI review vendor): Is the death of outsourced document review around the corner?","https://ediscoverytoday.com/2025/03/03/is-the-death-of-outsourced-document-review-around-the-corner-ediscovery-trends/"],["Morgan Lewis: Three years in, has generative AI made a meaningful contribution to e-discovery?","https://www.morganlewis.com/news/2025/09/three-years-in-has-generative-ai-made-a-meaningful-contribution-to-e-discovery"],["Relativity: Certifications","https://relativity.com/resources/certification/"],["CLOC: 2026 State of the Industry report preview","https://cloc.org/?p=15973"],["ACEDS: eDiscovery trends show continued burnout despite strong hiring (2024)","https://aceds.org/ediscovery-trends-show-continued-burnout-despite-strong-hiring/"]]},
 "policy":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"Policy analysts are spread across several BLS groups. Political scientists, a small group mostly in federal jobs, had a median of $142,080 in May 2025 (10th percentile under $83,350, 90th over $195,190), but most analysts earn less. Urban and regional planners had a median of $89,320 (10th under $60,010, 90th over $134,490). Indeed shows policy analyst job postings averaging about $88,800 (Oct 2026, 705 salaries). Entry roles like legislative aide or program assistant usually pay much less, and nonprofit and state jobs pay below federal and private-sector work.",
  growth:"BLS projects political scientists to shrink 2% from 2025 to 2035, with only about 400 openings a year, because demand depends on federal funding. Urban planners are projected to grow 4%, with about 3,200 openings a year. Recent news is worse than these projections for the federal side: OPM reported about 317,000 federal departures and only about 68,000 hires in 2025. Nonprofits also cut about 28,700 jobs in 2025 per Challenger, Gray and Christmas, while job applications rose sharply. State and local government, lobbying, and private consulting are steadier.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"An NCSL survey found 44% of legislative staff used AI tools in 2025, double the year before, mostly to summarize documents, research, analyze policy materials, edit, and transcribe. A 2026 George Mason pilot found AI good at summaries, comparisons with past laws, and spotting gaps, but weaker than human drafters at persuasion and at reading political dynamics, and sometimes overconfident. Humans still own judgment, building coalitions, testifying, and deciding which options are politically possible. Routine research and first drafts will need fewer hours."},
  sentiment:"People choose this work for the mission and often say it is meaningful but not well paid, especially at nonprofits and early on. Since 2025, policy graduates and job seekers describe a crowded market, with laid-off federal workers and new grads competing for the same government and nonprofit roles. Funding swings and political changes create job insecurity. Those in local government or private consulting tend to report steadier jobs and sometimes better pay than expected.",
  path:"Common starting jobs are legislative aide, program assistant, research assistant at a think tank, or campaign staff. Many get a master's in public policy or administration after a few years; a Harvard Kennedy School 2025 snapshot shows about a third of its policy grads went to government, a quarter to nonprofits, and the rest mostly private sector. People from other fields can enter by bringing expertise in an issue such as health, energy, or tech. Next steps are senior analyst, policy director, legislative director, or agency program manager.",
  notes:"No license is needed, though planners often seek AICP certification. A master's is typical for analyst roles and BLS lists it as the usual entry level for political scientists, though a bachelor's can get you into campaigns and entry government jobs. Jobs cluster in Washington, D.C. and state capitals like St. Paul. Federal jobs may need security clearances and can be affected by hiring freezes. Campaign and legislative jobs bring long hours in session or election season. Unpaid internships remain common and favor people who can afford them.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Political Scientists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/life-physical-and-social-science/political-scientists.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Urban and Regional Planners","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/life-physical-and-social-science/urban-and-regional-planners.htm"],["Indeed: Policy analyst salary","https://www.indeed.com/career/policy-analyst"],["FedManager: OPM reports 317,000 federal departures in 2025","https://www.fedmanager.com/news/opm-reports-317000-fed-departures-in-2025-dod-largest-drop-by-number"],["Chronicle of Philanthropy: How a year of funding whiplash reshaped the nonprofit work force","https://www.philanthropy.com/news/how-a-year-of-funding-whiplash-reshaped-the-nonprofit-work-force/"],["NCSL: When using AI in the legislature, keep humans in the loop","https://www.ncsl.org/resources/details/When-Using-AI-in-the-Legislature-Keep-Humans-in-the-Loop"],["George Mason University: The potential role of AI in legislative research and drafting","https://business.gmu.edu/news/2026-03/potential-role-ai-legislative-research-and-drafting"],["Harvard Kennedy School: Class of 2025 employment snapshot","https://www.hks.harvard.edu/more/employers/about-our-graduates/employment-snapshot/class-2025-employment-snapshot"]]},
 "fire":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS May 2025 medians: firefighters $59,280 (10th percentile under $34,910, 90th over $101,040); EMTs $44,470 (10th under $34,130, 90th over $62,500); paramedics $60,600 (10th under $44,230, 90th over $84,850); police and detectives $77,310 (10th $48,160, 90th $121,600). Overtime is common and can add a lot to firefighter pay; one city spent over $6.5 million on fire overtime in 2025. Big-city and West Coast departments pay well above rural ones, and private ambulance EMTs are usually at the low end.",
  growth:"BLS projects firefighters to grow 4% from 2025 to 2035 with about 26,800 openings a year, EMTs and paramedics 6% with about 18,200 openings a year, and police 3% with about 60,100 openings a year. Hiring news agrees there is demand: volunteer departments are shrinking fast (Connecticut lost nearly two-thirds of its volunteers since 2017), and many career departments face rising call volume without more staff. Paid jobs in large city departments are still competitive, with written, physical, and interview rounds.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is showing up around the edges: camera networks that spot wildfire smoke, drones with heat sensors that find hot spots, and tools that help dispatch and planning. Reporting from 2026 shows humans still confirm alerts and make decisions about sending crews and evacuations. The core work, entering burning buildings, treating patients, driving apparatus, and calming people in crisis, stays human. Paperwork and reports may get faster with AI, but the job itself is not at real risk."},
  sentiment:"People in the job often call it the best job in the world for the teamwork, purpose, and schedule with days off in a row. The biggest complaints are mandatory overtime, rising medical call volume, short staffing, and the mental toll of trauma. A 2026 Anchorage report described exhausted crews, injuries, and about 20% of firefighters considering other jobs. Health risks weigh on many: the WHO cancer agency classified firefighting as cancer-causing in 2022. Private ambulance EMTs often complain about low pay and burnout.",
  path:"Most start by earning EMT certification, often a one-semester course, then apply to departments that test for firefighter jobs. Many get experience through volunteer departments, private ambulance work, or wildland seasonal crews. After being hired you attend a fire academy for a few months, then serve a probationary year. People switching from unrelated jobs are common, though the physical test and age requirements matter. Later steps include paramedic school (usually after EMT experience), engineer or driver, lieutenant, captain, battalion chief, and chief, or roles in fire inspection and investigation.",
  notes:"You must be at least 18, have a driver's license, and pass medical, drug, and fitness tests; many departments use a standard physical ability test. All states require EMTs and paramedics to be licensed, and some areas require a firefighter endorsement or commercial license. Shifts are often 24 hours on and 48 or 72 off. Most career firefighters work for local governments and many are unionized through the IAFF. Long-term risks include cancer, injuries, and PTSD. Wildland jobs can be seasonal with long stretches in camps.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Firefighters","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/protective-service/firefighters.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: EMTs and Paramedics","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/emts-and-paramedics.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Police and Detectives","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/protective-service/police-and-detectives.htm"],["Anchorage Daily News: Anchorage firefighters grapple with burnout and injuries as call volumes climb (2026)","https://www.adn.com/alaska-news/anchorage/2026/06/13/anchorage-firefighters-grapple-with-burnout-and-injuries-as-call-volumes-climb/"],["Connecticut Public: Over half of CT fire departments are volunteer; a shortage is sparking a fire protection audit (2026)","https://www.ctpublic.org/news/2026-09-24/over-half-of-cts-fire-departments-are-volunteer-a-shortage-is-sparking-a-fire-protection-audit"],["AIHA: IARC says firefighters' occupational exposures cause cancer (2022)","https://www.aiha.org/news/220825-iarc-firefighters-occupational-exposures-cause-cancer"],["BetaNews: AI wildfire detection and drones are changing how the West fights fire (2026)","https://betanews.com/2026/05/04/ai-wildfire-detection/"]]},
 "hosp":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median pay for food service managers is $69,390 a year (May 2025), with the lowest 10% under $45,960 and the top 10% over $107,640. Lodging (hotel) managers are similar at a $69,250 median, but the top 10% earn over $128,880. Restaurant general manager pay on PayScale (July 2026) centers near $60k base, with most between $43k and $80k, and bonuses or profit sharing of a few thousand up to about $19k. Nearly half of PayScale respondents report no health benefits, so check the benefits package.",
  growth:"BLS projects food service manager jobs to grow 6% from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), with about 38,800 openings a year. Lodging managers grow 4%, with about 5,500 openings a year in a small field. Industry news is more cautious: restaurant payrolls rose only 181,000 in 2025, and the National Restaurant Association expects modest 1.3% real sales growth in 2026, with over 90% of operators calling labor and food costs a big challenge.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI is already common in back-office work: a 2026 Toast survey found nearly 9 in 10 small restaurant operators use some AI, mostly for inventory and food ordering, forecasting, scheduling, marketing, and menu tweaks. Voice ordering and kiosks are spreading. What stays human is the core of the job: running a busy shift, hiring and coaching staff, handling upset guests, fixing problems on the spot, and keeping food safe. Operators in that survey mostly want people overseeing AI work. Expect fewer hours spent on spreadsheets, not fewer managers."},
  sentiment:"People in the job often like the fast pace, the team feel, and that you can move up quickly without a degree. The main complaints are long and unpredictable hours, nights, weekends and holidays, being called in on short notice, thin staffing, and dealing with unhappy customers. Glassdoor-based research in 2024 gave the restaurant industry the highest burnout score of the industries it compared, with stress, long hours and poor management as top complaints. Rising costs in 2025 and 2026 are squeezing margins, which can mean pressure to do more with fewer staff.",
  path:"Most managers start as servers, cooks, front desk agents, or shift leads and move up within 2 to 5 years; BLS says under 5 years of related experience is typical. Chains and hotel groups run management-trainee programs that hire people from other fields, especially with retail or supervisory experience. A hospitality or business degree helps for upscale restaurants and full-service hotels but is not required. Common steps are shift lead, assistant manager, general manager, then multi-unit, district, or owner roles. Moving up usually means proving you can manage labor and food costs and develop assistant managers.",
  notes:"Many states and cities require a food safety manager certification (the optional Food Protection Manager Certification requires passing an exam); check your local health department. Expect to be on your feet most of the day, and many managers work over 40 hours. Hotel managers may be on call around the clock. Pay and hours vary a lot: school, office, and hospital cafeterias keep more regular hours, while fine dining and resorts pay more. Before taking a job, ask about salaried overtime expectations, bonus rules, and staffing levels.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Food Service Managers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/food-service-managers.htm"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Lodging Managers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/lodging-managers.htm"],["PayScale: General Manager, Restaurant salary (2026)","https://www.payscale.com/research/US/Job=General_Manager%2c_Restaurant/Salary"],["Nation's Restaurant News: Restaurant industry faces modest growth amid cost pressures in 2026","https://www.nrn.com/restaurant-insights/restaurant-industry-faces-modest-growth-amid-cost-pressures-in-2026"],["Toast (vendor blog): Restaurant AI statistics, 2026 survey","https://pos.toasttab.com/blog/data/restaurant-ai-statistics"],["Nation's Restaurant News: The restaurant industry has the highest degree of employee burnout (2024)","https://www.nrn.com/casual-dining/the-restaurant-industry-has-the-highest-degree-of-employee-burnout-study-shows"]]},
 "b311120":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median pay is $35,800 a year, or $17.21 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $27,040 and the top 10% over $45,040. Real yearly income is often lower because hours are short: PHI found about 46% of home care workers work only part-time, and median annual earnings were about $22,400 (2024 data). Pay depends heavily on state Medicaid rates. In 2026, base rates ranged from $13 an hour in Texas to about $19.65 in the New York City area, with California at $16.90 and Illinois at $18.75.",
  growth:"BLS projects 18% growth from 2025 to 2035, much faster than average, with about 760,500 openings a year, one of the largest numbers of any job. PHI projects over 6.1 million total openings from 2024 to 2034, mostly from people leaving the job. Demand news agrees: all 48 states that answered a KFF survey reported home care worker shortages in 2024. The risk is funding, since Medicaid pays for most home care and federal Medicaid cuts starting in 2027 could limit pay and hours in some states.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"The hands-on work cannot be done by AI: helping people bathe, dress, eat, move, and take medicine on time, keeping them company, and noticing when something is wrong. A 2025 Microsoft study found care jobs like this among the least overlapping with what AI chatbots do. Technology mainly touches the edges: electronic visit check-in and notes, scheduling apps, and remote sensors or monitors that alert staff to falls or health changes. These may change how visits are planned, but they do not replace the aide in the home."},
  sentiment:"Many aides say the work is meaningful and they bond closely with clients and families. The common complaints are low pay, too few or unpredictable hours, little or no benefits, working alone, and physical strain from lifting. PHI reports almost 60% of home care workers get some public assistance and about 40% live in low-income households. Turnover is high and agencies say they cannot hire enough people, yet some states have raised pay in 2025 and 2026. Workers worry that coming Medicaid cuts could undo those gains.",
  path:"This is one of the fastest jobs to start: a high school diploma is typical, some jobs do not require one, and training is short and often paid by the agency. Aides at Medicare-certified home health or hospice agencies must complete formal training and pass a test; the long-standing federal minimum is 75 hours with 16 clinical hours, and some states require more. People come from retail, food service, or caring for a family member. Common next steps are CNA, medication aide, then LPN or RN school, or moving into scheduling or care coordination at an agency.",
  notes:"Rules vary a lot by state: some require a license or certificate, a background check, and a competency exam, plus CPR or first aid. You will lift and move people, so back injuries are a real risk; ask for training in safe lifting. You may care for clients with dementia or mental health conditions who can act out, and you may be exposed to infections. Schedules vary and can include evenings and weekends.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Home Health and Personal Care Aides","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/home-health-aides-and-personal-care-aides.htm"],["Stateline via News From The States: Some states raise pay for home caregivers as shortage looms (Aug 2026)","https://www.newsfromthestates.com/node/424353"],["Home Health Care News: Home care industry to face 6.1M job openings by 2034 (PHI report, 2025)","https://homehealthcarenews.com/2025/09/home-care-industry-to-face-6-1m-job-openings-by-2034-as-low-wages-fuel-turnover/"],["Healthcare Dive: Medicaid cuts could worsen home care worker shortages, KFF (2025)","https://www.healthcaredive.com/news/medicaid-cuts-worsen-home-care-worker-shortages-kff/740539/"],["PHI: State home health aide training requirements (2011)","https://www.phinational.org/news/press-release-states-home-health-aide-training-requirements-lag-far-behind-nurse-aide-requirements-despite-trend-toward-home-and-community-based-care/"],["Fortune: Jobs least likely to be affected by AI, Microsoft study (2025)","https://fortune.com/2025/07/31/jobs-careers-unlikely-to-be-impacted-by-ai-microsoft"]]},
 "b499071":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median pay is $49,590 a year, or $23.84 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $35,350 and the top 10% over $77,180. Specialty skills such as HVAC or electrical work generally help you reach the higher end, though this varies by employer. Overtime and on-call pay can add a meaningful amount. Job postings vary widely by employer and region, so compare the full package.",
  growth:"BLS projects 4% growth from 2025 to 2035, about average, with about 148,700 openings a year, mostly from retirements and people changing jobs. That is a large, steady number of openings across almost every industry. Industry reporting in 2026 describes an aging maintenance workforce, with over two-thirds of facility technicians over 45 according to IFMA figures, and roles taking 45 to 60 days to fill, which points to steady demand for people willing to learn.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI and software are changing the paperwork side: work-order apps, sensors that flag equipment before it fails, and tools that triage tenant requests or suggest troubleshooting steps. Some apartment operators report fewer after-hours calls from smarter triage. But the actual repairs, like fixing a leak, replacing a motor, patching drywall, or getting a boiler running at 2 a.m., need a person on site with hands-on skill across many trades. Expect to use more apps and data, and to be valued for diagnosing problems AI cannot see."},
  sentiment:"People like the variety, solving a new problem every day, steady demand, and that you can learn on the job without a degree. Common complaints are on-call duty that means you are never fully off, being stretched thin across too many units or machines, aging buildings, and in apartments having to act as customer service rep and vendor coordinator too. A 2026 industry session on apartment maintenance described real burnout from on-call loads and pressure to adopt new apps.",
  path:"Most people start as a helper, porter, or junior maintenance tech and learn by assisting experienced workers; a high school diploma is typical and training takes months to a year or more. People often come from construction, retail, the military, or hobby repair work. A vocational program in HVAC, electrical, or industrial maintenance can shorten the learning curve and raise starting pay. Next steps include lead tech, maintenance supervisor or facilities manager, or specializing and getting licensed as an HVAC tech, electrician, or plumber.",
  notes:"The job is physical: climbing ladders, crawling into tight spaces, lifting, and working in heat or cold. BLS says it has one of the highest injury rates of any job, mostly sprains, cuts, and fractures. Complex electrical or plumbing work may need a state or local license; CareerOneStop lists state rules. Expect evening, weekend, and holiday on-call shifts for emergencies.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: General Maintenance and Repair Workers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/general-maintenance-and-repair-workers.htm"],["Coast (vendor blog): What 2026's skilled labor shortage means for maintenance teams","https://coastapp.com/blog/skilled-labor-shortage-maintenance/"],["HappyCo (vendor blog): What's really behind technician burnout (2026)","https://happy.co/resources/whats-behind-technician-burnout"],["Fortune: Jobs least likely to be affected by AI, Microsoft study (2025)","https://fortune.com/2025/07/31/jobs-careers-unlikely-to-be-impacted-by-ai-microsoft"]]},
 "b311131":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median pay for nursing assistants is $42,260 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $33,940 and the top 10% over $51,980. About 36% work in nursing homes and 32% in hospitals; pay differs by setting and region, and night or weekend shifts may carry extra pay depending on the employer. In a large 2025 national survey of CNAs, low pay was one of the top complaints, and better wages and benefits were the top thing that would keep them in their jobs.",
  growth:"BLS projects 3% growth from 2025 to 2035 for nursing assistants and orderlies, about average, but with about 203,300 openings a year because so many people leave or move on. Nursing homes and hospitals continue to report staffing shortages, and an aging population keeps demand steady. Recent news focuses more on keeping CNAs than on job cuts, though Medicaid funding pressure on nursing homes is a risk to watch.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"A 2025 Microsoft study of real AI use ranked nursing assistants as the second-least AI-applicable job it measured. The core work is physical and personal: bathing, feeding, turning and moving patients, taking vital signs, and noticing changes in a patient. AI is entering around the edges: fall-detection sensors, virtual sitters who watch several residents remotely, wearable monitors that flag fevers, and tools that find missing documentation. These can reduce charting and alert you sooner, but a person still has to answer the call light."},
  sentiment:"Many CNAs say the job is rewarding and that residents and patients become like family. The biggest complaints are short staffing (ranked the top worry two years running in a national CNA survey of nearly 3,000 aides), lack of respect from supervisors, burnout and exhaustion, low pay, and too little ongoing training. Turnover is high, especially in nursing homes. Still, in that survey 92% said they would stay CNAs if they got more education, raises, and recognition. Many use the job as a stepping stone into nursing.",
  path:"You complete a state-approved nurse aide program, usually a few weeks to a few months (the federal minimum is 75 hours, including 16 clinical), offered by community colleges, vocational schools, high schools, hospitals, and nursing homes. Some nursing homes pay for training if you work for them. Then you pass a state competency exam and are placed on the state registry. People switch in from retail, food service, and caregiving. Common next steps are medication aide, then LPN or RN programs; many nursing schools value CNA experience.",
  notes:"BLS says nursing assistants have one of the highest injury and illness rates of any job, mostly from lifting and moving patients, so safe-lifting training matters. Facilities run around the clock, so expect nights, weekends, and holidays, especially when new. State rules vary: most require the exam and registry listing, some require background checks and continuing education. Ask about staffing ratios, how many residents you will be assigned per shift, and whether lift equipment is available.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Nursing Assistants and Orderlies","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/nursing-assistants.htm"],["McKnight's Long-Term Care News: Major nurse aide survey reveals top worries, ways to improve","https://www.mcknights.com/news/major-nurse-aide-survey-reveals-top-worries-ways-to-improve/"],["Nurse.org: Nursing assistants, phlebotomists top AI-proof jobs (Microsoft study, 2025)","https://nurse.org/news/ai-proof-jobs-nursing-assistants-phlebotomists"],["Skilled Nursing News: Fall detection, audits: how AI helps Trilogy, Signature improve nursing home operations (2026)","https://skillednursingnews.com/2026/10/fall-detection-audits-how-ai-helps-trilogy-signature-improve-nursing-home-operations/"],["PHI: State home health aide and nurse aide training requirements (2011)","https://www.phinational.org/news/press-release-states-home-health-aide-training-requirements-lag-far-behind-nurse-aide-requirements-despite-trend-toward-home-and-community-based-care/"]]},
 "b472061":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for construction laborers is $47,120 a year (May 2025). For laborers and helpers combined, the median is $46,680, the lowest 10% earn under $34,820 and the top 10% over $76,400 (May 2025). Helpers in specific trades earn a bit less, from about $40,470 (painter and plasterer helpers) to $47,550 (mason and tile helpers). Pay rises with overtime, union scale in big cities, and skills like rigging or operating small equipment; slow winters in the North can cut yearly income.",
  growth:"BLS projects 7% growth for construction laborers from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), adding about 109,300 jobs; laborers and helpers together expect about 137,000 openings a year, mostly replacing people who leave. Hiring news agrees: in the 2026 AGC and NCCER survey, 87% of contractors had hourly craft openings and 88% said they were as hard or harder to fill than a year earlier. Immigration enforcement touched 29% of firms, most in the South.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"Generative AI barely touches this job: a 2025 Microsoft study found physical construction work among the least exposed to AI. The bigger change is jobsite robots: a 2026 industry survey found most contractors now use some robotics, mainly for accuracy, safety and reducing manual effort. Digging, carrying, setting forms, cleanup and helping tradespeople in messy, changing sites stay human for the foreseeable future, though some repetitive tasks may shift to machines over time."},
  sentiment:"Workers commonly say the job is a fast way to earn decent pay without school and a real door into the trades, and the current labor shortage gives them leverage. The main complaints are the physical toll, weather, irregular or seasonal hours, and layoffs between projects. Safety is a real concern: BLS lists construction laborers among the highest injury rates of any job. Crews in the South have also been shaken by immigration enforcement visits and workers leaving.",
  path:"Most people start with no experience: apply to a contractor, staffing agency or union hall and learn on the job by shadowing experienced workers. A high school diploma helps, and many employers want OSHA safety training and a driver's license. Apprenticeships sponsored by local unions or trade associations add structured training and higher pay. After a few years, people move into equipment operation, a skilled trade apprenticeship, foreman or supervisor jobs, or start their own small business.",
  notes:"Hard physical work outdoors in all weather, sometimes at heights or in trenches and confined spaces; highway jobs may run overnight. Expect OSHA 10 or 30 cards, and extra certs like rigging or scaffolding help you take on more complex work. Union jobs pay more and include benefits but depend on the local work supply. Work is seasonal in cold states. Watch for employers who skimp on safety gear or training, a sign to move on.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Construction Laborers and Helpers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/construction-and-extraction/construction-laborers-and-helpers.htm"],["BLS OEWS May 2025: Construction Laborers","https://www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes472061.htm"],["Roofing Contractor: Immigration enforcement affects 29% of contractors, AGC-NCCER survey finds (Sept 2026)","https://www.roofingcontractor.com/articles/102656-immigration-enforcement-affects-29-of-contractors-agc-nccer-survey-finds"],["Building Design + Construction: Adoption of jobsite robotics doubles in 2026, BuiltWorlds report","https://www.bdcnetwork.com/aec-tech/article/55395870/adoption-of-jobsite-robotics-doubles-in-2026-builtworlds-report"],["The Register: Microsoft researchers list jobs least exposed to AI (July 2025)","https://www.theregister.com/2025/07/29/microsoft_boffins_jobs_impacted_ai/"]]},
 "b319092":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $45,690 a year, or $21.97 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $36,050 and the top 10% over $59,310. The range is narrow, so experience alone does not raise pay much; certification, phlebotomy or EKG skills, specialty clinics, hospitals and high-cost metro areas tend to pay more. Low pay is the top complaint: a 2026 New York study found some full-time MAs still qualified for food stamps or Medicaid.",
  growth:"BLS projects 13% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), from about 833,900 to 941,500 jobs, with about 109,700 openings a year. Demand comes from an aging population and more care moving to clinics and outpatient centers, where 56% of MAs work in physician offices. Openings are plentiful, but many come from turnover and people leaving the field rather than new positions.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI is taking over much of the office side: scheduling and reminder calls, insurance eligibility checks, intake forms, prior authorization paperwork and drafting visit notes through ambient scribes. Health tech companies predict AI will handle most routine calls in 2026, though those forecasts come from vendors. The clinical side stays human: rooming patients, vitals, blood draws, injections, EKGs, calming nervous patients and translating. MAs focused mainly on front-desk work face more change than clinical MAs."},
  sentiment:"MAs often say they like patient contact, teamwork with providers and the steady demand for their skills. The loudest complaint, on forums and in research, is pay that feels low for the training and responsibility, with some saying restaurant or retail work pays similar for less stress. Understaffing, juggling front and back office work, precepting new hires and limited room to advance also come up often. Feeling valued by providers makes a big difference in how people rate the job.",
  path:"The usual route is a 1 to 2 year certificate or associate program at a community or technical college, including a clinical externship, then a certification exam such as CMA or RMA. Some clinics hire people with only a high school diploma and train them in several months. Next steps include lead MA or clinic supervisor, though advancement inside a practice is often limited, so many use it as a stepping stone to further health care training.",
  notes:"Rules vary by state: some require an accredited program or certification for tasks like injections, and some states (for example New York) limit giving vaccines. Many employers require certification and a Basic Life Support card even where the state does not. Mostly full time on weekdays; urgent care and hospitals add evenings and weekends. You stand and walk much of the day and handle blood and bodily fluids. Compare total pay and benefits closely; some practices exclude MAs from team bonuses.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Medical Assistants","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/medical-assistants.htm"],["GW Fitzhugh Mullan Institute for NY Health Foundation: Medical Assistant Focus Group Findings (2026)","https://gwhwi.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/MA-Focus-Group-Findings-Report.pdf"],["Annals of Family Medicine: Medical assistant burnout and job satisfaction study","https://www.annfammed.org/content/21/Supplement_1/4013"],["Fast Company: Inside the 2026 AI-enabled clinic (written by a health IT company executive, vendor view)","https://www.fastcompany.com/91465869/inside-the-2026-ai-enabled-clinic"],["Fishbowl: Why are medical assistants so undervalued (worker discussion)","https://www.fishbowlapp.com/post/why-are-medical-assistants-so-woefully-undervalued-i-would-make-more-at-a-restaurants-for-fewer-hours-and-significantly-less-stress-i"]]},
 "b471011":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS OEWS median is $79,920 a year, or $38.42 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn about $53,280 and the top 10% about $128,260. Pay depends heavily on trade and project size: foremen on big commercial, industrial or data center jobs, and union general foremen, earn well above the median, and a 2026 Fortune report cites union electrical foremen with overtime approaching $200,000. Residential crew leads tend to sit near the lower end.",
  growth:"O*NET, using BLS 2025 to 2035 projections, shows 5% to 6% growth (faster than average) and about 67,000 openings a year from roughly 913,000 jobs. There is no separate BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook page for this job. Hiring news agrees demand is strong: AGC's 2026 survey found most contractors struggling to fill craft jobs, and labor shortages delayed projects for 42% of firms, which makes experienced crew leaders valuable.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI is starting to handle the paperwork side: drafting daily logs and reports, RFIs, schedule updates and risk flags, safety checklists and toolbox talks, and photo-based progress tracking in software like Procore (much of the reporting on this comes from vendors). The core job stays human: leading crews on site, solving problems on the spot, coordinating trades, enforcing safety and making judgment calls. Supervisors who use these tools well may run larger jobs with less office time."},
  sentiment:"Supervisors often say they like the pay bump, responsibility and seeing a project come together, and many describe it as the natural reward for years in a trade. Common complaints are long hours, being squeezed between management deadlines and crew safety, constant firefighting and the stress of being responsible for people. Industry research in 2025 found many supervisors feel underprepared and lack training for leadership, safety and mental health issues on their crews.",
  path:"Almost everyone comes up through a trade: work as a laborer or apprentice, become a journeyworker, then a lead hand or foreman after about 5 or more years, per BLS. A high school diploma is typical; a construction management certificate, OSHA 30, first aid and reading blueprints and schedules help. Strong communicators get promoted faster. Next steps are general foreman, superintendent, construction manager or estimator, or starting your own contracting business.",
  notes:"Mostly on site, often outdoors, with early starts and long weeks during busy phases; some roles travel to remote or out-of-town projects, especially in oil, gas and mining. Some trades and states may require the foreman to hold a journeyman or master license. Union foremen are paid above scale but can be laid off between jobs. The job carries legal and safety responsibility for crews, so weak safety culture at an employer is a serious red flag.",
  sources:[["O*NET OnLine: First-Line Supervisors of Construction Trades and Extraction Workers (47-1011)","https://www.onetonline.org/link/summary/47-1011.00"],["BLS OEWS May 2025: First-Line Supervisors of Construction Trades and Extraction Workers","https://www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes471011.htm"],["My Next Move: First-Line Supervisors of Construction Trades and Extraction Workers","https://www.mynextmove.org/profile/summary/47-1011.00"],["Roofing Contractor: AGC-NCCER 2026 workforce survey findings","https://www.roofingcontractor.com/articles/102656-immigration-enforcement-affects-29-of-contractors-agc-nccer-survey-finds"],["ENR: Procore releases new AI agents (Nov 2024, vendor announcement)","https://enr.com/articles/59869-procore-releases-new-ai-agents-platform-integrations-at-groundbreak"],["Vector Solutions (vendor research): Construction supervisors strained by safety, mental health and training gaps","https://www.vectorsolutions.com/resources/newsroom/construction-supervisors-strained-by-safety-mental-health-and-training-gaps-new-research-from-vector-solutions/"]]},
 "b472111":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $63,190 a year, or $30.38 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $42,640 and the top 10% over $108,510. Apprentices start low and get raises each year: a 2026 Fortune report describes a first-year apprentice at about $42,000, and IBEW Local 26 near Washington, D.C. starting apprentices around $26 an hour with journeymen near $59.50 an hour (over $120,000) plus benefits. Overtime on data center jobs and foreman roles push pay higher.",
  growth:"BLS projects 9% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than the 3% average), from about 821,000 to 896,900 jobs, with about 72,700 openings a year. BLS points to data centers, grid upgrades and solar and wind hookups. News strongly agrees: Microsoft's president called electrician shortages the top issue slowing its data center buildout. One caution: big data center jobs are temporary, and some reporting warns new apprentices may finish after the peak.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is likely to help mostly around the edges: estimating and bidding software, load calculations, code lookups, scheduling and diagnostic tools for troubleshooting. The hands-on work of pulling wire, bending conduit, installing panels and finding faults in real buildings stays human, and a 2025 Microsoft study ranked physical trades among the least exposed to generative AI. AI is actually raising demand for electricians, since data centers need huge amounts of electrical work."},
  sentiment:"Electricians generally speak well of the trade: solid pay without college debt, paid training, problem solving and strong job security right now. Common complaints are wear on the knees and back, long or early hours, commuting or traveling to big jobs, and layoffs between projects. Union versus non-union is a constant debate: union locals offer set raises, benefits and pensions but can leave members waiting on the out-of-work list, while open shop can mean faster entry and residential work.",
  path:"Most people enter through a 4 to 5 year paid apprenticeship with about 2,000 hours of on-the-job training a year plus classes, run by IBEW and NECA union programs or non-union groups like IEC. Applications have surged, so spots can be competitive. Trade school or military experience can shorten it. After passing a state journeyman exam you can specialize (industrial, controls, solar, low voltage), then move up to master electrician, foreman, estimator or run your own contracting business.",
  notes:"Most states require a license exam on the National Electrical Code and local codes, plus continuing education; rules vary a lot by state and city. Shock, burn and fall risks are real, and the work involves cramped spaces, heights, heat or cold and long periods kneeling. Overtime and evening or weekend work are common. Union strength varies by region, so check which path dominates locally. Be wary of jobs that keep you as an unregistered helper with no hours counting toward a license.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Electricians","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/construction-and-extraction/electricians.htm"],["Fortune: AI data centers face an electrician shortage (March 2026)","https://fortune.com/2026/03/02/ai-data-centers-electrician-shortage-gen-z-training-careers/"],["Quartz: Apprentice electricians and the data center timing gap (2026)","https://qz.com/apprentice-electrician-data-center-timing-gap-071026"],["Mike Holt Forum: Apprenticeship questions, union vs nonunion (2025 thread)","https://forums.mikeholt.com/threads/apprenticeship-questions-union-vs-nonunion.2586761/post-2990175"],["The Register: Microsoft researchers list jobs least exposed to AI (July 2025)","https://www.theregister.com/2025/07/29/microsoft_boffins_jobs_impacted_ai/"]]},
 "b472073":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for construction equipment operators is $59,600 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $41,550 and the top 10% over $100,660. Most are paid hourly, so overtime and the length of the building season make a big difference to yearly income. Union operators on prevailing-wage public jobs usually earn toward the top of the range, though we did not find a current national union scale to quote. Contractors keep raising pay: in AGC's 2025 survey 95% of firms raised base pay in the past year.",
  growth:"BLS projects 4% growth from 2025 to 2035 (about average), with about 42,500 openings a year in a field of about 534,100 jobs, mostly from retirements and people leaving. Hiring news is stronger than that average suggests: 77% of contractors that employ crane and heavy equipment operators told AGC in mid-2025 they are hard to find, and the data center and infrastructure build-out is adding demand. ABC estimates construction overall needs up to 349,000 extra workers in 2026.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"Machine control (GPS grading guidance) already does some of the precision work an operator used to judge by eye. Startups such as Bedrock Robotics now sell kits that let large excavators dig on their own, with humans supervising remotely and handling odd cases; this is early, mostly on big repetitive earthmoving jobs. Most work stays human: tight urban sites, changing ground and weather, working around crews, rigging, and machine upkeep. Expect more tech skills to be needed, not fewer operators soon."},
  sentiment:"Operators often say they like running big machines, working outdoors, and good pay without college debt, and many say joining the union (in Minnesota, IUOE Local 49) brought better benefits and steadier pay. Common complaints are seasonal layoffs in cold states, long days in peak season, travel to far-off jobsites, dirt and noise, and years of sitting and vibration that wear on the back. Newcomers often say the hardest part is getting seat time on the machines at first.",
  path:"Typical ways in are a 3 to 4 year paid apprenticeship (union or contractor run), starting as a laborer or oiler and learning on lighter machines, or a private operator school; school is faster but costs money and does not guarantee a job. A high school diploma or GED is usually needed, and career changers from trucking, farming, or landscaping often move in easily. After 3 to 5 years you can run several machine types, then move to foreman, superintendent, crane operator, trainer, or your own excavating business.",
  notes:"A commercial driver's license (CDL) is often needed to haul equipment, and some states license certain machines; some states and cities require a crane license for certain work. Work happens in nearly all weather, can stop in winter in cold states, and may include night shifts. Pile driver operators have one of the highest injury rates of any job. Union apprenticeships run through local joint union-contractor committees. Be wary of pricey operator schools that promise jobs; ask for placement rates first.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Construction Equipment Operators","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/construction-and-extraction/construction-equipment-operators.htm"],["AGC and NCCER: 2025 Workforce Survey analysis","https://www.agc.org/sites/default/files/users/user21902/2025%20Workforce%20Survey%20Analysis_V2.pdf"],["DCD: Construction worker shortage hits US data center build-out (May 2026)","https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/analysis/construction-worker-shortage-us-data-center/"],["Sacra: Bedrock Robotics company profile (investor research)","https://sacra.com/c/bedrock-robotics"],["IUOE Local 49: member story on moving from non-union to union","https://local49.org/?p=7138"]]},
 "b292052":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $45,750 a year, or $22.00 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $36,020 and the top 10% over $61,040. Hospital technicians, especially those who prepare sterile IV or chemotherapy drugs, usually earn more and get better benefits than retail technicians. Becker's reports a 2025 mean technician wage of about $46,600. Pay is the most common reason technicians give for leaving.",
  growth:"BLS projects 6% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), with about 44,700 openings a year in a field of about 471,200 jobs, many from high turnover. The picture is split: hospitals are short of technicians (in a 2025 ASHP survey, 88% reported shortages of experienced techs and 92% of sterile compounding techs), while retail is shrinking, with Rite Aid closing all its stores in 2025 and Walgreens and CVS closing hundreds more.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"Machines already count and bottle many prescriptions, and big chains fill a growing share at central fill sites with robots. Hospitals use automated cabinets, carousels, and IV robots, and software is taking over insurance and prior authorization paperwork; some systems are piloting AI chat for routine patient questions. Still human: handling patients at the counter, sorting out insurance problems, sterile compounding, inventory judgment, and catching errors. Hospital leaders call these tools complements, but routine retail filling jobs are most exposed."},
  sentiment:"Many technicians say they like helping patients and that the job is a fast way into healthcare. Retail techs often describe heavy understaffing, nonstop phones and drive-thru lines, angry customers, and pay close to retail cashier rates, which fed the 2023 pharmacy walkouts. Hospital techs tend to be more satisfied, citing better pay, structure, and less public contact. A common view is to use retail for a year or two of experience, then move to a hospital.",
  path:"You can start with a high school diploma by training on the job at a retail pharmacy (usually under a year) or take a pharmacy technician certificate program (often a year or less; some are associate degrees). Most people then earn national certification (such as PTCB's CPhT). Next steps include hospital, sterile compounding or chemo tech, lead tech, pharmacy buyer, informatics, or using the job as a stepping stone to pharmacy school or nursing.",
  notes:"Most states regulate technicians through registration or licensing with the state board of pharmacy, often with a background check, minimum age, and continuing education; some require national certification. Rules vary a lot by state, so check your board. Work is mostly standing, and nights and weekends are common. Retail roles may now include giving vaccines in some states. Red flag: chronic understaffing in retail stores; ask about tech-to-pharmacist staffing levels before accepting.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Pharmacy Technicians","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/pharmacy-technicians.htm"],["Becker's Hospital Review: 5 numbers defining the pharmacy staffing crisis (June 2026)","https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/pharmacy/5-numbers-defining-the-pharmacy-staffing-crisis/"],["Becker's Hospital Review: Pharmacy staffing gaps fuel automation surge (March 2026)","https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/pharmacy/pharmacy-staffing-gaps-fuel-automation-surge/"],["American Prospect: Pharmacy workers walk out over understaffing (Nov 2023)","https://prospect.org/labor/11-03-2023-pharmageddon-pharmacist-walkout-strike/"],["Pharmacy Times: Shortages persist in an evolving profession (2023)","https://www.pharmacytimes.com/view/shortages-persist-in-an-evolving-profession"],["SAN: Rite Aid closes all stores nationwide after second bankruptcy (Oct 2025)","https://san.com/cc/rite-aid-closes-all-stores-nationwide-after-second-bankruptcy/"],["CareerVillage: How does retail pharmacy tech compare to hospital pharmacy tech","https://www.careervillage.org/questions/1232155/how-does-retail-pharmacy-tech-compare-to-hospital-pharmacy-tech"]]},
 "b472152":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $63,800 a year, or $30.67 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $44,150 and the top 10% over $108,420. Apprentices start at a fraction of journey pay and get raises as they progress. Licensed master plumbers who run their own business, union pipefitters on big industrial jobs, and those working lots of overtime can earn well above the median; we did not find a reliable current national figure for business owners.",
  growth:"BLS projects 7% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), with about 42,000 openings a year in a field of about 510,600 jobs. Hiring news agrees: contractors widely report trouble finding licensed plumbers, about one in five plumbers is over 55, and data center, factory, and housing work all need pipe trades. Industry estimates of a shortage of hundreds of thousands of plumbers by 2027 come from manufacturers and are uncertain.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI mostly helps the office side: answering calls, scheduling and dispatch, quoting, and estimating. Design software and robots can help with layout and measuring on large jobs. The core work stays human: diagnosing leaks and clogs in old buildings, cutting, fitting, and joining pipe in tight spaces, meeting local codes, and explaining options to customers. Industry leaders often point to plumbing as one of the jobs AI cannot do, and Gen Z interest in the trades is rising partly for that reason."},
  sentiment:"Plumbers often say they like solving real problems, good pay without college debt, steady demand, and the option to start their own business. Common complaints are dirty and cramped work, on-call nights and weekends, wear on knees and back over the years, and low pay early in an apprenticeship. Forums are full of questions about how to get into a union apprenticeship and whether to go self-employed. Many young people now see the trade as safe from AI.",
  path:"Most people do a 4 to 5 year paid apprenticeship, about 2,000 hours of on-the-job work plus classroom time each year, through a union local, contractor group, or employer; a high school diploma is usually required, and trade school is optional. Career changers are common, though apprentice pay starts low. After the apprenticeship and a state exam you become a journeyworker; later steps are master plumber, foreman, estimator, inspector, or owning a shop.",
  notes:"Most states and many cities require a plumbing license, usually after 2 to 5 years of experience plus an exam; some also license pipefitters and gas work. Expect heavy lifting, ladders, tight spaces, outdoor work, and cuts, burns, and falls as common injuries. Union and contractor apprenticeships pay you while you train, and spots can be competitive. Pipefitters and steamfitters mostly work in factories, plants, and big buildings, while plumbers often do homes and service calls.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Plumbers, Pipefitters, and Steamfitters","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/construction-and-extraction/plumbers-pipefitters-and-steamfitters.htm"],["Axios: Gen Z is building an AI-resistant college backup plan (Aug 2026)","https://axios.com/2026/08/15/gen-z-skilled-trades-ai-job-displacement"],["simPRO (vendor blog): Plumbing industry statistics for 2026","https://www.simprogroup.com/blog/plumbing-industry-statistics-2026"],["GummySearch: r/plumbers community summary","https://gummysearch.com/r/plumbers"],["AGC and NCCER: 2025 Workforce Survey analysis","https://www.agc.org/sites/default/files/users/user21902/2025%20Workforce%20Survey%20Analysis_V2.pdf"]]},
 "b499041":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for industrial machinery mechanics is $64,520 a year (May 2025). BLS gives percentiles only for the wider group of mechanics, maintenance workers, and millwrights: the lowest 10% earn under $45,760 and the top 10% over $94,530. Overtime is common, so yearly pay often runs above base. Pay tends to be higher in refineries, chemical plants, and automated warehouses than in small shops; we did not find a reliable current posting survey to quote.",
  growth:"BLS projects 18% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), about 79,700 new jobs on top of 446,900; openings for the wider group are about 51,900 a year. The reason is automation: more robots and conveyors mean more machines to fix. Recent news is mixed: US factory employment fell for much of 2023 to 2025, but manufacturers still report trouble finding skilled workers, with Deloitte and the Manufacturing Institute warning up to 1.9 million jobs could go unfilled by 2033.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"Sensors and AI-based predictive maintenance software increasingly flag failing bearings, motors, and pumps before they break, and AI tools can search manuals and suggest likely causes. That changes what mechanics do, with more data, PLC, and electronics work, but it does not replace the hands-on part: taking apart, repairing, aligning, and rebuilding machines, and judging what is safe to run. Adoption is still early; one maintenance vendor survey found predictive maintenance use rising only from 9% to 16%."},
  sentiment:"Mechanics often say they like the problem solving, variety, and steady demand, and many like the pride of keeping a plant running. Common complaints are forced overtime, on-call rotations, night and weekend shifts, long stretches of 60 or more hours during shutdowns, and dirty, loud, sometimes hot conditions. Some feel pressure from management when lines go down. Shift choice matters a lot: some plants offer four 10-hour days, others run rotating 12s.",
  path:"Common ways in are starting as a maintenance helper, machine operator, or general maintenance worker and learning on the job (usually over a year), or earning a 2-year associate degree in industrial maintenance covering hydraulics, pneumatics, welding, and electrical. Apprenticeships of 3 to 4 years exist, especially for millwrights. Military mechanics and auto or diesel techs move over easily. Later steps are lead or senior tech, PLC or controls tech, reliability tech, planner, or maintenance supervisor.",
  notes:"No license is usually required, though BLS lists no required license or certification; employer training on specific equipment and controls is common. Work involves lifting, climbing, tight spaces, and noise, and dangerous machinery means strict lockout and safety rules. Apprenticeships may be sponsored by employers or local unions. Jobs are tied to manufacturing and can be hit by plant layoffs or closures, so ask about the plant's stability and how overtime and on-call are handled.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Industrial Machinery Mechanics, Machinery Maintenance Workers, and Millwrights","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/industrial-machinery-mechanics-and-maintenance-workers-and-millwrights.htm"],["The Manufacturing Institute and Deloitte: Manufacturers need as many as 3.8 million new employees by 2033 (2024)","https://themanufacturinginstitute.org/manufacturers-need-as-many-as-3-8-million-new-employees-by-2033/"],["AI Magazine: Fluke (vendor) study on skills gaps and maintenance tech adoption (May 2026)","https://aimagazine.com/news/fluke-skills-shortages-cause-78-of-manufacturing-obstacles"],["Alliance for American Manufacturing: Factories lose 8k jobs in December (Jan 2026)","https://www.americanmanufacturing.org/press-release/factories-lose-8k-jobs-in-december-as-manufacturing-slump-persists/"],["Garage Journal forum: What's your work day? (maintenance techs on shifts and overtime)","https://www.garagejournal.com/forum/threads/whats-your-work-day.226917/post-3554469"]]},
 "b493023":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $50,620 a year ($24.34 an hour, May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $34,660 and the top 10% over $81,790. Many shops pay flat rate, meaning you earn the book hours for a job no matter how long it takes, so fast experienced techs can earn well above the median while new techs often earn less. A 2025 Ohio news report quoted a trade school saying well-trained, certified techs can reach six figures, but that is the top of the range, not typical.",
  growth:"BLS projects 5% growth from 2025 to 2035, faster than average, with about 66,200 openings a year (most from people retiring or leaving). Hiring news agrees there is a shortage: J.D. Power counted about 56,000 unfilled dealership tech jobs, NADA says about twice as many techs retire each year as graduate, and Ford's CEO has said the company is short about 5,000 technicians. EV and driver-assist (ADAS) skills are especially scarce.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is showing up as a helper: connected scan tools that suggest likely causes and next steps, remote experts guiding calibrations, and software that checks that no repair step was skipped. It may let less experienced staff handle simple diagnostics. The hands-on core stays human: taking things apart, testing, repairing, and road-testing real cars in awkward positions. Industry writers (some tied to diagnostic vendors) frame AI as a fix for the shortage rather than a replacement."},
  sentiment:"Techs often like the problem-solving and the steady demand, and good ones are hard to replace. Common complaints are low starting pay, flat-rate pay that punishes slow or warranty-heavy jobs, having to buy thousands of dollars of your own tools, constant retraining, and wear on the body. Some techs say extra EV training has not paid off for them. Many shops are short-staffed, which means busy days but also pressure.",
  path:"Most people take a 6-month to 2-year automotive technology program at a high school, community college, technical school, or a manufacturer or dealer program; others start as a lube tech or helper with only a high school diploma and learn on the job. Career changers can enter this way at any age. ASE certifications follow after 1 to 3 years of experience. Later steps include master tech, diagnostic or EV specialist, shop foreman, service advisor or manager, or opening your own shop.",
  notes:"The EPA requires refrigerant-handling certification for air-conditioning work. ASE certification (pass a test plus 1 to 3 years of work experience, renewed every 5 years) is widely preferred and can raise pay. The job has one of the highest injury rates of any occupation: heavy lifting, standing all day, fumes, and chemicals. Budget for tools; some employers help with tools or tuition. About a third work at dealers, a quarter at repair shops, and 15% are self-employed.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Automotive Service Technicians and Mechanics","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/automotive-service-technicians-and-mechanics.htm"],["Ideastream Public Media: Ohio drivers face wait times as auto shops search for mechanics (Feb 2025)","https://www.ideastream.org/2025-02-12/ohio-drivers-face-wait-times-as-auto-shops-search-for-mechanics"],["MOTOR (author from a diagnostics vendor): AI and connected diagnostics will be essential in bridging technician skills gaps (May 2026)","https://www.motor.com/2026/05/ai-automotive-technician-shortage-solution/"],["ASE: Work experience requirements","https://ase.com/?p=2631"],["InsideEVs: Mechanics say they make less money with an EV certification","https://insideevs.com/news/774664/ev-mechanic-certification-less-money/"]]},
 "b472031":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $60,580 a year ($29.12 an hour, May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $40,410 and the top 10% over $99,910. Nonresidential (commercial) work pays a bit more, with a median of $63,780, than residential at $60,600. Union pay can be much higher in some regions: Washington State's union carpenter apprenticeship reports graduates earning a median of about $54 an hour, roughly $98,600 a year. Residential wage growth slowed to about 2% in early 2026, below inflation.",
  growth:"BLS projects 4% growth from 2025 to 2035, about average, with about 62,800 openings a year, mostly from retirements. Recent news is mixed: ABC says construction needs about 349,000 new workers in 2026, but construction hiring hit a record low rate in early 2026 and residential construction has lost jobs as high interest rates slowed housing. Data center and power projects are the hot spots.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"Software and robots help around the edges: layout robots print wall locations on floors, factories pre-build wall panels and precut lumber, and robots handle repetitive drilling, fastening, and moving materials. Prefab could reduce some framing work on repeated designs like tract homes. Most carpentry stays human because every site is different: measuring, cutting, fitting, fixing mistakes, finish work, and repairs in old buildings. Experts say humanoid robots doing site work are years away."},
  sentiment:"Carpenters often take pride in building something real and like that the work is not desk-bound. Common complaints are wear on the body (knees, back, shoulders), weather and seasonal slowdowns, layoffs between jobs, and pay that varies a lot between union and non-union work. In 2026 many in residential work are feeling the housing slowdown, while commercial and data center work is busier. Burnout and long hours to meet deadlines come up often.",
  path:"Most start with a high school diploma, either as a laborer or helper learning on the job or through a 3 to 4 year paid apprenticeship run by unions or contractor groups; Washington's union program is 4 years and 8,000 hours with no tuition. Career changers are common. After that come journey-level carpenter, specialties like finish, form, or cabinet work, then foreman, superintendent, estimator, or running your own contracting business (25% are self-employed).",
  notes:"All carpenters must complete the OSHA 10-hour safety course; a driver's license is often needed, and some states require a contractor license to work for yourself. The work is physical: lifting, kneeling, climbing, and risk of falls and cuts, so protective gear is standard. Union membership (the UBC) varies a lot by region and drives much of the pay gap. Work slows when housing or interest rates turn, and immigration enforcement and tariffs are affecting crews and costs in 2026.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Carpenters","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/construction-and-extraction/carpenters.htm"],["Construction Executive: Construction industry must attract 349,000 workers in 2026 (ABC, Jan 2026)","https://constructionexec.com/?p=62523"],["Metaintro (career blog): The construction hiring boom is now cooling (Jul 2026)","https://www.metaintro.com/blog/construction-hiring-boom-cooling-workers-2026"],["Metaintro (career blog): Construction robots enter US homebuilding (Sep 2026, citing CNBC)","https://www.metaintro.com/blog/construction-robots-homebuilding-skilled-trades-jobs"],["Washington Career Bridge: Carpenter apprenticeship (Washington State UBC JATC)","https://careerbridge.wa.gov/learn/program/2918"]]},
 "b292061":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $64,400 a year ($30.96 an hour, May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $49,740 and the top 10% over $83,440. Pay depends on setting: nursing and residential care facilities, the biggest employer, pay a median of $68,020, government $68,970, home health $63,580, hospitals $61,160, and doctors' offices $59,520. Night, weekend, and agency shifts often pay extra.",
  growth:"BLS projects 3% growth from 2025 to 2035, about average, with about 51,800 openings a year, mostly to replace people who leave or retire. Demand is strongest in nursing homes, senior living, and home health, where operators describe ongoing staffing shortages. An NCSBN study found about 40% of RNs and LPNs plan to leave or retire by 2029, which would keep openings high.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI is starting to draft charting: ambient tools listen at the bedside and fill in notes for the nurse to review, care-plan tools cut end-of-shift paperwork, and monitoring software flags patients who may be getting worse. Most of this so far is in hospitals with RNs. The core of LPN work stays human: giving medications, wound care, checking vital signs, noticing changes in a resident, and comforting patients and families. Surveys show many nurses still do not fully trust AI tools."},
  sentiment:"LPNs often value that they can start nursing in about a year and make a real difference for residents they get to know. The biggest complaints are workload, especially in nursing homes where one LPN may cover 25 to 45 residents and the med pass alone is rushed, plus short staffing, being asked to act beyond their scope, and lower pay and respect than RNs. Stress and burnout are the top reasons nurses give for planning to leave.",
  path:"Complete a state-approved practical nursing program, usually about 1 year, at a community college or technical school, with supervised clinical hours, then pass the NCLEX-PN exam (about 87% of first-time U.S.-educated takers passed in 2025). Many start as CNAs first. Career changers do well here. Later steps include charge nurse, wound care or IV certification, MDS coordinator, or an LPN-to-RN bridge program to become a registered nurse.",
  notes:"A state nursing license is required, and what LPNs may do (scope of practice) differs by state, so check your state board of nursing. LPNs work under an RN or doctor and cannot do some assessments independently. Expect 8 to 12 hour shifts, nights, weekends, and holidays, lots of standing and lifting patients, and exposure to illness. Nursing homes employ over a third of LPNs; hospitals hire fewer LPNs in many regions.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Licensed Practical and Licensed Vocational Nurses","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/licensed-practical-and-licensed-vocational-nurses.htm"],["Skilled Nursing News: The workforce linchpin, the nursing home LPN role (2022)","https://skillednursingnews.com/2022/08/the-workforce-linchpin-nursing-home-lpn-role-weaves-together-resident-knowledge-clinical-expertise/"],["Incredible Health nurse forum: I have 26 residents to one nurse, is this normal?","https://incrediblehealth.com/nurse-advice/questions/07a67507/i-have-26-residents-to-one-nurse-is-this-normal"],["WebProNews: AI steps in to ease nurses' load as hospitals race to cut burnout (May 2026)","https://www.webpronews.com/ai-steps-in-to-ease-nurses-load-as-hospitals-race-to-cut-burnout/"],["Labster (summary of NCSBN 2024 National Nursing Workforce Study)","https://community.labster.com/industry-news-learner/post/small-steps-big-concerns-ncsbn-s-2024-nursing-workforce-study-reveals-ukAPphITIjciSQF"],["Kaplan (test-prep vendor): NCLEX pass rates 2026","https://www.kaptest.com/study/nclex/nclex-pass-rates-what-you-need-to-know/"]]},
 "b491011":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $79,860 a year ($38.39 an hour, May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $49,600 and the top 10% over $126,790. Pay depends a lot on what you supervise: a small auto shop service manager earns very differently from a maintenance supervisor at a plant, utility, or airline. The highest-paying states by median are Alaska ($98,110), Washington, Connecticut, California, and New Jersey (all about $93,000 to $94,000).",
  growth:"BLS projections for 2025 to 2035 show average growth (about 3 to 4%) and about 48,900 openings a year, many from retirements. Employers agree they need people: a 2026 MaintainX survey (vendor) of 211 maintenance leaders found over 70% of teams were understaffed at times and 37% chronically, mainly because qualified candidates are hard to find, and most roles took 60 days or more to fill.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI and maintenance software are taking over parts of the desk work: sensor-based predictive maintenance that forecasts failures, automatic scheduling of routine maintenance, work order tracking, and reports. This changes the job toward reading data and planning. The human core stays: leading and training a crew, inspecting work, making safety calls, handling breakdowns, and dealing with customers and budgets. Most AI claims in this field come from software vendors, so treat them with caution."},
  sentiment:"Supervisors often like the mix of hands-on work and leadership and the better pay than the techs they lead. Common complaints are being squeezed between management and crew, chronic understaffing that means overtime and covering shifts yourself, after-hours calls when equipment fails, and pressure to cut downtime and costs. Losing experienced techs to retirement while new hires take months to get up to speed is a frequent worry.",
  path:"Almost everyone gets here by first working as a mechanic, technician, or installer, usually for a few years (BLS lists under 5 years of related experience), then becoming a lead and supervisor. A high school diploma is typical; a trade certificate, associate degree, or trade licenses help. Someone from an unrelated field usually needs to start as a technician first. Next steps are maintenance or service manager, superintendent, facilities or plant manager, or operations roles.",
  notes:"Requirements follow the trade you supervise: for example ASE for auto shops, FAA licenses in aviation, electrical or HVAC licenses in buildings. Expect physical work on the floor, safety duties, and on-call or overtime. Software skills matter more now, especially maintenance management systems (CMMS), Excel, and scheduling tools. This is not an entry-level job: you normally need years of hands-on experience first.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics: First-Line Supervisors of Mechanics, Installers, and Repairers","https://www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes491011.htm"],["O*NET OnLine: First-Line Supervisors of Mechanics, Installers, and Repairers","https://www.onetonline.org/link/summary/49-1011.00"],["Wagedex (BLS data compiler): First-line supervisors of mechanics, installers, and repairers","https://wagedex.com/jobs/first-line-supervisors-of-mechanics-installers-and-repairers"],["KRDO / Stacker (written by MaintainX, vendor): What 211 maintenance leaders said about skilled labor shortages (Sep 2026)","https://krdo.com/stacker-small-business/2026/09/08/what-211-maintenance-leaders-said-about-skilled-labor-shortages/"],["Techstrong.ai: The impact of IoT and AI on maintenance management (2024)","https://techstrong.ai/articles/the-impact-of-iot-and-ai-on-maintenance-management"]]},
 "b499021":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $61,010 a year ($29.33 an hour, May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $40,050 and the top 10% over $95,210. Most techs are paid hourly, so overtime in peak heating and cooling seasons adds a lot to yearly income. Some residential service companies pay partly by commission or flat rate on repairs and sales, which can raise or lower take-home pay; check how pay is figured before accepting an offer. Commercial, refrigeration, and union work usually pays toward the top of the range.",
  growth:"BLS projects 11% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), adding about 48,200 jobs, with about 40,600 openings a year in a field of about 440,900 jobs; many openings come from retirements. Hiring news agrees: contractors call the workforce their top problem, training enrollment has risen, and trade trainers expect data center cooling work to add demand. In 2025 tariffs, higher equipment prices, and the switch to new A2L refrigerants squeezed some contractors, and some federal efficiency tax credits ended after 2025.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI mostly helps around the edges: smart thermostats and remote monitoring flag problems early, diagnostic apps suggest likely faults, and software handles scheduling, after-hours calls, load calculations, and follow-up with customers. The core work stays human: crawling into attics and crawl spaces, brazing and wiring, handling refrigerant safely, and figuring out odd problems on site. A 2026 analysis reported in ACHR News rated HVAC techs low for AI exposure, while office and dispatch jobs at HVAC firms face much higher risk."},
  sentiment:"Techs often say they like the steady demand, solving a new problem at each call, decent pay without college debt, and the option to start their own business. Common complaints are long hours and little time off in summer heat waves and winter cold snaps, on-call nights, hot attics and cold rooftops, and wear on the body over the years. Many service techs dislike pressure to sell new systems or add-ons instead of just fixing things, and some say pay at residential shops lags the skill needed.",
  path:"Most people take a 6 month to 2 year HVAC program at a technical or community college, or start as a helper and learn on the job; some join a union or contractor apprenticeship that runs several years. Career changers from mechanical, electrical, maintenance, or military work often move in easily. After a few years you can work alone on service calls, then specialize in commercial, refrigeration, or controls, or move to lead tech, service manager, sales, or your own contracting business, which may require a contractor license.",
  notes:"The EPA requires refrigerant handling certification (Section 608) for nearly all techs, and some states and cities require licenses, so check locally. BLS says HVAC techs have one of the highest injury and illness rates of any job: burns, electrical shock, and strains from heavy equipment. A driver's license is usually needed. Work in cold states like Minnesota is busy in both seasons; in hot states summer is the peak. Watch for shops that pay mostly on commission or push heavy upselling.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Heating, Air Conditioning, and Refrigeration Mechanics and Installers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/heating-air-conditioning-and-refrigeration-mechanics-and-installers.htm"],["ACHR News: AI and HVAC, techs are safe but office roles face high risk (Mar 2026)","https://www.achrnews.com/articles/165979-ai-and-hvac-techs-are-safe-but-office-roles-face-high-risk"],["ACHR News: From A2L to tariffs, HVACR had a year (2025 review)","https://ebook.achrnews.com/hvac-year-in-review-2025/news-year-in-review/"],["Contractor+ (vendor blog): HVAC technician shortage collides with data center boom (Jul 2026)","https://contractorplus.app/blog/hvac-technician-shortage-collides-with-data-center-boom"],["ACHR News: Contractors share tips to avoid technician burnout as the heat waves hit (2019)","https://www.achrnews.com/articles/141560-contractors-share-tips-to-avoid-technician-burnout-as-the-heat-waves-hit"],["Ground News: HVAC service job demands 5 years in sales, and techs react (Jul 2026)","https://ground.news/article/hvac-service-job-demands-5-years-in-sales-and-techs-say-that-tells-you-everything"]]},
 "b319091":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $48,070 a year ($23.11 an hour, May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $37,130 and the top 10% over $62,250, so the pay range is narrow and the ceiling is low. Assistants licensed for expanded duties usually earn more. A 2026 Dental Assisting National Board (DANB) survey found wages have not kept up with living costs, and about 23% of assistants hold more than one job. Benefits vary a lot between small private offices and larger group practices.",
  growth:"BLS projects 7% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), with about 53,000 openings a year in a field of about 388,600 jobs, mostly from people leaving the job. Hiring news agrees there are plenty of openings: in the ADA Health Policy Institute's Q3 2025 survey, 69% of practices recruiting assistants said hiring was very or extremely hard, though that was down from 75% in 2024. Good job security comes partly from high turnover.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is already in many dental offices: tools like Pearl and Overjet flag possible cavities and bone loss on x-rays for the dentist, and software handles reminders, scheduling, insurance checks, claims, and some charting notes. That cuts front-desk and paperwork time, which matters since many assistants also do office work. The chairside work stays human: setting up and sterilizing, passing instruments, suction, taking x-rays and impressions, and calming nervous patients. Diagnosis stays with the dentist."},
  sentiment:"Many assistants like helping people, the fast pace, short training, and regular daytime hours with few nights. The big complaints are low pay, little room to move up, and feeling unappreciated. In DANB's 2026 survey only about half were satisfied (down from much higher a decade ago), 43% felt valued by their employer, one in three had thought about leaving, and about one in five had changed jobs in the past year. Certified assistants stayed with employers more often.",
  path:"Many people start with a 1 year certificate program at a community college or technical school; 2 year associate programs exist but are less common. In some states you can be trained on the job, while others require an accredited program and an exam for certain duties. It is a common first healthcare job and a way in for career changers. Next steps include expanded function assistant, office manager, treatment coordinator, dental assisting teacher, or going back to school for dental hygiene.",
  notes:"Rules differ widely by state. Most states require a credential to take x-rays, and expanded duties such as coronal polishing require licensure or certification, often with an exam and CPR. Minnesota has a Licensed Dental Assistant credential through its Board of Dentistry. DANB's national Certified Dental Assistant exam is widely recognized. About 91% work in dentists' offices. Expect gloves, masks, eyewear, infection-control work, and a lot of time bent over patients, which can strain the neck and back.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Dental Assistants","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/dental-assistants.htm"],["DANB: Dental assisting trends and insights from 2025","https://www.danb.org/news-blog/detail/blog/dental-assisting-trends-and-insights-2025"],["Becker's Dental Review: Dental assistant job satisfaction rates dip, 8 notes (Sep 2026, DANB survey)","https://www.beckersdental.com/staffing-issues/dental-assistant-job-satisfaction-rates-dip-8-notes/"],["DentistryIQ: How artificial intelligence is transforming the role of dental assistants (Sep 2025)","https://www.dentistryiq.com/dental-assisting/career-enhancements/article/55319429/how-artificial-intelligence-is-transforming-the-role-of-dental-assistants"]]},
 "b292010":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $62,930 a year ($30.26 an hour, May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $38,910 and the top 10% over $100,990. This group mixes two levels: technologists (also called medical laboratory scientists, usually with a bachelor's) generally earn more than technicians (usually with an associate's), but BLS no longer gives separate figures. Night and weekend shift pay can add to income. We did not find a reliable current salary survey to compare against BLS.",
  growth:"BLS projects 3% growth from 2025 to 2035 (about average), with about 20,800 openings a year in a field of about 343,000 jobs; BLS notes lab automation may slow demand. Even so, labs keep reporting shortages: ASCP's 2024 vacancy survey found vacancy rates down from 2022 but still above pre-pandemic levels, with retirements rising in most lab departments. A 2026 ASCLS article cites about 25% vacancy rates and says too few training spots is a big cause. A federal bill to fund more training was introduced in 2025.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"Much of the routine testing is already done by automated analyzers, and labs increasingly use robotic tracks, digital plate imaging in microbiology, and AI tools that sort normal results or flag unusual cells and cultures. That is why BLS expects automation to slow job growth. People still collect and handle specimens, run quality control, troubleshoot instruments, do complex identifications and blood bank work, decide which results are valid, and call critical values to doctors. ASCP found labs see AI adoption as a challenge but are hopeful about it."},
  sentiment:"People in the field often say they like science, problem solving, and knowing their results drive most medical decisions, without direct patient care. The most common complaints are understaffing, heavy workloads, burnout, and feeling invisible: many patients and even hospital staff do not know the job exists. A 2025 report cited studies finding most lab staff have burnout symptoms. Some say the pay is low for a bachelor's level science job compared with nursing or other healthcare roles.",
  path:"Technicians usually earn an associate's degree in clinical laboratory science or a certificate (including through the military). Technologists usually earn a bachelor's in medical laboratory science, or a biology or chemistry degree plus a 1 to 2 year accredited program, which is a common route for career changers with a science degree. Certification usually follows school. Technicians can move up to technologist with more school; technologists can specialize, become lead tech, supervisor, lab manager, or move to sales, quality, or public health labs.",
  notes:"Some states require a license, and some tie it to national certification (ASCP is the best known); many employers prefer certified staff, so check your state. About 36% work in hospitals and 19% in medical and diagnostic labs. Expect infectious specimens and chemicals with masks, gloves, and goggles, long periods standing, repetitive motion, and nights, weekends, or holidays since hospital labs run around the clock. New grads often start on evening or night shifts.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Clinical Laboratory Technologists and Technicians","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/clinical-laboratory-technologists-and-technicians.htm"],["ASCP: AI, retirements, and staffing gaps from the 2024 vacancy survey (Oct 2025)","https://www.ascp.org/news/news-details/2025/10/15/ai--retirements--and-staffing-gaps--what-every-pathologist-and-medical-laboratory-professional-should-know-from-the-2024-vacancy-survey"],["ASCLS Today: Invisible hands, essential work (Jun 2026)","https://ascls.org/invisible-hands-essential-work/"],["The Pathologist: The lab burnout crisis (Jul 2025)","https://thepathologist.com/issues/2025/articles/july/the-lab-burnout-crisis/summary/"],["CAP Today: Microbiology automation, finding the right mix (2014)","https://www.captodayonline.com/microbiology-automation-finding-the-right-mix/"]]},
 "b291171":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for nurse practitioners is $132,300 a year (May 2025). BLS wage data in this app put the lowest 10% near $101,000 and the top 10% near $174,000 (May 2025). Pay varies by specialty and setting: psychiatric NPs tend to earn more than primary care NPs, and job boards like Indeed showed psychiatric NP base pay around $145,000 in 2026. Hospital jobs with nights, call, or acute care often pay more than clinic jobs, and some jobs pay partly on patient volume.",
  growth:"BLS projects 41% growth from 2025 to 2035, one of the fastest of any job, from about 336,300 to 474,100 jobs, with about 32,200 openings a year across all advanced practice nurses (NPs are the large majority). A 2026 Health Affairs study projects NP supply growing about 11% a year through 2030, far faster than doctors. The catch: so many new NPs are graduating that new grads report hard job searches in popular cities, while rural areas and psychiatry still have strong need.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI scribes that listen to visits and draft notes are spreading fast and can cut evening charting, one of the biggest burnout causes. AI also helps with prior authorizations, inbox replies, coding, and flagging drug interactions or abnormal results. The core stays human and legally tied to the license: examining patients, deciding what is wrong, prescribing, doing procedures, and talking through hard choices. NPs must still check AI notes for errors. Expect AI to change daily workflow more than the number of jobs."},
  sentiment:"Many NPs like the autonomy, the pay jump over bedside nursing, more regular hours in clinics, and a wide choice of specialties. Common complaints are heavy patient loads and short visit times, charting after hours, and some physicians and patients questioning their training. A big worry in forums and industry blogs is a crowded market as online NP programs multiply: new grads without strong nursing experience can wait months for a first job in desirable cities, while experienced NPs and those willing to move find work faster.",
  path:"You must first be a registered nurse. Most people work as an RN for a few years, then complete a master's (about 2 to 3 years) or a Doctor of Nursing Practice in a chosen population such as family, adult-gerontology, acute care, pediatrics, or psychiatric mental health. Bridge programs exist for RNs with an associate's degree, and some programs admit people with a bachelor's in another field. Then pass a national certification exam and get a state license. Later steps include specialty roles, lead NP, clinic director, teaching, or your own practice where allowed.",
  notes:"Scope of practice differs by state: about half the states plus DC let NPs practice fully on their own, often after a set number of supervised hours (Minnesota requires 2,080), while others require physician collaboration or supervision. Most states require national certification. The schooling is long and can be costly, and quality varies, so check program accreditation and clinical placement help. Hospital roles can mean nights, weekends, and call; some jobs carry high patient quotas.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Nurse Anesthetists, Nurse Midwives, and Nurse Practitioners","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/nurse-anesthetists-nurse-midwives-and-nurse-practitioners.htm"],["Becker's Hospital Review: Nurse practitioner workforce growth to far outpace physicians and PAs (Jul 2026, Health Affairs study)","https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/workforce/nurse-practitioner-workforce-growth-to-far-outpace-physicians-physician-assistants-study/"],["ThriveAP (NP employer blog): Nurse practitioner market saturation, should you worry?","https://provider.thriveap.com/blog/nurse-practitioner-market-saturation-should-you-worry"],["Nurse.org: Is the PMHNP field oversaturated? 2026 job market","https://nurse.org/education/pmhnp-job-market"],["RegisteredNursing.org: Nurse practitioner scope of practice by state (Aug 2026)","https://www.registerednursing.org/articles/nurse-practitioner-scope-of-practice/"]]},
 "b291051":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $140,910 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $99,290 and the top 10% over $174,230. Pay varies by setting: hospitals $157,290, ambulatory care $153,920, general merchandise retailers $151,370, and pharmacies and drug stores $132,940 (May 2025 medians). A Drug Channels analysis of the same data found average pay rose 2.7% in 2025, barely ahead of inflation, and that pharmacists' pay edge over other health practitioners has shrunk from about 54% a decade ago to about 30%.",
  growth:"BLS projects 5% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), with about 12,500 openings a year in a field of roughly 325,000 jobs. The mix is shifting: retail pharmacies lost 8,200 pharmacist jobs in 2025 and are at their lowest level since 2010, while hospitals added about 3,000. Rite Aid has gone out of business and CVS and Walgreens have closed nearly 3,000 stores since 2018, so retail jobs are shrinking even as hospital and clinic roles grow.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"Automation and AI already handle much of the operational side: data entry, prescription processing, inventory, robotic dispensing, and flagging interactions. Pharmacy experts expect most operational steps to include AI within about five years, with clinical uses arriving more slowly. The pharmacist stays legally responsible for verification, judging whether a drug and dose fit the patient, counseling, vaccinations, and working with doctors on therapy. Studies of AI drug information tools are mixed, and some found they underperform pharmacists. The bigger near-term threat to retail jobs is store economics, not AI."},
  sentiment:"Mood splits sharply by setting. Retail pharmacists describe heavy prescription volume, thin staffing, few breaks, and angry customers when insurance denies a drug; in 2023 staff at CVS and Walgreens stores walked out over working conditions. Burnout is widely reported. Hospital and clinical pharmacists are generally happier and see more growth, but those jobs are competitive and often expect a residency. Many in the field worry about store closures and pay that has barely kept up with inflation.",
  path:"You need a Doctor of Pharmacy (PharmD), usually about 2 years of college prerequisites then a 4-year program (some schools offer 3-year or 6-year tracks). Career changers with a science background can enter after finishing prerequisites. After school you pass two licensing exams and complete state internship hours. Hospital and specialty roles often require a 1–2 year residency. Later steps include clinical specialist, pharmacy manager or director, or roles in industry, insurers, and managed care.",
  notes:"Every state requires a license (two exams plus internship hours) and continuing education; most states require certification to give vaccines. Retail and 24-hour hospital jobs include nights, weekends, and holidays, and the work involves a lot of standing. PharmD tuition is high, so weigh debt against a retail market that is shrinking. Pharmacy school applications fell for 11 years and graduates dropped 24% from 2018 to 2024, which may ease competition for jobs, but whether supply and demand balance is still uncertain.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Pharmacists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/pharmacists.htm"],["Drug Channels: Pharmacist salaries and employment in 2025, retail declines, hospital gains (June 2026)","https://www.drugchannels.net/2026/06/pharmacist-salaries-and-employment-in.html"],["UW–Madison School of Pharmacy: Dean's column, Spring 2026","https://pharmacy.wisc.edu/2026/04/08/deans-column-spring-2026/"],["Drug Topics: AI use in pharmacy practice evolution (Aug 2026)","https://www.drugtopics.com/view/ai-use-in-pharmacy-practice-evolution"],["Wisconsin Public Radio: Pharmacists seek help with increasing demands amid walkouts (Nov 2023)","https://wpr.org/wisconsin-pharmacists-help-increasing-demands-walkouts"]]},
 "b493031":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $61,770 a year, or $29.70 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $44,530 and the top 10% over $88,740. Local government shops pay the most (median $71,460), followed by wholesale trade ($63,070), auto repair ($60,330), rental and leasing ($60,220), and trucking companies ($59,820). Overtime is common and certifications can raise pay. In a 2026 technician survey, 82% said pay is the industry's biggest issue, though 67% called their own shop's pay fair.",
  growth:"BLS projects 4% growth from 2025 to 2035 (about average), with about 24,400 openings a year, mostly to replace retirees and people leaving the trade, in a field of about 316,000 jobs. Industry research agrees there is a shortage: the American Transportation Research Institute found 65.5% of diesel shops were understaffed in 2025, with about 19% of positions unfilled. Freight downturns can slow trucking hiring for a while, but skilled techs remain hard to find.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI and telematics now read fault codes and sensor data, rank likely causes, suggest repair steps, and predict failures before a truck breaks down, which speeds up diagnosis, especially for newer techs. Shop software also automates work orders and parts lookups. The hands-on core stays human: tearing down and rebuilding engines, brakes, and transmissions, working in tight spaces, judging worn parts, and roadside repairs. Trucks are getting more electronic, so expect more computer-based diagnostics rather than fewer jobs."},
  sentiment:"Techs like the steady demand, solving real mechanical puzzles, and the fact that you can start without a degree. Common complaints are pay that lags the skill required, buying expensive tools out of pocket, flat-rate pay systems (many prefer hourly or salary), and poor management communication. An industry study found 44% of techs were considering moving to other technical fields. The work is hard on the body, and many shops are short-staffed, which means overtime and pressure to get trucks back on the road.",
  path:"Many people start straight from high school or from farm, auto, or military mechanic work as a shop helper doing fluid checks and parts cleaning, then learn on the job over several years. A postsecondary diesel program at a community or trade college speeds things up and is preferred by many employers. Some dealers and fleets sponsor training. After 2–5 years you can earn certifications and move up to lead tech, shop foreman, service manager, field service tech, or fleet maintenance manager.",
  notes:"Industry certification is often preferred or required and can raise pay; it typically needs work experience plus an exam. A commercial driver's license may be needed to test-drive trucks and buses. Expect heavy lifting, awkward positions, noise, and common cuts and sprains; some shops run 24 hours. Tool costs are a real barrier: in ATRI research, 29% named tool costs as the top barrier to entering the field, so ask about tool allowances. Government and transit jobs are often unionized and pay more.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Diesel Service Technicians and Mechanics","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/diesel-service-technicians-and-mechanics.htm"],["Fleet Equipment: ATRI research on the diesel technician shortage in trucking","https://www.fleetequipmentmag.com/atri-diesel-technician-shortage-in-trucking/"],["Wrenchway: Diesel technician statistics 2026, job satisfaction and pay (recruiting platform)","https://info.wrenchway.com/blog/diesel-technician-statistics-2026-job-satisfaction-pay-industry-trends/"],["ShopView (vendor blog): AI-powered diesel diagnostics for heavy-duty repair","https://shopview.com/blog/ai-powered-diesel-diagnostics-for-heavy-duty-repair"],["FreightWaves: Diesel technician demand remains extreme (Apr 2023)","https://www.freightwaves.com/news/loaded-and-rolling-diesel-technician-demand-remains-extreme"]]},
 "b291123":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $102,760 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $77,140 and the top 10% over $135,140. Pay growth has been slow: APTA data reported by KFF Health News found wages in most regions kept pace with or fell behind inflation from 2016 to 2021, and Medicare payment cuts limit what clinics can pay. New graduates often start around $80,000.",
  growth:"BLS projects 12% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), with about 13,400 openings a year in a field of roughly 283,700 jobs, driven by an aging population and more people living with chronic conditions. Shortage reports agree: an APTA survey found an outpatient vacancy rate of 17%, patients in many areas wait weeks for appointments, and rural and high-cost areas are hardest to staff. Schools graduate about 12,000 PTs a year and cannot expand quickly.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is mostly helping with paperwork: ambient scribes can listen to a visit and draft the note for the therapist to review, and software helps with scheduling, billing, home exercise programs, and remote monitoring. Time savings are proven in physician settings but not yet studied in PT clinics, and the tools still make mistakes with PT terms. The core stays human: hands-on assessment, deciding on a diagnosis and plan, guiding and correcting exercises, manual therapy, and motivating patients over weeks of recovery."},
  sentiment:"PTs love helping people regain movement and seeing progress, and many enjoy the variety of settings. The big complaints are productivity quotas (seeing more patients per hour, sometimes overlapping), unpaid after-hours charting, insurance paperwork, and pay that has not kept up with high student debt. Corporate and private-equity ownership of clinics is often blamed for pressure. Burnout is common, and more than 22,000 PTs left their jobs in 2021. Many shift to home health, travel, or part-time work for better balance.",
  path:"Earn a bachelor's degree with prerequisites (anatomy, physiology, chemistry, physics), then a 3-year Doctor of Physical Therapy program, including clinical rotations. Career changers can enter after finishing prerequisites; many programs want observation hours. Pass the national licensing exam to practice. Optional residencies (about 1 year) and board certification lead to specialist roles in orthopedics, sports, neuro, pediatrics, or geriatrics. Later steps include clinic director, owning a practice, or teaching.",
  notes:"Every state requires a license, which means passing a national exam; some also require a law exam or background check, plus continuing education. The work is physical: lots of standing, lifting and moving patients, with real risk of back injury. Debt is a red flag: an APTA report found recent grads averaged about $142,000 in education debt, and some programs cost over $200,000 in tuition. Medicare payment rates fell for several years in a row and a further conversion-factor cut was proposed for 2027, which squeezes clinic budgets.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Physical Therapists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/physical-therapists.htm"],["KFF Health News via WUSF: Back pain? Be prepared to wait for a physical therapist (Dec 2023)","https://www.wusf.org/health-news-florida/2023-12-03/back-pain-bum-knee-be-prepared-to-wait-for-a-physical-therapist"],["Physitrack (vendor blog): Why physical therapists are burning out, the reimbursement squeeze (Aug 2026)","https://www.physitrack.com/insights/physical-therapy-reimbursement-burnout"],["Physitrack (vendor blog): Does AI documentation actually fix PT burnout? (June 2026)","https://www.physitrack.com/insights/ai-documentation-pt-burnout"],["Illinois Physical Therapy Association: Federal affairs update, September 2026","https://www.ipta.org/news/734517/Updates-from-Your-Federal-Affairs-Liaison-September-2026.htm"]]},
 "b292034":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for radiologic technologists and technicians is $80,110 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $55,980 and the top 10% over $118,660. Techs who add CT, MRI, or interventional skills usually earn more, and night, weekend, and on-call shifts often pay differentials. Travel contracts are a popular way to earn more, though pay varies widely by region and contract; exact current travel rates were not verified.",
  growth:"BLS projects 5% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average), about 11,800 new jobs, in a field of about 234,200 jobs; radiologic and MRI technologists together have about 15,300 openings a year. Hiring news agrees: a 2025 ASRT survey of imaging managers found vacancy rates of 15.6% for radiography, a record 19.4% for CT, and 17.4% for MRI, so cross-trained techs are in especially high demand.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI is built into newer scanners: camera-based auto-positioning, suggested scan length and protocols, dose reduction, and deep-learning image reconstruction that cleans up images. A 2025 study found these tools cut CT setup time and dose, but techs still had to check positioning and monitor results. AI also flags urgent findings for radiologists. What stays human is the patient: explaining the exam, positioning people who are hurt, scared, or immobile, radiation safety, handling contrast reactions, and judging when an image is not good enough."},
  sentiment:"Rad techs like the short training path, solid pay for a 2-year degree, and steady demand. The common complaints are understaffing, heavy workloads in busy hospitals, rotating nights and weekends, and physical strain from moving patients. Burnout has pushed many toward travel jobs: in a 2023 AMN Healthcare survey of allied health travelers, about 70% said workplace fatigue drove their decision to travel. Some feel undervalued compared with other clinical roles.",
  path:"Most people complete a 2-year associate degree in an accredited radiography program, with lots of clinical hours. Then you pass the national ARRT certification exam and get a state license where required. Career changers often enter through community college programs. After a year or two, many add CT, MRI, mammography, or interventional work through on-the-job training and extra exams, then move into lead tech, imaging manager, educator, or sales and applications roles.",
  notes:"Most states require a license or certification, and employers usually want ARRT certification even where the state does not; CPR or basic life support may also be required. About 60% work in hospitals, so expect evenings, nights, weekends, or on-call shifts. The job means long periods standing and lifting or turning patients. Radiation exposure is limited with shielding and monitoring badges. Admission to accredited programs can be competitive, so check program waitlists and ARRT pass rates.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Radiologic and MRI Technologists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/radiologic-technologists.htm"],["ASRT: Staffing and workplace survey shows vacancy rates near record highs (July 2025)","https://www.asrt.org/news/article/2025/07/24/asrt-staffing-and-workplace-survey-shows-vacancy-rate-increases-near-record-highs-aligning-with-overall-health-care-profession-trends"],["AuntMinnie Europe: CT radiographers can play key role in AI implementation (EuroSafe 2025 study)","https://www.auntminnieeurope.com/15754438"],["Radiology Business: Burnout propelling radiologic technologists to seek travel assignments (May 2023)","https://radiologybusiness.com/topics/healthcare-management/healthcare-staffing/burnout-radiologic-technologists-travel-assignments"],["AMN Healthcare (staffing firm blog): AI in radiology, will it replace techs?","https://www.amnhealthcare.com/blog/allied/travel/ai-in-radiology-will-it-replace-techs-or-make-you-a-super-tech/"]]},
 "b472141":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $49,400 a year, about $23.75 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $37,440 and the top 10% over $78,810. Apprentices earn less than fully trained painters. Pay is usually hourly, so overtime and the length of the outdoor season matter. IUPAT says its union commercial painters average about 12% above the national average, though it gives no exact figures. Bridge, tank, and industrial coatings work generally pays more than house painting, but we did not find current national figures to quote.",
  growth:"BLS projects 3% growth from 2025 to 2035 (about average), with about 24,900 openings a year in a field of about 326,900 jobs, mostly from people retiring or leaving. Contractors say construction labor is still short: in AGC's 2025 survey 88% of firms had craft openings and 95% raised base pay, though painters were not broken out. Immigration enforcement hit some crews in 2025, and painting is often squeezed at the end of projects when earlier trades run late.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"Software already helps with estimating, bids, and color previews, and spray robots exist: Canvas finishes drywall beside crews, PaintJet paints exteriors from lifts, and Okibo and others spray interior walls. These are mostly early, used on large new buildings with open, repetitive walls, cost a lot up front, and still need people to set up, refill, and supervise. Prep work, masking, trim, repairs in occupied homes, color matching, and dealing with customers stay human. Expect more machines on big jobs, not fewer painters soon."},
  sentiment:"Painters often say they like seeing a clear result each day, the low cost of getting in, and the option to start their own business. Common complaints are low starting pay in residential work, being rushed at the end of jobs, ladder and fall risks, fumes and dust, and wear on the shoulders, knees, and back. Union commercial and industrial painters tend to report better pay and benefits than non-union house painters. We found little recent forum data, so treat this as a general picture.",
  path:"Most people learn on the job as a helper, often with no prior experience; many come from general labor, retail, or other trades. A 3 to 4 year paid apprenticeship (union, such as the Finishing Trades Institute in Minnesota, or through ABC contractors) teaches commercial and industrial work and usually needs a high school diploma or GED and age 18. After a few years you can move to lead painter, foreman, estimator, or specialty coatings, or run your own painting business.",
  notes:"Work on pre-1978 homes and child care sites that disturbs paint requires EPA lead-safe (RRP) certified renovators and firms; some states license lead removal separately. Bridge and tank work means heights, harnesses, and confined spaces with special training. The job is physical: bending, kneeling, climbing. About 31% are self-employed, and outdoor work can be seasonal in cold states like Minnesota. A driver's license is often required.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Painters, Construction and Maintenance","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/construction-and-extraction/painters-construction-and-maintenance.htm"],["Builder: Construction's dirty work is going robotic (Jul 2025)","https://www.builderonline.com/building/behind-the-gloss-constructions-dirty-work-is-going-robotic/"],["IUPAT: Commercial painter trade page (union site)","https://www.iupat.org/trades/commercial-painter/"],["Minnesota DLI: Painter apprenticeships","https://www.dli.mn.gov/node/7201"],["Florida Health: EPA Renovation, Repair and Painting rule","https://www.floridahealth.gov/community-environmental-public-health/environmental-public-health/lead/renovation-repair-and-painting-rrp/"],["AGC: 2025 Workforce Survey analysis","https://www.agc.org/sites/default/files/users/user21902/2025%20Workforce%20Survey%20Analysis_V2.pdf"]]},
 "b291292":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $98,100 a year, about $47.16 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $74,880 and the top 10% over $126,050. Pay is mostly hourly, and many hygienists work part time or for more than one office, so yearly income depends on days worked. Benefits vary a lot between offices. In a 2025 survey of about 2,000 hygienists by the staffing company GoTu, nearly half said they had not had a raise in over two years.",
  growth:"BLS projects 8% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), with about 15,000 openings a year in a field of about 225,300 jobs. Hiring news agrees that demand is strong: in an ADA survey of dentists in Q3 2025, hygienists were the hardest role to recruit, most dentists trying to hire called it extremely challenging, and about 57% of open hygienist jobs were still unfilled.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI already reads X-rays to flag decay and measure bone loss (FDA-cleared tools such as Overjet and Pearl), writes visit notes, and lets hygienists speak gum measurements into the chart by voice, though a 2026 Dentaltown review found voice charting still clunky and slow to adopt. The core of the job stays human: cleaning teeth by hand, probing gums, giving local anesthetic where allowed, and coaching nervous patients. Expect less typing, not fewer hygienists."},
  sentiment:"Hygienists often praise the pay for a two to three year degree, flexible and part-time schedules, and helping patients. The most common complaints are burnout (63% in the 2025 GoTu survey), tight appointment times, flat raises, and, commonly reported though not measured in that survey, strain on the neck, back, and hands. Many take temp or multi-office work for control over schedules, trading away steady benefits. Even so, 79% in that survey planned to stay at least another ten years.",
  path:"You need a degree from an accredited dental hygiene program, usually an associate's that takes about three years with prerequisites; bachelor's programs also exist. Career changers often start with science prerequisites at a community college, and some come from dental assisting. Then you pass written and clinical licensing exams and get a state license. Later options include a bachelor's or master's for teaching, public health, sales, or office management, or, with more schooling, dental therapy where states allow it.",
  notes:"Every state requires a license, with rules that vary; most need an accredited degree plus written and clinical exams (regional clinical exams are run by groups such as CDCA-WREB-CITA, CRDTS, and SRTA). Local anesthetic needs extra training in many states. CPR certification and continuing education are required. About 94% work in dentists' offices. Program seats are limited, so admission can be competitive. Repetitive hand work is the main physical risk.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Dental Hygienists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/dental-hygienists.htm"],["Becker's Dental Review: How dentists are faring against workforce shortages (Oct 2025, ADA HPI data)","https://www.beckersdental.com/staffing-issues/how-dentists-are-faring-against-workforce-shortages/"],["ADHA Hygienist Hub: What 2,000 RDHs revealed in a state of work report (GoTu, staffing vendor, May 2025)","https://www.adha.org/hygienist-hub/what-2000-rdhs-revealed-in-a-state-of-work-report/"],["Dentaltown: Why AI voice perio charting still feels hard in the hygiene chair (Jul 2026)","https://www.dentaltown.com/news/details/15001/why-ai-voice-perio-charting-still-feels-hard-in-the-hygiene-chair"],["Becker's Dental Review: Overjet receives FDA clearance for AI software","https://www.beckersdental.com/ai-teledentistry/overjet-receives-fda-clearance-for-ai-software"],["ADHA: Overview of clinical examinations (2024)","https://www.adha.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Overview_Clinical_Examinations_7-2024.pdf"]]},
 "b472051":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $57,020 a year (May 2025). BLS wage data put the lowest 10% near $39,000 and the top 10% near $85,000. Pay is hourly, so overtime in busy months and winter slowdowns in cold states change yearly income a lot. Union cement masons on large commercial and public jobs usually earn toward the top of the range with health and pension benefits, but we did not find a current national union scale to quote. Contractors keep raising pay: 95% raised base pay in AGC's 2025 survey.",
  growth:"BLS projects about 2% growth from 2025 to 2035 (slower than average), adding about 3,300 jobs to roughly 204,800, with about 13,600 openings a year mostly from people retiring or leaving. Hiring news is stronger than that suggests: in AGC's 2025 survey 88% of contractors had craft openings and a majority of firms employing each tracked craft said it was hard to fill.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"Software helps with estimating, scheduling, and checking slab flatness, and machines already do some leveling: laser screeds level large slabs with smaller crews, though the maker's labor-saving claims come from a vendor. 3D concrete printing is being tried for some walls and homes but is still rare. The hands-on core stays human: setting forms, reading how fresh concrete is curing in changing weather, troweling, edging, cutting joints, patching, and decorative work. Expect smaller crews on big flat floors, not the end of the trade."},
  sentiment:"Finishers often say they like working outdoors, seeing a finished slab at the end of the day, and decent pay without college debt, and union members cite good benefits. The biggest complaints are the toll on knees, back, and hands from kneeling and heavy work, very early starts and long days when a pour cannot wait, heat and cold, and layoffs in winter. Many say the work is hard to keep doing into your 50s, so planning a move to foreman or estimating matters. We found little recent forum data, so treat this as general.",
  path:"Most start as laborers or helpers on a concrete crew with no experience; people from landscaping, farming, or other trades move in easily. Others join a paid apprenticeship (length varies by program; uncertain, often about 3 years) through the cement masons union (OPCMIA) or contractor groups, which usually needs a high school diploma or GED. Experience can earn ACI Flatwork Finisher certification (1,500 hours plus a hands-on test). After several years you can become a lead finisher, foreman, superintendent, or start a flatwork or decorative concrete business.",
  notes:"No state license is usually needed for workers, but ACI flatwork certifications (good for five years) are sometimes required on commercial jobs. The work is heavy: lifting, kneeling, bending, and long hours standing, with risks from cuts, falls, and falling objects; dust and wet cement add skin and lung hazards (general safety guidance, not from BLS). Work is mostly outdoors and slows in bad weather and cold-state winters. Immigration enforcement disrupted some crews in 2025, mostly on building sites. Union strength varies a lot by region.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Masonry Workers (includes cement masons and concrete finishers)","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/construction-and-extraction/brickmasons-blockmasons-and-stonemasons.htm"],["BLS OEWS: Cement Masons and Concrete Finishers","https://Www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes472051.htm"],["ACI: Concrete flatwork associate, finisher, and advanced finisher certification","https://www.concrete.org/certification/certificationprograms/m/details/pgm/concrete%20flatwork%20finishing%26cert%3Dconcrete%20flatwork%20associate%20and%20advanced%20finisher"],["Somero (vendor page): The impact of laser screeds on the concrete industry","https://www.somero.com/?p=410"],["OPCMIA: Training and apprenticeship (union site)","https://opcmia.org/training/"],["AGC: 2025 Workforce Survey analysis","https://www.agc.org/sites/default/files/users/user21902/2025%20Workforce%20Survey%20Analysis_V2.pdf"]]},
 "b292072":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $51,140 a year (May 2025), about $24.59 an hour; the lowest 10% earn under $37,000 and the top 10% over $81,150. Certification makes a real difference in this field: a secondary summary of AAPC's 2025 salary survey puts credentialed CPC coders at an average of about $64,500 and says certified coders earn roughly 25 to 30% more than uncertified ones. That figure comes from a test-prep site, not from AAPC directly, so treat it as approximate.",
  growth:"BLS projects 8% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), from about 200,700 jobs, with about 14,000 openings a year, mostly to replace people who leave. Hiring news is mixed: hospitals report coders are still in short supply, but AI now handles a growing share of simple coding, and new coders say entry-level openings are scarce.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"AI already codes many simple, high-volume charts on its own. At UMass Memorial, AI handles about 86% of radiology coding, while complex MRI and multi-part procedures stay lower and go to people. Humans still review AI codes, handle rare or messy cases with several conditions, run audits, fix denials, and make sure codes meet payer and legal rules. Coders were moved to harder work rather than laid off there, but expect fewer pure data-entry and simple-coding jobs and more auditing, review, and specialty work."},
  sentiment:"People like that the work is often remote, quiet, and detail-focused, with no direct patient care. The biggest complaint in 2026 forum threads is breaking in: many newly certified coders with the apprentice (CPC-A) label report months of searching because most postings want two or more years of experience. Some feel the certificate alone was not worth it. Experienced coders worry about production quotas and AI, but most hospital reporting describes AI easing backlogs rather than cutting staff.",
  path:"Most people take a postsecondary certificate program (often under a year) or an associate degree, then sit for a credential such as CPC (AAPC) or CCA, CCS, or RHIT (AHIMA). Because new coders struggle to land a first coding job, a common route is to start in a nearby role such as front desk, medical records clerk, billing, or revenue cycle, then move into coding. AAPC lets you remove the apprentice label with verified experience or a practicum. Later steps include coding auditor, coding supervisor, clinical documentation specialist, or health information manager.",
  notes:"Certification is often required or strongly preferred, and many employers want it on hire or soon after. The work is sitting at a computer for long hours; hospitals may require nights or weekends. Remote work is common, though remote share figures online come from secondary sites and vary. Red flags: pricey online programs that promise quick remote jobs, since entry-level remote roles are hard to get without experience. Keeping a credential requires ongoing continuing education.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Medical Records Specialists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/medical-records-and-health-information-technicians.htm"],["HFMA: AI coding at UMass Memorial tempers staffing shortage (2025)","https://www.hfma.org/revenue-cycle/ai-coding-at-umass-memorial-tempers-staffing-shortage/"],["AAPC forum: CPC-A looking for entry level positions (2026)","https://www.aapc.com/discuss/threads/cpc-a-looking-for-entry-level-positions.205869/"],["HIA (coding services vendor blog): How using AI will affect the work of medical coders","https://hiacode.com/blog/how-using-ai-will-affect-the-work-of-medical-coders"],["Practice Test Geeks (test-prep site): CPC salary and career outlook, citing AAPC 2025 survey","https://practicetestgeeks.com/cpc/salary-outlook"]]},
 "b151253":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for software QA analysts and testers is $104,300 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $61,440 and the top 10% over $167,010. Pay rises with automation and coding skill: one job-listing tracker (an industry blog with unclear methods) says roles asking for AI tool skills pay roughly 15 to 25% more. Manual-only testing jobs tend to sit at the low end of the range.",
  growth:"BLS projects 6% growth from 2025 to 2035, from about 187,600 to 198,200 jobs. BLS gives about 106,100 openings a year only for developers, QA analysts, and testers combined; the occupation-level data used by this app shows about 10,700 a year for QA alone. Hiring news is cooler than the projection for beginners: employers want automation and API testing skills even for junior roles, and manual tester listings are shrinking while SDET and quality engineer roles grow.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium–High",text:"AI already drafts test cases from user stories, writes and cleans up bug reports, summarizes failed test runs, generates test data, and fixes broken test scripts automatically. A 2025 industry survey found about 90% of companies are pursuing generative AI in testing, though only 15% use it company-wide. What stays human: deciding what is risky and worth testing, exploratory and usability testing, test strategy, checking AI output for wrong assumptions, and explaining risk to the business. Manual, scripted testing is the most exposed."},
  sentiment:"Testers often like the problem-solving and being the person who catches what others miss, but many feel undervalued compared with developers and say QA is the first team cut or offshored. Job seekers since 2024 describe a tough market: fewer openings, more competition, and employers unwilling to train beginners. People who learned automation and coding report better luck and pay. Release crunches and late-night bug hunts can make the work stressful.",
  path:"BLS lists a bachelor's degree in computer science or a related field as typical, but many testers come from support, help desk, game testing, or other fields after a bootcamp or self-study. Learning a test automation tool (such as Selenium, Playwright, or Cypress), API testing with Postman, SQL, and a language like Python or Java is now close to required. Internships or a portfolio of test projects help. After 2 to 5 years, people move to SDET, QA lead, test architect, developer, or DevOps roles.",
  notes:"No license is required. Certifications such as ISTQB are optional; they help some resumes but matter less than automation skill. The work is desk-based and often remote or hybrid, with crunch time near releases. Game QA is a separate, lower-paid niche with frequent layoffs. Red flag: roles titled manual tester with no automation path, which are the most likely to be cut or replaced by AI tools.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Software Developers, Quality Assurance Analysts, and Testers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/software-developers.htm"],["Capgemini (consulting firm press release): World Quality Report 2025","https://www.capgemini.com/us-en/news/press-releases/world-quality-report-2025-ai-adoption-surges-in-quality-engineering-but-enterprise-level-scaling-remains-elusive/"],["SoftwareTestPilot (industry blog): How AI is changing QA in 2026","https://softwaretestpilot.com/blog/ai-in-testing/how-ai-is-changing-qa-2026"],["The Test Lead (Substack): Why you cannot find a QA or QE software testing job","https://thetestlead.substack.com/p/why-you-cant-find-a-qa-or-qe-software"]]},
 "b291127":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $97,870 a year (May 2025), about $47 an hour; the lowest 10% earn under $62,900 and the top 10% over $134,160. Pay varies by setting: school jobs follow teacher pay scales and include summers off, while hospitals, nursing homes, home health, and contract work often pay more per hour. ASHA publishes separate salary surveys by setting and state for comparison.",
  growth:"BLS projects 17% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), adding about 32,100 jobs to roughly 193,400, with about 12,500 openings a year. Hiring news agrees: in ASHA's 2024 schools survey, a shortage of SLPs was the top barrier to manageable caseloads, and a 2026 review cites ASHA saying openings can outnumber available SLPs by 60 to 90%.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is mostly helping with paperwork: drafting session notes, reports, and therapy goals, and making practice materials. Research is exploring AI speech analysis, screening, and language-practice companions, but ASHA writing describes these as early, without a strong evidence base yet. The core job stays human: hands-on assessment, swallowing evaluations, building trust with children and families, adjusting therapy in the moment, and clinical judgment. AI will likely cut documentation time more than it cuts jobs."},
  sentiment:"SLPs strongly value the purpose of the work and seeing clients improve, and job security is excellent. The main complaints are high caseloads, paperwork, limited control over schedules, and pay that feels low for a master's degree. School SLPs report a median caseload of 50 against a manageable 40, plus about 6 hours a week of documentation and pressure to make up missed sessions. In medical settings, billing and productivity quotas are a common source of burnout, and early-career clinicians are especially at risk.",
  path:"You need a bachelor's degree (any major works if you take prerequisite courses), then a roughly 2-year accredited master's in speech-language pathology with supervised clinical hours. After that comes a paid clinical fellowship of several months under a certified SLP and a national exam, which leads to ASHA certification and a state license. Career changers often do a one-year post-bacc of prerequisites first. Total time from a new start is usually 6 to 7 years. Later options include specializing (swallowing, voice, autism, AAC), supervising, private practice, or a doctorate.",
  notes:"Every state requires a license, and most employers want ASHA certification; schools may need a separate state teaching credential. Master's programs are competitive and costly. The work is mostly on-site in schools, clinics, hospitals, and nursing homes; telepractice exists but is a minority. Some settings, especially home health and nursing homes, involve travel between sites and strict productivity targets. Bilingual SLPs are in especially high demand. Part-time and per-diem work is common.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Speech-Language Pathologists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/speech-language-pathologists.htm"],["ASHA 2024 Schools Survey: SLP caseload and workload","https://asha.org/siteassets/surveys/2024-schools-survey-slp-caseload.pdf"],["Journal of Health and Wellness (2026): Burnout in speech-language pathology, a persistent challenge","https://wcujhw.scholasticahq.com/article/163868-burnout-in-speech-language-pathology-a-persistent-challenge-and-a-call-for-change"],["ASHA Leader: AI as a treatment companion for multilingual children","https://leader.pubs.asha.org/do/10.1044/leader.FTR2.28012023.ai-treatment-slp.50/full/"],["Bilinguistics (SLP practice blog): SLP burnout","https://bilinguistics.com/slp-burnout/"]]},
 "b151241":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $134,050 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $79,900 and the top 10% over $202,680. Job postings for the architect title often run higher: a 2026 recruiter salary guide puts network architects at about $150k to $218k, with senior network engineers at $125k to $168k. Specialists who blend networking with cloud, security, or AI infrastructure skills earn the most, per 2026 industry reporting.",
  growth:"BLS projects 8% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), from about 181,800 to 195,800 jobs, with about 9,600 openings a year. January 2026 reporting describes a split market: strong demand for architects who can design secure, cloud and AI-ready networks, but fewer openings for generalists and entry-level manual configuration work.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI and automation already handle much routine work: generating configs and reference designs, validating changes, drafting diagrams and compliance reports, and forecasting capacity. Intent-based and SD-WAN platforms may automate standard designs within a few years. What stays human is designing for real business constraints, picking vendors, owning the risk when a network fails, and translating between leadership and engineers. AI workloads also create new demand for complex network design, which helps this role."},
  sentiment:"People in networking like the pay and the problem solving, and senior architects are generally in demand. Common complaints are long hours, after-hours change windows and outages, and feeling under-supported; a 2024 IT salary survey found many infrastructure workers citing stress as a reason to look for a new job. Many worry that automation is shrinking the junior roles that used to feed into architecture, so the ladder is harder to climb than before.",
  path:"This is not an entry-level job. Most people start as a help desk tech, network or systems administrator, or network engineer, then spend about 5 or more years building experience before moving to architect. A bachelor's in computer science, IT, or engineering is typical, though some employers accept experience instead and some prefer a master's. Career changers usually start with networking certifications and a support or junior admin job. Later steps include IT manager, director, or chief technology officer.",
  notes:"Mostly office or remote work with occasional time in server rooms; most work full time and some work more than 40 hours, including nights or weekends for upgrades. Vendor or vendor-neutral certifications (for example Cisco, cloud, or security certs) are often required or preferred. Skills in automation (Python, infrastructure as code), cloud networking, and zero-trust security now matter as much as classic routing and switching.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Computer Network Architects","https://www.bls.gov/OOH/computer-and-information-technology/computer-network-architects.htm"],["TechTarget: Advanced skills drive networking job market in 2026","https://www.techtarget.com/it-infrastructure/feature/Advanced-skills-drive-networking-job-market-in-2026"],["KORE1 (recruiter): Network engineer salary guide 2026","https://www.kore1.com/network-engineer-salary-guide/"],["JobZoneRisk: Will AI replace computer network architect jobs?","https://jobzonerisk.com/roles/computer-network-architect"],["Gluware (vendor blog) on ITPro Today 2024 salary report: IT pros weigh salary, flexibility and mental health","https://gluware.com/it-pros-weigh-salary-flexibility-and-mental-health-as-burnout-looms/"]]},
 "b493042":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS reports a median of $65,510 a year (May 2025) for mobile heavy equipment mechanics. For the broader heavy vehicle and mobile equipment group, the lowest 10% earn under $45,460 and the top 10% over $93,450. Government jobs pay the most (median $70,350), followed by transportation and warehousing ($68,600), heavy construction ($63,770), and rental and leasing ($61,620). Overtime is common and can add a lot to yearly pay; manufacturer certifications usually raise pay.",
  growth:"BLS projects 7% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), from about 180,300 to 192,500 jobs, tied to construction and infrastructure spending. Industry news agrees and is stronger: equipment dealers report a worsening shortage, with a 2026 dealer association report saying about 10,000 technicians are needed each year in the U.S. and 80% of dealers cannot meet demand, and Caterpillar says its dealers may need over 38,000 technicians by 2028.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI and telematics are changing the computer side: machines report fault codes remotely, software helps read codes and plan preventive maintenance, and fleet systems can automate work orders, parts ordering, and records. The core job stays human: confirming faults on site, tracing wiring, and rebuilding hydraulic cylinders, pumps, transmissions, and undercarriages on machines in muddy, unpredictable job sites. More electronics and complex equipment actually make skilled diagnosis more valuable."},
  sentiment:"Industry reports describe steady work and plenty of job offers because of the technician shortage, and many mechanics take pride in keeping big machines running. The common downsides are physical: heavy lifting, grease and dirt, awkward positions, noisy shops, and weather in the field. Long hours, evenings, weekends, and overtime are normal, especially for field techs who drive long distances. Buying your own tools can cost thousands of dollars over a career.",
  path:"A high school diploma is the usual minimum, but many employers prefer a 1 to 2 year diesel or heavy equipment certificate or a 2-year associate degree. Entry-level workers usually train on the job for a few months and become fully qualified in 3 to 4 years; school graduates need less time. Dealer-sponsored programs and apprenticeships are common ways in, including from farm, auto, or military mechanic work. Later steps include field service tech, lead tech, shop foreman, service manager, or trainer.",
  notes:"Physically demanding: lifting heavy parts, working under and around large machines, and handling hydraulics under pressure. No state license is required, but manufacturer certifications are valued and usually raise pay. Expect to buy your own hand tools. Work is tied to construction and mining cycles, so slowdowns can cut overtime or hours. Jobs are spread nationwide, with field roles in rural, mining, and logging areas. The trade is getting more electronic, so comfort with laptops and diagnostic software matters.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Heavy Vehicle and Mobile Equipment Service Technicians","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/heavy-vehicle-and-mobile-equipment-service-technicians.htm"],["Associated Equipment Distributors: The equipment industry technician shortage (2026 report)","https://aednet.org/industry-insight-news/industry-reports/the-equipment-industry-technician-shortage/"],["HeavyQuip Magazine: Caterpillar puts technician and operator skills in focus at CONEXPO 2026","https://www.heavyquipmag.com/2026/03/18/caterpillar-puts-technician-and-operator-skills-in-focus-at-conexpo-2026/"],["Red Seal (Canada): Trends in the heavy duty equipment technician trade","https://red-seal.ca/eng/trades/heavydutyequiptech/trends.shtml"],["JobZoneRisk: Will AI replace mobile heavy equipment mechanic jobs?","https://jobzonerisk.com/roles/heavy-equipment-mechanic"]]},
 "b291122":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $100,330 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $71,690 and the top 10% over $131,950. Pay varies by setting: home health ($106,300) and nursing facilities ($105,850) pay the most, then hospitals ($102,820) and therapy clinics ($97,940), while schools pay least ($87,300). Part-time and per-diem work is common and is usually paid hourly.",
  growth:"BLS projects 15% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), from about 169,600 to 194,800 jobs, with about 10,000 openings a year, driven by an aging population and chronic conditions. Demand is real, but payment pressure tempers it: AOTA reported in 2026 that years of Medicare therapy cuts have pushed rates to levels it calls unsustainable, which squeezes clinics and can limit raises.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is starting to help with paperwork: drafting notes and reports, summarizing client data, scoring some standardized tests, sending reminders, and analyzing data from wearables or motion sensors. The core work stays human: watching how a person actually does daily tasks, hands-on treatment, adapting homes and routines, and building trust with clients and families. AOTA guidance says AI output should be treated as extra data, not the final answer, and raises privacy, bias, and accuracy concerns."},
  sentiment:"OTs generally find the work meaningful and like the variety of settings, from pediatrics in schools to hand therapy and rehab. Common complaints are heavy productivity targets and documentation, especially in skilled nursing and home health, pay that has not kept up with growing student debt, and Medicare reimbursement cuts. Some OTs leave the field or move into non-clinical roles. Mood varies a lot by setting, with outpatient and school roles often rated more balanced than high-productivity facility jobs.",
  path:"You need a degree from an accredited OT program: a master's (MOT, usually 2 to 3 years after a bachelor's) or an entry-level doctorate (OTD), which takes longer and costs more. Both lead to the same NBCOT exam and state license. Admission is selective and often requires prerequisite science courses, a solid GPA, and observation hours. Career changers can enter from any bachelor's. Later options include specialty certification (such as hand therapy), management, private practice, or teaching.",
  notes:"Every state requires a license, which means an accredited degree, supervised fieldwork, passing the national exam (OTR), and continuing education. The job is physical: long periods on your feet and lifting or moving clients, and OTs have one of the higher injury rates of all occupations. Schedules may include nights or weekends. The cheaper master's route remains fully valid since AOTA and ACOTE kept dual entry-level degrees; weigh tuition and debt carefully against pay.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Occupational Therapists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/occupational-therapists.htm"],["AOTA OT Practice: Momentum builds to end long-standing cuts to occupational therapy (Aug 2026)","https://www.aota.org/publications/ot-practice/ot-practice-issues/2026/momentum-builds-to-end-long-standing-cuts-to-occupational-therapy"],["AOTA: Artificial intelligence's role in occupational therapy, the innovation and instability","https://aota.org/publications/student-articles/career-advice/ai-innovation-instability"],["Messiah University (school blog): Do I need an MOT or OTD to become a licensed OT?","https://www.messiah.edu/news/2023/05/do-i-need-an-mot-or-otd-to-become-a-licensed-occup"],["University of Cincinnati (school blog): Occupational therapy job outlook 2025","https://www.online.uc.edu/blog/2025/07/n100637.html"]]},
 "b291071":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $135,880 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $99,380 and the top 10% over $190,280. By setting, government pays most ($157,040), then outpatient centers ($153,500), hospitals ($141,150), and physician offices ($132,000). The AAPA 2025 salary report found median total pay of $134,000 (up 5.5% from 2023), and $140,000 for hospital PAs; California, Hawaii, and Alaska pay the highest base salaries.",
  growth:"BLS projects 21% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), from about 168,900 to 204,600 jobs, with about 11,500 openings a year. Hiring news agrees: NCCPA reported in October 2025 that 12,400 PAs were newly certified in 2024 (up about 25% from 2020), yet new PAs found jobs in about 2.6 months on average and 78% got more than one offer.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI is moving fast into documentation: ambient AI scribes listen to visits and draft notes, and 2025 studies suggest they cut charting time and burnout, though they occasionally make clinically important errors that a clinician must catch. AI also helps with decision support, inbox messages, and reading test results. The core stays human and licensed: examining patients, diagnosing, doing procedures, prescribing, and talking through care plans. AI is more likely to change the workload than replace PAs."},
  sentiment:"Most PAs like the pay, the patient contact, and the flexibility to switch specialties without retraining. Burnout is real: an NCCPA-based study found about 34% of PAs reported burnout symptoms, highest in emergency medicine (about 42%) and lowest in dermatology (about 26%), and work-life balance was the strongest protection. Charting is another common burden, which is why AI scribes are spreading.",
  path:"Most students first earn a bachelor's with science prerequisites and gain hands-on patient care experience, often as a medical assistant, EMT, CNA, or scribe. Then comes an accredited PA master's program of at least 2 years after the bachelor's, the national certification exam (PA-C), and a state license. Admission is competitive. Career changers often need 1 to 3 years of prerequisites and patient care hours first. Later steps include specialty roles, lead PA, administration, or teaching.",
  notes:"Every state requires a license; PAs must keep up continuing education and pass a recertification exam on a set cycle to keep the PA-C credential. Hours can include nights, weekends, holidays, and on-call, especially in hospitals and emergency medicine. How independently PAs can practice depends on state law. About 90% of PAs work in metro areas, but rural and primary care jobs are often easier to land. Expect large student loan debt from the program.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Physician Assistants","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/physician-assistants.htm"],["AAPA: 2025 salary report shows rising compensation","https://www.aapa.org/news-central/2025/04/aapa-2025-salary-report-rising-compensation-confirms-pa-professions-vital-role-in-healthcare-system/"],["NCCPA: New report highlights growth and readiness of recently board certified PAs (Oct 2025)","https://www.nccpa.net/new-nccpa-report-highlights-growth-readiness-and-impact-of-recently-board-certified-pas"],["NCCPA: Work-life balance is greatest defense against PA job stress","https://www.nccpa.net/news/work-life-balance-is-greatest-defense-against-pa-job-stress-new-study-finds"],["HealthDay: AI-based ambient scribes may reduce physician documentation time and burnout (Dec 2025)","https://www.healthday.com/healthpro-news/general-health/ai-based-ambient-scribes-may-reduce-physician-documentation-time-burnout"]]},
 "b292053":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for psychiatric technicians is $45,130 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $35,090 and the top 10% over $60,750. Psychiatric aides, a closely related job with less training, earn almost the same median ($44,910). Pay is usually hourly, and many people add to it with night, weekend, and overtime shifts, since units run around the clock. A 2025 survey of hospital technicians found 71% felt underpaid, though it covered all tech types, not only psychiatric ones.",
  growth:"BLS projects 22% growth for psychiatric technicians from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), from about 161,500 to 197,500 jobs. Technicians and aides together have about 21,900 openings a year, mostly to replace people who leave. News agrees demand is real: several states have added psychiatric beds or bought hospitals and then struggled to staff them, and Kansas lawmakers have cited a shortage of mental health technicians.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI can help with paperwork: drafting shift notes, logging vital signs, tracking supplies, and scheduling. Psychiatrists already use AI scribes for notes, but those tools still make mistakes and a person must check them. The core of this job stays human: watching patients on the unit, talking someone down from a crisis, deciding when a patient is unsafe, giving medication, helping with daily care, and building trust. Expect AI to cut some charting time, not jobs."},
  sentiment:"People say the work matters and that helping patients through a crisis can be rewarding, and many use it as a stepping stone to nursing, counseling, or psychology. The main complaints are safety and staffing: techs at a Nebraska state hospital protested in 2023 over rising assaults on staff, being alone with large groups of patients, and forced overtime on 12- or 16-hour shifts. Low pay for hard, emotional work and burnout come up often.",
  path:"Common routes are a short certificate or associate degree in psychiatric or behavioral health technology, or starting as a psychiatric aide or nursing assistant and training on the job. Some employers hire people with related experience and train them, and some prefer a bachelor's degree in psychology. CPR or basic life support certification is usually required. Many techs later go on to become LPNs or registered nurses, counselors, or social workers, or move into lead tech roles.",
  notes:"A few states license psychiatric technicians. California, for example, requires an approved program (or 576 theory hours and 954 clinical hours), fingerprints, and a state exam. BLS says psychiatric techs and aides have some of the highest injury and illness rates of any job, from lifting patients and from aggressive patients, so you get training in safe holds and de-escalation. Expect nights, weekends, holidays, and long standing.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Psychiatric Technicians and Aides","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/psychiatric-technicians-and-aides.htm"],["California BVNPT: Summary of requirements for licensure as a psychiatric technician","https://www.bvnpt.ca.gov/applicants/summary_pt.shtml"],["Nebraska Examiner: Mental health techs protest working conditions at Nebraska psychiatric hospital (Oct 2023)","https://nebraskaexaminer.com/2023/10/21/mental-health-techs-protest-working-conditions-safety-at-nebraska-psychiatric-hospital/"],["Incredible Health: 6th Annual State of Nursing Technicians Report (2025)","https://www.incrediblehealth.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/6th-Annual-State-of-Nursing-Technicians-Report-_ForReview_web.pdf"],["Carlat Hospital Psychiatry Report: The rise of AI scribes (2026)","https://www.thecarlatreport.com/articles/5868-transforming-psychiatric-documentation-the-rise-of-ai-scribes"],["News From the States: Kansas expands psychiatric beds by 32% (Mar 2023)","https://www.newsfromthestates.com/node/165465"]]},
 "b474051":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is about $50,260 a year (May 2025), roughly $24 an hour; the lowest 10% earn under $36,230 and the top 10% over $71,970. Pay is higher in some West Coast areas: Oregon has the highest state median at about $65,330. Most jobs are with state DOTs, counties, and cities, so pay is set by pay scales and often comes with a pension and health benefits. Snow season adds overtime, which is not counted in these figures.",
  growth:"The app's BLS data shows about 3% growth from 2025 to 2035 (about average), with about 11,800 openings a year, mostly to replace retirees and people who leave. I could not open the BLS handbook page to confirm the 2025–35 numbers. Hiring news agrees there is steady demand: state DOTs have reported snowplow driver shortages for several winters, with some states 20 to 30% short, and say many CDL holders leave for better-paying private trucking jobs.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is starting to help find road problems: in a 2026 Ohio pilot, cars with cameras spotted potholes with about 89% accuracy, so crews spend less time searching and more time fixing. Route planning, weather forecasting, and equipment tracking also use software. The actual work stays hands-on: patching pavement, fixing guardrails and signs, mowing, clearing drains, setting up work zones, and plowing snow. Self-driving plows and repair robots are still experiments."},
  sentiment:"People value stable public jobs, pensions, and benefits, and many like working outside with a crew. Common complaints are pay that lags private trucking and construction, being on call for storms at night and on holidays, long 12-hour shifts in winter, and danger from passing traffic. Agencies have struggled to fill plow seats since about 2021, which means more overtime for the people who stay.",
  path:"Most people start with a high school diploma and get a commercial driver's license (CDL); some DOTs run their own CDL training programs, and seasonal winter plow jobs are a common way in. Experience in construction, landscaping, farming, or truck driving helps. Training on equipment takes months on the job. Later steps include equipment operator, crew lead, maintenance supervisor, or moving into heavy equipment or mechanic work.",
  notes:"A CDL is usually required within months of hire, along with drug and alcohol testing. The work is physical and outdoors in heat, cold, and storms. Being struck by vehicles is the big hazard: highway maintenance workers have had the highest rate of struck-by deaths in construction. Pay and the amount of snow work vary a lot by region; northern states have heavy winter overtime.",
  sources:[["BLS OEWS: Highway Maintenance Workers (May 2025 data index)","https://www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes474051.htm"],["Wagedex: Highway Maintenance Workers, May 2025 BLS OEWS figures","https://wagedex.com/jobs/highway-maintenance-workers"],["AASHTO Journal: Survey highlights snowplow driver shortage (2022)","https://aashtojournal.transportation.org/?p=21086"],["AASHTO Journal: State DOTs adjust as snowplow driver shortage persists (2022)","https://aashtojournal.transportation.org/?p=25519"],["University of Cincinnati: AI technology could change how potholes are repaired in Ohio (Feb 2026)","https://www.uc.edu/news/articles/2026/02/ai-technology-could-change-how-potholes-are-repaired-in-ohio.html"],["Equipment World: Construction struck-by deaths up 34% since 2010 (2017)","https://www.equipmentworld.com/workforce/safety/article/14967812/construction-struck-by-deaths-up-34-since-2010"]]},
 "b493021":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for auto body repairers is $54,890 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $36,910 and the top 10% over $87,940. Most shops and dealers pay by the task (flat rate or a base plus a set amount per job), so earnings depend on how much work comes in and how fast you are. Skilled, certified techs at busy shops can earn well above the median, while slow weeks hurt. A 2026 survey found only 56% of auto, diesel, and collision techs think their pay is fair.",
  growth:"BLS projects 4% growth from 2025 to 2035 (about average), from about 163,200 to 169,300 jobs, with about 13,900 openings a year for body and glass repairers combined. Industry data says demand is strong: a 2026 TechForce report found collision schools fill only about 42% of yearly demand and collision has the highest turnover of any tech field. Repairable claims fell about 10% in 2025, so shop work can be uneven.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI mostly affects estimating, not the hands-on repair. About a quarter of insurance appraisals are now photo-based, and tools like CCC use AI to read damage photos and draft estimates. The repair itself stays human: pulling frames, replacing panels, welding, sanding, painting, and matching color. Newer cars add work, such as calibrating driver-assist sensors after a repair. Expect AI to speed up paperwork and parts ordering rather than replace techs."},
  sentiment:"Many techs like the craft and the satisfaction of making a wrecked car look new. Mood across the repair trades is gloomy though: in a 2026 survey of over 5,500 workers, 70% would not recommend the field, 77% said it is not improving, and 21% said they will probably leave. Top complaints are pay, flat-rate pay structure, buying your own tools, physical wear, and insurer pressure to repair cars cheaply and fast. Few respondents were collision techs, so it is a broad picture.",
  path:"Many start with a 6-month to 1-year collision repair program at a trade school or community college, while others start as a shop helper or detailer and learn on the job. BLS says you can be trained in about a year but need 2 to 3 years to work fully on your own. Certification is recommended and often raises pay; manufacturer and paint maker programs are common. Later steps include painter, frame specialist, estimator, shop manager, insurance appraiser, or owning a shop.",
  notes:"Techs usually buy their own tools, which can cost thousands; tools and supplies were the top barrier named by TechForce scholarship applicants. The work is physical, often in cramped or awkward positions, with dust, noise, and paint chemicals, so protective gear matters. Ask how pay works (flat rate vs. hourly plus bonus) before taking a job.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Automotive Body and Glass Repairers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/automotive-body-and-glass-repairers.htm"],["Autobody News: Collision tech pipeline fills just 42% of demand, TechForce report (June 2026)","https://www.autobodynews.com/news/collision-repairs-technician-pipeline-fills-just-42-of-demand-techforce-report-finds"],["Repairer Driven News: More than 73,000 collision techs needed by 2029 (June 2026)","https://www.repairerdrivennews.com/2026/06/08/techforce-more-than-73000-collision-techs-needed-by-2029-retention-partially-to-blame/"],["Autobody News: Technician survey signals growing retention risk (Mar 2026)","https://www.autobodynews.com/news/technician-survey-signals-growing-retention-risk-across-auto-repair-industry"],["Repairer Driven News: Technicians want proper equipment, paid vacation, retirement (Apr 2026)","https://www.repairerdrivennews.com/2026/04/09/technicians-want-proper-equipment-paid-vacation-and-retirement-funds-survey-results-show/"],["Repairer Driven News: CCC report shows shift to DRP and AI appraisals (Apr 2026)","https://www.repairerdrivennews.com/2026/04/10/ccc-report-shows-shift-to-drp-and-ai-appraisals/"]]},
 "b474011":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $74,690 a year (May 2025), about $35.91 an hour; the lowest 10% earn under $47,140 and the top 10% over $114,200. Pay is similar across employers: engineering firms about $77,500, construction $74,460, and local and state government about $73,500 to $73,900. About 4 in 10 work for government, where jobs usually include a pension and good benefits, which tradespeople often say make up for any pay cut.",
  growth:"BLS projects little or no change from 2025 to 2035 (0%), staying at about 153,900 jobs, but about 14,800 openings a year as people retire or leave. BLS notes remote inspections may reduce some government demand. The field is old: in an ICC 2025 industry report, about 79% of respondents were Baby Boomers or Gen X, and ICC calls building a new workforce pipeline a priority, so retirements should keep openings steady.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI is entering plan review: tools pre-screen permit drawings against codes, flag missing pages, and cite code sections, with about 87 to 92% accuracy in a 2025 Seattle test. Some cities also do video inspections for simple jobs. But a person still makes every approval, and AI reviews drawings, not what was actually built. Field inspection stays human: climbing, measuring, judging work on site, and explaining fixes to builders. Plan reviewers will feel AI first."},
  sentiment:"Former tradespeople on trade forums often call city inspection a great job: regular weekday hours, no bidding or payroll, and solid pensions and health benefits, though starting pay can be lower than running your own business. Downsides include office politics, pushback from contractors when work fails, heavy workloads in busy building seasons, and having to fail work by people you know. Overall mood is fairly positive. These views come from forum posts, not a survey.",
  path:"Most inspectors come from the trades, with five or more years as a carpenter, electrician, plumber, or builder, then pass a certification exam and train under an experienced inspector. Others take a community college certificate or associate degree in building inspection, or a degree in construction, engineering, or architecture. Many start as a permit tech or residential inspector. Later steps include combination inspector, plans examiner, chief inspector, or building official.",
  notes:"Most states and cities require a license or certification; many use International Code Council (ICC) exams such as Residential Building Inspector, with continuing education to keep it. A driver's license is needed. Work involves climbing ladders and crawling into tight spaces, mostly alone, with some office time for reports. Home inspectors are often self-employed and work evenings and weekends. Rules on doing side work in your own area vary, so check local ethics rules.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Construction and Building Inspectors","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/construction-and-extraction/construction-and-building-inspectors.htm"],["ICC Building Safety Journal: The code department squeeze, workforce demand (July 2026)","https://www.iccsafe.org/building-safety-journal/bsj-dives/the-code-department-squeeze-workforce-demand-and-the-case-for-local-optimization"],["Florida League of Cities: Buyers guide to AI tools for building permitting (2026)","https://www.flcities.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Buyers-Guide-AI-Tools-for-Building-Permitting.pdf"],["ICC: Residential Building Inspector B1 exam content","https://iccsafe.org/wp-content/uploads/b1_content.pdf"],["Mike Holt Forums: Electricians discuss taking a city inspector job","https://forums.mikeholt.com/threads/take-the-job-or-not.95471/post-95471"],["ICC: Survey of code professionals predicts retirement exodus (2014)","https://www.iccsafe.org/building-safety-journal/press-releases/survey-of-code-professionals-predicts-substantial-retirement-exodus"]]},
 "b151231":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $76,220 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $47,120 and the top 10% over $127,780. Pay rises most with specialization: a January 2026 TechTarget report says adding network security, cloud, or AI-infrastructure skills can move a generalist from about $80,000 toward $140,000. Pure help-desk style network roles sit at the lower end of the range.",
  growth:"BLS projects only 1% growth from 2025 to 2035 (about 152,500 to 154,400 jobs), slower than average, with roughly 9,000 openings a year, nearly all from people leaving. The wider computer support group is projected to shrink 3%. Hiring news agrees on a split market: demand stays strong for network security, cloud, and automation skills, while roles built on manual configuration and basic troubleshooting are expected to shrink.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"AI monitoring tools (AIOps) already watch networks around the clock, filter alerts, and fix common faults like DNS, DHCP, and VLAN errors on their own, and chatbots take many simple user tickets. Vendor and industry claims of 70% or more automation of desk troubleshooting are likely optimistic. What stays human: messy multi-vendor outages, judging test results in context, working with vendors, and hands-on work like cabling and swapping hardware. Expect fewer pure tier-1 roles and more value in security and automation skills."},
  sentiment:"People who stay in networking describe it as solid, problem-solving work with good pay once you specialize, but many newcomers say the CCNA alone no longer gets them hired and that entry roles want experience. Common complaints are on-call duty, night or weekend maintenance windows, and blame when outages hit. Worry about AI and automation shrinking junior roles is a frequent theme in 2026 forums and career articles.",
  path:"Most people start with an associate degree or IT certifications (CompTIA Network+, then Cisco CCNA) and a first job on a help desk or as a desktop or field technician, then move into network support in 1–3 years. Career changers often come through help desk work plus a home lab that shows real troubleshooting. Later steps are network administrator, network engineer, network security, cloud networking, or network architect, where pay is much higher.",
  notes:"No license is required, but certifications matter a lot for getting interviews; check local postings before paying for one. Expect some nights, weekends, and on-call shifts, since networks must run 24 hours a day. Some roles include on-site work with cabling and hardware in wiring closets. Red flag: jobs titled network specialist that are really low-paid help desk roles; ask what the daily work is.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Computer Support Specialists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-support-specialists.htm"],["BLS OEWS: Computer Network Support Specialists (May 2025)","https://www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes151231.htm"],["TechTarget: Advanced skills drive networking job market in 2026","https://www.techtarget.com/it-infrastructure/feature/Advanced-skills-drive-networking-job-market-in-2026"],["JobZoneRisk: Will AI replace network support specialist jobs? (relies partly on vendor sources)","https://jobzonerisk.com/roles/network-support-specialist"],["TechExams community (Infosec Institute): Do not buy into get your CCNA, get a good job automatically","https://community.infosecinstitute.com/discussion/comment/509654"],["SimeonOnSecurity: Is IT a good career in 2026? The AI reality check","https://simeononsecurity.com/it-career-playbook/getting-started-in-it/is-it-a-good-career/"]]},
 "b319097":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $45,230 a year, or $21.75 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $35,780 and the top 10% over $58,780. The range is narrow, so experience raises pay only modestly. Hospitals and outpatient labs may pay extra for nights, weekends, and holidays, and mobile or donor-center roles sometimes add travel pay. Pay is highest in states with licensing and higher living costs, such as California.",
  growth:"BLS projects 7% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), adding about 9,800 jobs, with about 18,000 openings a year, many from people moving on. Hospitals report real shortages: a July 2025 CAP Today report quotes Northwestern Medicine saying staff absences left outpatient samples uncollected. Robotic blood draw devices are arriving, but slowly and with a phlebotomist still in charge.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"In August 2026 the FDA authorized Aletta, the first robot that draws blood on its own, for adult outpatients. It uses imaging to find a vein, inserts the needle, fills tubes, and bandages, but a trained phlebotomist must start each session, check every sample, handle patients it cannot find a vein on, and clean it. One person may oversee up to three units. Hard sticks, children, hospital inpatients, home visits, and calming nervous patients stay human. Long-term staffing effects are not yet known."},
  sentiment:"Many phlebotomists like the short training, steady demand, and patient contact, and many use the job as a stepping stone to nursing, PA, or lab careers. The common complaints are low pay for the responsibility, few raises, heavy draw quotas, and short staffing; employee reviews at big lab chains often mention high turnover and being overworked. Those reviews are individual accounts, not surveys, so treat them as a general mood rather than hard data.",
  path:"Most people take a phlebotomy certificate program at a community college or technical school, usually under a year and often just a few months, including supervised draws. Some employers hire high school graduates and train on the job. Then sit for a national certification such as CPT (NHA), PBT (ASCP), or RPT (AMT). It is an easy switch from retail or customer service. Later steps include lead phlebotomist, lab assistant, medical lab technician, nursing, or other clinical training.",
  notes:"California, Louisiana, Nevada, and Washington require a state license or certification; elsewhere employers usually prefer national certification. Basic Life Support (CPR) and a driver's license may be needed. Long periods of standing, exposure to blood and needle-stick risk, and nights, weekends, and holidays in hospitals and labs. About 36% work in hospitals and 33% in labs. Red flag: pricey quick programs that skip enough real supervised draws.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Phlebotomists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/phlebotomists.htm"],["CAP Today: Too few phlebotomists. Is Aletta the answer? (July 2025)","https://www.captodayonline.com/too-few-phlebotomists-is-aletta-the-answer/"],["Patient Care: FDA authorizes Aletta robotic blood draw device for outpatient use (Aug 2026)","https://www.patientcareonline.com/view/fda-authorizes-aletta-robotic-blood-draw-device-for-outpatient-use"],["Epocrates: First autonomous blood draw robot authorized by FDA","https://www.epocrates.com/online/article/first-autonomous-blood-draw-robot-authorized-by-fda"],["Wikipedia: Phlebotomy licensure in the United States","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phlebotomy_licensure_in_the_United_States"],["Glassdoor: Labcorp phlebotomist review (individual account)","https://www.glassdoor.de/Bewertungen/Bewertungen-Labcorp-E1679-RVW47160971.htm"]]},
 "b492022":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS OEWS puts the median for this exact occupation at about $64,000 a year (May 2025), with the lowest 10% near $44,000 and the top 10% near $97,000. The OOH page, which groups these techs with line installers, gives a median of $66,200 ($31.83 an hour). Entry pay in postings is lower: a current Spectrum installer posting starts at $20.50 an hour with 10% raises per tech level. Union phone company jobs usually pay more than contractor work.",
  growth:"BLS projects the telecom installer and line worker group to shrink 3% from 2025 to 2035 as 5G upgrades and funded broadband builds finish and fiber needs fewer repairs, yet it still expects about 20,300 openings a year for the group (about 11,300 for this occupation) from retirements and turnover. News points the other way in the near term: Pew (Oct 2025) and RCR Wireless (Aug 2026) report shortages of fiber technicians as BEAD rural broadband and AI data centers compete for the same workers.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI and remote tools now catch many faults before customers notice, reroute traffic, plan routes, and guide customers through self-installs or video troubleshooting, which cuts the number of service visits. That is the main pressure on jobs, along with copper being retired. The hands-on work stays human: running and splicing cable, installing and fixing equipment in homes, central offices, and data centers, climbing, and solving odd field problems. Much of the reporting on AI here comes from vendors and consultants, so take bold claims with caution."},
  sentiment:"Techs often like being out of the office, solving real problems, and learning fiber skills that are in demand, especially for data centers. Common complaints are working in heat, cold, attics, and crawl spaces, tight job quotas, long hours during storms, and lower pay and benefits at contractors compared with union phone companies. Union coverage in the sector fell from 26% in 2000 to 11% in 2024, per Pew, as work shifted to contractors.",
  path:"Many start with just a high school diploma and a driver's license as a cable installer or field tech, trained on the job over months to years; a certificate or associate degree in electronics, telecom, or networking helps. Short fiber courses (some free, funded by states or companies) and registered apprenticeships like TIRAP are growing. Next steps include fiber splicer, central office or data center tech, lead tech, network technician, or supervisor.",
  notes:"Physically demanding: climbing ladders up to about 32 feet, lifting up to 75 pounds, confined spaces, and all weather. BLS lists telecom installers among the jobs with high injury rates, mostly falls and strains. A clean driving record is usually required; nights, weekends, and on-call work are common. Pay and security are better at union carriers (often CWA) than with piece-rate contractors. Red flag: contractor jobs that pay per job and make you supply your own truck and tools.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Telecommunications Equipment Installers and Repairers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/telecommunications-equipment-installers-and-repairers-except-line-installers.htm"],["Pew: Demand for broadband workforce expected to rise to meet BEAD requirements (Oct 2025)","https://www.pew.org/en/research-and-analysis/issue-briefs/2025/10/demand-for-broadband-workforce-expected-to-rise-to-meet-bead-requirements"],["RCR Wireless: AI data centers and BEAD compete for fiber skills (Aug 2026)","https://rcrwireless.com/20260813/network-infrastructure/ai-data-center-fiber-bead-skills"],["Route Fifty: How states can address broadband worker shortages (Jul 2026)","https://www.route-fifty.com/digital-government/2026/07/how-states-can-address-broadband-worker-shortages/414614/"],["Spectrum cable installer job posting ($20.50 an hour start)","https://talents.vaia.com/companies/spectrum/spicer/cable-installer-20-ph-starting-spicer-151302686/"],["Sand Technologies (consultancy blog): How will AI affect the future telecom workforce","https://www.sandtech.com/insight/how-will-ai-affect-the-future-telecom-workforce"]]},
 "b291126":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $82,280 a year, or $39.56 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $63,660 and the top 10% over $118,050. Because 80% work in hospitals that run around the clock, night, weekend, and holiday differentials and overtime add to base pay. Travel and contract RT jobs, which grew during COVID and have stayed, often pay more for short assignments.",
  growth:"BLS projects 9% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), with about 8,600 openings a year, driven by an aging population, more COPD and pneumonia, and more care at home and in clinics. Hiring news agrees: in an AARC survey reported in late 2024, 87% of respiratory care leaders said there is a local shortage now, and program enrollment has fallen about 27% since 2010 while many RTs near retirement.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI tools can flag patients who are getting worse, read ventilator waveforms for problems, suggest ventilator settings, score readiness to come off a ventilator, and draft charting. These help but still need a therapist to check and act on them. The core work stays human: bedside assessment, managing airways in emergencies and codes, adjusting ventilators on very sick patients, suctioning, arterial blood draws, and coaching scared patients and families. Expect AI to reduce paperwork, not jobs."},
  sentiment:"RTs often say the work is meaningful and respected, with real impact in ICUs and emergencies, and that jobs are easy to find with flexible three-day, 12-hour schedules and travel options. Burnout is the big complaint: AARC surveys have found most RTs report burnout, tied to short staffing, heavy workloads, very sick patients, and seeing many deaths. Some also feel the career ladder is short beyond lead or management roles.",
  path:"Most people complete an associate degree in respiratory therapy at an accredited program (about two years, with clinical rotations); some employers prefer a bachelor's. Then pass the NBRC exams for the CRT and then RRT credential and get a state license. Career changers often finish prerequisites first. Later steps include specialty credentials (neonatal and pediatric, adult critical care, sleep, pulmonary function), lead therapist, department manager, educator, or sales and clinical roles with equipment makers.",
  notes:"A state license is required everywhere except Alaska, and many employers require the RRT within a set time. Continuing education keeps the license active. Expect long periods of standing, lifting and turning patients, 12-hour shifts, nights, weekends, holidays, and exposure to infections like flu, TB, and COVID. Programs are competitive and rigorous. Pay and demand vary by region, and travel contracts can boost income.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Respiratory Therapists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/respiratory-therapists.htm"],["Becker's Hospital Review: As RSV cases climb, respiratory therapist deficits loom larger (Dec 2024)","https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/workforce/as-rsv-cases-climb-respiratory-therapist-deficits-loom-larger.html"],["NBRC: Why the world needs more RTs, and how you can help (2021)","https://www.nbrc.org/why-the-world-needs-more-rts-and-how-you-can-help/"],["Respiratory Therapy Zone: Pros and cons of being a respiratory therapist","https://www.respiratorytherapyzone.com/?p=156773"],["CareerExplorer: AI impact on respiratory therapists","https://www.careerexplorer.com/careers/respiratory-therapist/ai-impact/"]]},
 "b493011":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for aircraft mechanics and service technicians is $79,870 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $48,780 and the top 10% over $128,890. Pay depends a lot on employer: airlines, pay at the top, while small general aviation shops pay less. A federal College Scorecard figure cited by NPR in 2026 shows graduates of one large mechanic school earning a median of about $80,800 four years out. Overtime and shift premiums are common.",
  growth:"BLS projects 5% growth from 2025 to 2035 (about average), from about 140,000 jobs, with roughly 11,400 openings a year for mechanics alone (13,100 counting avionics techs), mostly to replace retirees. Hiring news agrees and is stronger than the BLS number suggests: Boeing forecasts North America needs about 123,000 new maintenance technicians through 2044, and NPR reported in July 2026 that new graduates were leaving school with job offers from airlines and GE Aerospace.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is spreading in the planning and diagnosis side: predictive maintenance flags parts likely to fail from sensor and flight data, computer vision speeds up engine borescope inspections, and software helps plan inspections and sort fault reports. The core job stays human: hands-on repair and replacement, troubleshooting odd problems, following FAA-approved procedures, and the licensed sign-off that legally returns an aircraft to service. Expect more data and tablet work, not fewer mechanics; most of the AI reporting comes from schools and vendors with an interest in the topic."},
  sentiment:"People in the trade are proud of the work: in a January 2026 General Aviation News survey of maintenance shops, 92% said they were proud of their profession, though fewer than half felt optimistic about general aviation overall and staffing was the top constraint. A frequent complaint is that pay at small general aviation shops lags auto and marine repair despite more legal responsibility. Airline jobs pay better but often mean nights, weekends, holidays, and pressure to get planes out on time.",
  path:"The usual route is an FAA-approved aviation maintenance technician school (often under two years; some community colleges add an associate degree), then passing the FAA written, oral, and practical tests for the airframe and powerplant (A&P) certificate. Others qualify through military aviation maintenance or documented on-the-job experience working under a certified mechanic. Many new mechanics start at regional airlines or repair stations and move to major airlines after two or three years. Later steps include inspector, Inspection Authorization (IA), lead, or maintenance manager.",
  notes:"Employers usually want both airframe and powerplant ratings. Work is in hangars, repair stations, or on the ramp, sometimes outdoors in all weather, with crouching, lying under aircraft, ladders, scaffolds, noise, and vibration. Shifts of 8 or 10 hours often include nights, weekends, and holidays. School costs vary widely: one private school charges about $42,000, while community college programs usually cost less, so compare before borrowing.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Aircraft and Avionics Equipment Mechanics and Technicians","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/aircraft-and-avionics-equipment-mechanics-and-technicians.htm"],["NPR via WUNC: New aviation mechanics graduate with jobs in hand thanks to a labor shortage (July 2026)","https://www.wunc.org/2026-07-09/new-aviation-mechanics-graduate-with-jobs-in-hand-thanks-to-a-labor-shortage"],["General Aviation News: General aviation maintenance, what is working and what is not (Jan 2026)","https://generalaviationnews.com/2026/01/09/general-aviation-maintenance-whats-working-whats-not/"],["Aviation Institute of Maintenance (school blog): How AI will change the role of aircraft mechanics","https://aviationmaintenance.edu/ai-aircraft-maintenance-future-mechanics/"],["Mechanics Log: How bad is the mechanic shortage","https://mechanicslog.beehiiv.com/p/how-bad-is-the-mechanic-shortage"]]},
 "b472181":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for roofers is $55,440 a year, about $26.65 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $37,460 and the top 10% over $81,720. Pay varies by state: BLS-based figures show mean pay around $67,000 to $72,000 in Massachusetts, New York, Illinois, and Washington but about $44,000 to $47,000 in Texas and Florida. Union roofers get benefits and pension on top of the hourly rate. Residential shingle crews are often paid by the square installed, which can pay well for fast crews but swings with the weather and roof type.",
  growth:"BLS projects 5% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than the 3% average), about 8,800 new jobs, with about 12,000 openings a year, mostly replacing people who leave the trade. Hiring news agrees that workers are scarce: contractors report trouble filling craft jobs, and roofing trade press in 2026 calls the labor shortage structural, made worse by stricter immigration enforcement in an industry that relies heavily on immigrant crews.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI and drones are taking over the office and inspection side: satellite and drone images produce roof measurements without climbing, computer vision flags hail damage or missing shingles, and software drafts estimates, insurance photo notes, and crew schedules. The actual roofing stays human: tearing off old roofs, installing shingles, metal, or membrane, flashing, and working safely at height on roofs that are all different. Roofing robots are still experimental. The best AI write-ups come from roofing software vendors, so treat their claims with care."},
  sentiment:"People in the trade value steady demand, quick entry without school, and the chance to earn well during busy storm seasons or move into running crews or a business. Common complaints are hard physical wear on knees and backs, heat, seasonal feast-or-famine work in colder states, and piece-rate pressure that pushes speed over quality. Storm-season overtime brings burnout and injury risk, and crews often get poached by storm-chasing contractors.",
  path:"Most roofers start as helpers or laborers and learn on the job; no diploma is required, though employers usually want OSHA safety training and sometimes a driver's license. Some enter through a union apprenticeship (about three to four years, starting near half of journey-level pay). Over a few years you can move to lead roofer, foreman, or superintendent, or sideways into estimating, sales, project management, safety, or owning a roofing company. About 20% of roofers are self-employed.",
  notes:"This is one of the most dangerous jobs in the country: BLS data put roofers' 2023 fatal injury rate at 51.8 per 100,000 workers, third highest of all occupations, with falls causing most deaths. Expect climbing, kneeling, heavy lifting, and outdoor work in extreme heat and cold. Work slows in northern winters. Red flags: crews that skip fall protection and storm-chasing outfits with no local base.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Roofers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/construction-and-extraction/roofers.htm"],["Roofing Contractor: Roofing remains a top 3 deadliest occupation in the US (Dec 2024)","https://www.roofingcontractor.com/articles/100253-roofing-remains-a-top-3-deadliest-occupation-in-the-us"],["Roofing Contractor: Navigating immigration regulations in roofing (Jan 2026)","https://www.roofingcontractor.com/articles/101790-navigating-immigration-regulations-in-roofing"],["JobNimbus (vendor blog): Roofer salary 2026, union rates, top states","https://wf.jobnimbus.com/blog/how-much-do-roofers-make"],["RoofSnap (vendor blog): 9 AI applications in the roofing industry","https://roofsnap.com/blog/ai-applications-roofing-industry/"],["NRCA: Labor shortage will continue to affect contractors in 2024","https://nrca.net/RoofingNews/labor-shortage-will-continue-to-affect-contractors-in-2024.1-11-2024.11889/Details/Story"]]},
 "b499051":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for electrical power-line installers and repairers is $95,320 a year, about $45.83 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $51,470 (mostly apprentices) and the top 10% over $128,690. Overtime and storm restoration work often push yearly earnings well above the base rate, and many lineworkers are union members with pensions and health benefits.",
  growth:"BLS projects 10% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), with about 10,900 openings a year. Hiring news strongly agrees: POWER Magazine reported in January 2026 that 89% of transmission and distribution construction employers have trouble finding qualified workers, retirements outpace new entrants in grid jobs, and Goldman Sachs estimates utility apprenticeships need to rise from about 45,000 to 65,000 a year, driven by data centers, factory reshoring, and electrification.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"Drones and AI are changing inspection: cameras and infrared sensors check towers and lines from the ground, AI sorts thousands of images to spot damage, and predictive maintenance helps utilities plan work. Utilities say this lets crews decide when a climb is needed and prepare ahead, not replace them. Climbing poles, working from bucket trucks, stringing and splicing energized lines, and storm restoration remain hands-on human work, and some lineworkers are being trained as drone pilots."},
  sentiment:"Lineworkers praise the pay, overtime, union benefits, strong crew culture, and the pride of restoring power after storms. The biggest complaints are time away from home: storm callouts at any hour, out-of-state deployments lasting days to months, and missed holidays and family events, which a 2026 T&D World feature on lineworker spouses describes in detail. The job is also physically punishing and stressful, with constant awareness of electrocution and fall risk.",
  path:"Most enter with a high school diploma through a paid apprenticeship of up to about 3 years with a utility, contractor, or union program. Many first take a lineworker certificate course (BLS notes 1-year certificate and 2-year associate programs at community colleges) to stand out, or start as a groundman or utility helper. A CDL is usually needed. After topping out as a journeyman lineworker, steps include foreman, general foreman, trouble or substation work, safety, or supervision.",
  notes:"Expect climbing poles and towers, heavy gear, and outdoor work in all weather, often over 40 hours a week and with long stretches during storms. Electrocution is the main hazard, and industry press ranks line work among the most dangerous jobs, though training and protective equipment have cut risk. A commercial driver's license is usually required. Many lineworkers belong to unions. Pay and overtime vary by region and employer, with storm-prone areas busiest.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Line Installers and Repairers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/line-installers-and-repairers.htm"],["POWER Magazine: Bridging the gap, how the power industry is tackling its workforce crisis (Jan 2026)","https://www.powermag.com/bridging-the-gap-how-the-power-industry-is-tackling-its-workforce-crisis/"],["T&D World: Line wives, the power behind the power (Aug 2026)","https://www.tdworld.com/electric-utility-operations/article/55393128/line-wives-the-power-behind-the-power"],["CareerVillage: What are the pros and cons of being a lineman","https://www.careervillage.org/questions/1188040/what-are-the-pros-and-cons-of-being-a-lineman"],["Rockefeller Institute: How drones are changing the role of the electric utility worker (2019)","https://rockinst.org/blog/how-drones-are-changing-the-role-of-the-electric-utility-worker/"]]},
 "b292056":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $47,380 a year, about $22.78 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $35,710 and the top 10% over $63,180. NAVTA's 2024 member survey found average gross income of about $53,800, a bit above BLS, likely because members skew credentialed and experienced. Low pay is the top complaint: 39% call it the biggest challenge of the job. Specialty, emergency, research, and industry jobs tend to pay more than general practice.",
  growth:"BLS projects 9% growth from 2025 to 2035, much faster than average, from about 131,400 to 143,800 jobs, with about 13,400 openings a year. Many openings come from people leaving the field. AVMA economic reporting shows practices consolidating into larger hospitals and corporate groups (about 30% of U.S. practices), which keeps demand for skilled techs steady, though high turnover rather than fast new hiring drives much of it.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"A 2026 Digitail and AAHA survey found most veterinary staff now use AI, mainly scribes that draft medical notes and help with client messages; AI reading of x-rays, lab results, and cytology is growing but less common. That trims charting and paperwork for techs. The core work stays human and hands-on: restraining and nursing animals, placing catheters, anesthesia monitoring, dental cleanings, drawing blood, and calming owners. Fear of job loss among vet staff fell from 37% to 20% in two years."},
  sentiment:"Techs love the animals and the teamwork, but surveys show heavy strain. In NAVTA's 2024 survey, 88% had felt compassion fatigue, only 8% were extremely satisfied (down from 25% in 2022), and just 57% would choose the career again. Common complaints are low pay for the skill level, being underused (only 36% feel fully used, and many see little difference from untrained assistants), student debt, and turnover. Many work a second job.",
  path:"The usual route is a two-year AVMA-accredited associate degree in veterinary technology (four-year bachelor's for technologists), then passing the national VTNE exam and any state requirements to become a CVT, LVT, or RVT. Many people start as a kennel worker or vet assistant first; a few states (Montana, Wisconsin, Alaska, Utah) still allow on-the-job training routes. Later steps include a specialty credential (VTS) in areas like anesthesia, emergency, or dentistry, practice or hospital manager, research, teaching, or industry sales.",
  notes:"Most states require a credential to use the tech title, and rules vary by state; NAVTA is pushing to rename the role registered veterinary nurse. The VTNE costs about $375 and is offered four times a year. The work is physical: lifting large dogs, standing all day, and risk of bites, scratches, and exposure to anesthesia gases and x-rays. Emergency and specialty hospitals run nights, weekends, and holidays. Euthanasia and difficult owners add emotional load. Watch for clinics that pay credentialed techs the same as untrained staff.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Veterinary Technologists and Technicians","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/veterinary-technologists-and-technicians.htm"],["NAVTA: 2024 Demographic Survey reveals strong feelings on title protection, income, role clarity, and wellness (Apr 2025)","https://navta.net/news/navtas-2024-demographic-survey-reveals-strong-feelings-on-title-protection-income-role-clarity-and-wellness/"],["AVMA At Work: Veterinary technicians being paid more but still face concerns about wages, burnout, debt (2022 survey)","https://atwork.avma.org/news/veterinary-technicians-being-paid-more-still-face-concerns-about-wages-burnout-debt"],["dvm360: AI use among veterinary professionals more than doubles in 2 years (Digitail and AAHA survey, Oct 2026; Digitail is an AI vendor)","https://www.dvm360.com/view/artificial-intelligence-use-among-veterinary-professionals-more-than-doubles-in-2-years"],["ExamCert: How to become a veterinary technician (2026 guide, test-prep site)","https://www.examcert.app/blog/how-to-become-a-veterinary-technician/"],["dvm360: 2025 economic state of the veterinary profession (AVMA report summary)","https://www.dvm360.com/view/2025-economic-state-of-the-veterinary-profession-trends-and-opportunities-for-your-practice"]]},
 "b319096":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $38,150 a year, about $18.34 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $30,120 and the top 10% over $49,150. Research animal jobs at universities and labs often pay a bit more: 2026 postings for lab animal caretakers and technicians commonly list about $17 to $29 an hour, with AALAS certification preferred. Some jobs are part time, so yearly income can be lower than the hourly rate suggests.",
  growth:"BLS projects 9% growth from 2025 to 2035, much faster than average, from about 132,100 to 144,200 jobs. There are about 23,200 openings a year, a high number for the field size, mostly because many people move on to other jobs or leave the workforce. That means jobs are easy to find, but it also reflects high turnover. About 86% of jobs are in veterinary clinics and hospitals; the rest are in universities and research labs.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"This is hands-on work: feeding and watering animals, cleaning and disinfecting cages, restraining pets for exams, giving oral or topical medicine, and sterilizing equipment. AI tools spreading through vet clinics in 2026, such as note-writing scribes and client messaging, mostly affect veterinarians, techs, and front desk staff, not animal care itself. Research facilities may add more monitoring sensors and automated records, but someone still has to handle and care for the animals."},
  sentiment:"Many people take the job out of love for animals and as a way into vet tech or vet school, and they value the hands-on learning. Common complaints are low pay, short staffing, physical wear, and the emotional weight of sick animals and euthanasia. Turnover is high, and forum posts often describe people jumping between clinics for small raises or leaving the field. Lab animal jobs at universities are often seen as steadier with better benefits, though some find research work emotionally hard.",
  path:"No degree is needed; most people start with a high school diploma and learn on the job in a few weeks to months, often after volunteering at a shelter or working as a kennel attendant. Optional programs lead to NAVTA's Approved Veterinary Assistant designation. In research settings, AALAS certifications (ALAT, then LAT and LATG) build with experience and are often required for promotion. Common next steps are an accredited vet tech program, lab animal technician or supervisor roles, or vet school.",
  notes:"BLS reports this job has one of the highest injury and illness rates of any occupation, mostly from bites, scratches, and kicks, plus lifting and long hours standing. Shifts can include nights, weekends, and holidays, and some jobs are part time. Assistants may help with euthanasia and handle abused or very sick animals. Research facility jobs may require background checks and work with lab animals, which some people find hard. Assistants cannot do tasks that state law reserves for credentialed techs.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Veterinary Assistants and Laboratory Animal Caretakers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/veterinary-assistants-and-laboratory-animal-caretakers.htm"],["NAVTA: Veterinary assistants and the Approved Veterinary Assistant designation","https://navta.net/veterinary-assistants"],["O*NET OnLine: Assistant Laboratory Animal Technician (ALAT) certification","https://onetonline.org/link/certinfo/2366-A"],["ZipRecruiter: ALAT laboratory animal job postings (2026)","https://www.ziprecruiter.com/Jobs/Alat/2"],["dvm360: AI use among veterinary professionals more than doubles in 2 years (Digitail and AAHA survey, Oct 2026; Digitail is an AI vendor)","https://www.dvm360.com/view/artificial-intelligence-use-among-veterinary-professionals-more-than-doubles-in-2-years"]]},
 "b291021":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for general dentists is $170,950 a year (May 2025); for all dentists the lowest 10% earn under $89,100 and the top 10% over $337,210. The ADA reports that inflation-adjusted income for general dentists fell from about $267,000 in 2010 to roughly $207,000 to $210,000 in 2024. Practice owners usually earn more than employees, but take on business risk. New grads often carry large loans, so early take-home pay can feel smaller than the salary suggests.",
  growth:"BLS projects 6% growth for dentists from 2025 to 2035, faster than average, with about 4,800 openings a year across all dentists; general dentists number about 141,900. The ADA expects dentist supply to keep growing through 2040. Demand is uneven: about 59.5 dentists per 100,000 people nationally, from about 40 in Arkansas to over 100 in Washington, D.C., so rural areas often need dentists more than big cities.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"FDA-cleared AI from companies like Pearl, Overjet, and VideaHealth already reads x-rays to flag cavities, calculus, and bone loss, acting as a second opinion, and helps with notes, patient education, and insurance claim paperwork. Research shows these tools improve detection but still need more real-world validation. The core work stays human: hands-on procedures like fillings, crowns, root canals, and extractions, final diagnosis, treatment decisions, and earning patient trust."},
  sentiment:"Dentists generally value the steady demand, good pay, and control over their schedules, with fewer nights and weekends than most health jobs. In an ADA survey for 2026, the biggest worries were low insurance reimbursement and claim denials (55%), hiring and keeping staff (54%), and rising overhead (42%); burnout ranked low at 5%. Common complaints are student debt, pressure to meet production goals at some corporate (DSO) offices, and back and neck strain.",
  path:"The path is a bachelor's degree with science prerequisites, the Dental Admission Test, and four years of dental school for a DDS or DMD, so about eight years after high school. Then pass the national board exam and a state or regional clinical exam to get licensed. No residency is required for general dentistry, though some do a one-year residency. Many start as associates in private or DSO offices, then buy into or open a practice; others specialize (orthodontics, oral surgery) with 2 to 6 more years.",
  notes:"A license is required in every state. Dental school is costly: the average 2025 graduate with debt owed about $297,800 (ADEA). About one-third of dentists are in solo practice, but DSO affiliation has doubled since 2015 to about 16%, and more than one in four newer dentists work with DSOs. The job is physically demanding on the back, neck, hands, and eyes. Running a practice means managing staff, insurance, and overhead. Women are about 40% of dentists and half of those under 35.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Dentists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/dentists.htm"],["DentistryIQ: 2024 ADA HPI Workforce Report unpacks emerging trends (Oct 2025)","https://www.dentistryiq.com/dentistry/research-and-news/article/55318380/the-future-of-dentistry-2024-ada-hpi-workforce-report-unpacks-emerging-trends"],["Becker's Dental Review: Reimbursements, staffing and expenses among dentists' challenges for 2026 (ADA HPI survey)","https://www.beckersdental.com/?p=21680"],["ADEA: Dentists of Tomorrow 2025, graduating dental student survey","https://www.adea.org/home/publications/research-and-data/graduating-oral-health-students/dentists-of-tomorrow-2025"],["Wikipedia: Dental AI (overview of FDA-cleared tools and evidence)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dental_AI"]]},
 "b472211":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $61,800 a year, about $29.71 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $38,630 and the top 10% over $105,650. Pay depends heavily on union status and region. As one example, the union contract for Sheet Metal Local 10 in St. Cloud, Minnesota pays journeyworkers $49.33 an hour ($81.68 with benefits) for 2025–26, with apprentices starting at 50% of that and stepping up every 1,000 hours. Apprentices start well below the median, so the first few years pay less.",
  growth:"BLS projects 3% growth from 2025 to 2035 (about average), with about 9,800 openings a year, mostly from retirements and people leaving the trade. Trade press in late 2025 described data center construction as one of the largest building waves in years and a major driver of HVAC duct work, while contractors flagged labor shortages, tariff and steel price swings, and canceled megaprojects as risks. Shop fabrication work depends more on manufacturing cycles.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI and software mostly touch the office and shop side: CAD and BIM detailing, nesting parts on sheets, and CNC plasma, laser, and press brake programming. Fabrication shops are adding robotic welding and automated lasers, which lets fewer or less experienced people run machines, but owners describe this as covering a worker shortage rather than replacing skilled staff. Field installation of ductwork on job sites, measuring, problem-solving around other trades, and climbing and rigging stay human."},
  sentiment:"People in the trade generally like the pay and benefits once they reach journeyworker, the earn-while-you-learn apprenticeship, and seeing a finished building. Common complaints are the physical toll on knees and back, weather, early starts, and periods of layoff between jobs in construction. Union members often praise pensions and health plans; online forums show many questions from new apprentices about getting into a local and passing entry tests. Hard data on sentiment is thin, so treat this as a general picture.",
  path:"Most people enter through a 4- or 5-year apprenticeship that mixes paid on-the-job training with classroom instruction; you need to be 18 with a high school diploma or equivalent, and many union programs use an aptitude test and interview. Career changers are common since no prior experience is required. Factory and shop workers often learn on the job or at a technical school instead. After journeyworker status, people move into foreman, superintendent, estimating, CAD detailing, testing and balancing, or starting a contracting business.",
  notes:"The work is physical: lifting heavy material, standing, bending, and working on ladders and scaffolds at height, sometimes outdoors in all weather. Cuts, burns, and falls are the main injury risks, and shop work brings noise and dust. Apprenticeships are run by unions (SMART, the Sheet Metal Workers union) with contractors, and by nonunion programs; union pay varies a lot by city. Some areas require welding certifications or licenses for certain work. Construction jobs end, so expect gaps between projects in slow years.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Sheet Metal Workers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/construction-and-extraction/sheet-metal-workers.htm"],["SMART Local 10: St. Cloud wage and apprentice rate sheet, 2025–26","https://www.smw10.org/uploads/1/3/3/9/133979004/st._cloud_com_4-28-2025.pdf"],["ACHR News: How the sheet metal industry is responding to the data center boom (Nov 2025)","https://www.achrnews.com/articles/165468-how-the-sheet-metal-industry-is-responding-to-the-data-center-boom"],["The Fabricator: The search for workers continues to occupy the minds of metal fabricators (2023)","https://blog.thefabricator.com/thefabricator/article/shopmanagement/the-search-for-workers-continues-to-occupy-the-minds-of-metal-fabricators"],["GummySearch: r/SheetMetalUnion subreddit overview","https://gummysearch.com/r/SheetMetalUnion/"]]},
 "b292055":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for surgical technologists is $64,650 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $45,940 and the top 10% over $96,940. State medians range from about $49,000 in West Virginia and Alabama to over $80,000 in Hawaii, Oregon, California, Massachusetts, and Alaska. Becker's notes Minnesota ranks highest once cost of living is factored in. Travel contracts and specialties such as heart, neuro, orthopedic, and robotic surgery can pay more, but solid current figures for those are hard to find.",
  growth:"BLS projects 5% growth from 2025 to 2035 (faster than average) for surgical technologists and assistants combined, with about 8,600 openings a year for the combined group. A July 2026 Becker's report describes an ongoing shortage: accredited programs have dropped from over 500 to about 430, while an aging population, more surgeries, and the shift to outpatient surgery centers raise demand. Hiring news agrees that experienced, certified techs are in demand.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is starting to help with counting and tracking instruments and sponges, inventory, and case scheduling; a 2026 study found an image-based system cut simulated instrument counts by about a third, though it was small and run by the system's developers. Robotic surgery changes the job rather than removing it, since techs set up, drape, and troubleshoot the robot. Anticipating the surgeon's next need, keeping the sterile field, and hands-on work at the table stay human."},
  sentiment:"Techs often say they love the fast pace, seeing surgery up close, and working with good teams and surgeons. The most common complaint is pay that feels low for the stress and skill involved, followed by understaffing, call shifts, burnout, and feeling undervalued compared with nurses. Pay lags the broader health care field, which reporting links to the shortage.",
  path:"Most people complete an accredited certificate or associate degree program in surgical technology at a community college, vocational school, or hospital, with supervised clinical rotations in real operating rooms. Military training also counts. Career changers with no health care background can enter this way. After graduating, most take the CST exam from the NBSTSA. Later steps include specializing (heart, neuro, ortho, robotics), travel contracts, surgical first assistant, OR educator or lead tech, sterile processing management, or nursing school.",
  notes:"Certification is not always required by law, but many employers require or prefer the CST, and some states regulate surgical techs, so check your state. You will need CPR or basic life support. The job means long hours standing in sterile gear, lifting heavy instrument trays, exposure to blood and infection, and night, weekend, and holiday call at hospitals. BLS says surgical techs have one of the highest injury and illness rates of any job. Watch out for nonaccredited programs, which can block certification.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Surgical Assistants and Technologists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/surgical-technologists.htm"],["Becker's Hospital Review: The surgical technologist shortage, explained (July 2026)","https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/academic-medical-center/academic-medical-centers-workforce/the-surgical-technologist-shortage-explained/"],["Trade Career Path: Surgical tech salary 2026 by state and specialty","https://tradecareerpath.com/trades/surgical-tech/salary/"],["PayScale: Certified Surgical Technologist employee reviews","https://www.payscale.com/research/US/Job=Certified_Surgical_Technologist_(CST)/Reviews"],["HealthManagement.org: Image-based system shortens simulated instrument counts (July 2026)","https://healthmanagement.org/c/it/news/image-based-system-shortens-simulated-instrument-counts"]]},
 "b312021":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for physical therapist assistants is $68,380 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $47,750 and the top 10% over $89,510. Pay depends mainly on location, setting, experience, and local demand. The pay ceiling is fairly low, since the top 10% sit only about $20,000 above the median. Medicare cuts have made some employers hold back raises.",
  growth:"BLS projects 23% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), driven by an aging population and chronic conditions. BLS lists about 24,900 openings a year for assistants and aides combined; the app's BLS data puts PTAs alone near 18,500. Recent reporting is more mixed: Medicare payment rates have fallen in real terms, and since 2022 Medicare pays 15% less for outpatient services delivered by assistants, which has led some employers to rethink PTA staffing.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is mainly arriving in paperwork: AI scribes that draft visit notes, pre-visit summaries, billing codes, and home exercise programs. Vendors claim large time savings (vendor blog), but notes still need review and signing, and independent tests of AI scribes still find errors. The core of the job is hands-on: guiding exercises, manual therapy, gait and balance training, helping patients move safely, and keeping them motivated. That work stays human."},
  sentiment:"PTAs often say they enjoy helping people regain movement, building relationships with patients, and having a good job with only a two-year degree. Common complaints are high productivity quotas, back-to-back patients, documentation done off the clock, and pay that grows slowly. Medicare cuts have hurt morale, and about half of physical therapists report burnout, which PTAs often share in the same clinics. The limited career ladder is another common frustration.",
  path:"You need an associate degree (about 2 years) from a CAPTE-accredited PTA program, with anatomy, physiology, and kinesiology plus supervised clinical rotations, then pass the national PTA licensing exam (NPTE) and any state law exam. Programs can be competitive and often want prerequisite courses and observation hours. Career changers are common. Later options include specializing (geriatrics, pediatrics, sports), clinic coordination or management, teaching, or going back for a Doctor of Physical Therapy degree to become a PT.",
  notes:"All states require PTAs to be licensed or certified, usually with continuing education to keep it. PTAs must work under a physical therapist's supervision, and the rules vary by state and payer. The job is physical: on your feet, kneeling, bending, and lifting or moving patients, with a risk of back injury. Part-time and weekend work is common. Watch first-time exam pass rates when choosing a program, since some report pass rates far below their eventual rates. Medicare's 15% payment cut for assistant care can affect hiring in some settings.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Physical Therapist Assistants and Aides","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/physical-therapist-assistants-and-aides.htm"],["Skilled Nursing News: Medicare therapy cut worsens SNF staffing crisis (2022)","https://skillednursingnews.com/2022/04/were-hurting-big-time-medicare-therapy-cut-worsens-snf-staffing-crisis-care-access/"],["Physitrack (vendor blog): Why physical therapists are burning out, the reimbursement squeeze (Aug 2026)","https://www.physitrack.com/insights/physical-therapy-reimbursement-burnout"],["Physitrack (vendor blog): Physical therapist burnout statistics (Aug 2026)","https://www.physitrack.com/insights/physical-therapist-burnout-statistics-workforce"],["SPRY (vendor blog): Finish PT notes faster with AI documentation (July 2026)","https://www.sprypt.com/blog/finish-pt-notes-faster-ai-documentation"],["St. Catherine University: PTA program accreditation and outcomes","https://www.stkate.edu/accreditation/program-accreditation/pta-accreditation"]]},
 "b152031":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is $88,940 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $57,060 and the top 10% over $159,910. Finance and insurance plus consulting firms employ about half of all analysts. Self-reported salary sites run lower and are thin (PayScale shows about $68k median from only 10 profiles in 2026), so treat them with caution. Titles like operations research scientist or applied scientist at large tech and logistics companies usually pay well above the BLS median, though exact figures vary by company.",
  growth:"BLS projects 12% growth from 2025 to 2035, much faster than the 3% average, adding about 13,500 jobs to a field of about 113,100, with about 7,500 openings a year. Industry signals mostly agree: Gurobi's 2025 optimization survey found demand for optimization professionals stayed high despite layoffs elsewhere, and more companies now employ more than one. The general entry-level job market for 2025 grads was slow, so first jobs can still take effort.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"AI already speeds up the coding and routine parts: drafting optimization models from plain-language descriptions, writing Python or solver code, tuning solver settings, preparing data, and building simple what-if interfaces for managers. Research finds these tools still struggle with complex constraints, can produce convincing but wrong models, and cannot judge if a result is truly optimal. The human core stays: framing the real business problem with decision makers, checking that a model fits reality and can actually be solved, and explaining tradeoffs. Expect fewer hours on boilerplate and more on judgment and communication."},
  sentiment:"Online OR communities mostly discuss optimization methods, simulation, supply chain work, and whether a master's or PhD is needed, which suggests degree level is a common worry for newcomers. People generally describe the work as intellectually satisfying and well paid, with the frustration that the title overlaps with data scientist and analyst roles, making the field hard to search for. Detailed first-hand mood data was thin in what we found, so this summary is partly uncertain.",
  path:"Most people start with a bachelor's in operations research, industrial engineering, applied math, statistics, computer science, or business analytics with heavy math, and many employers prefer a master's. Python, SQL, and an optimization solver are the practical skills to show. Career changers often come from data analyst, logistics, finance, or engineering jobs, and military veterans enter the field often. After a few years, paths lead to senior or lead analyst, operations research scientist, analytics manager, or consulting.",
  notes:"No license is required. INFORMS offers analytics certifications: it stopped enrolling new aCAP candidates in June 2025 and now has CAP-Essentials and CAP-Pro (no minimum eligibility) plus CAP-Expert (education and experience required). Defense and federal jobs may need a background check or security clearance. Work is mostly office based and full time, with some travel to clients or sites. Job titles vary a lot, so search under analytics, decision science, and optimization too.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Operations Research Analysts","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm"],["Food Logistics: Gurobi survey on optimization and AI (Sept 2025, summarizes vendor report)","https://www.foodlogistics.com/home/news/22950858/gurobi-survey-reveals-how-organizations-harness-optimization-and-emerging-ai-technologies"],["arXiv: Making a case for research collaboration between AI and operations research experts (2025)","https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.21076"],["INFORMS Certified Analytics Professional: aCAP and new CAP tiers","https://certifiedanalytics.org/acap"],["PayScale: Operation Research Analyst salary (small sample)","https://www.payscale.com/research/US/Job=Operation_Research_Analyst/Salary"],["GummySearch: r/OperationsResearch topic summary","https://gummysearch.com/r/OperationsResearch"]]},
 "b493093":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median is about $38k a year (May 2025 OEWS; O*NET lists $18.13 an hour, $37,710); the lowest 10% earn under about $30k and the top 10% over about $50k. Current postings are similar: a May 2026 Les Schwab tire technician job in Yakima, WA listed $14.85 to $24.50 an hour plus a quarterly bonus, health benefits, retirement, and tuition help. Commercial truck tire work and higher-minimum-wage states tend to pay more.",
  growth:"BLS projects about average growth of 3 to 4% from 2025 to 2035, with about 12,900 openings a year in a field of roughly 108,000 to 112,000 jobs, mostly from turnover. Shops report trouble finding and keeping technicians: a 2026 TechForce report cited by Tire Review says the auto service field is about 20,800 workers short each year. Tire shops keep hiring steadily, especially before winter and in spring.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"Software already handles scheduling, tire lookup, inventory, and digital inspection reports, and AI phone receptionists are spreading at shops. Robotic tire changers exist: RoboTire systems have run at a few Discount Tire stores since 2023, with the company claiming faster service, but technicians still run the process and these machines are rare. The hands-on work stays human for now: lifting wheels, mounting and balancing, finding and patching punctures, TPMS service, roadside calls, and spotting worn or damaged parts."},
  sentiment:"Workers say the job is a good way to learn hands-on skills fast, and many like their coworkers and the team pace. The most common complaints are low pay for hard physical labor, very busy shops with little downtime or skipped lunch breaks, uneven management, and pressure to upsell. Some say it is easy to move up at the bigger chains while others found no clear path, so it depends heavily on the store.",
  path:"Most people start with no experience; a high school diploma helps but is not always required, and a valid driver's license usually is. Training is on the job, from a few days to a few months. Many use it as a first step: within 1 to 3 years techs move into lube, brake, and alignment work, become automotive service technicians (often adding ASE certifications or a trade school program), or shift to sales, assistant manager, and store manager roles. Commercial truck tire service is another path.",
  notes:"The work is physically demanding: postings list frequent lifting of 35 to 75 pounds and constant squatting, bending, and kneeling, and many shops are hot, cold, or open to the weather. Big truck and off-road tires are dangerous; OSHA rule 1910.177 requires training and restraining cages for them, and TIA offers certified training courses. Seasonal rushes bring long days. Unions are uncommon in retail tire shops. Check whether the employer pays for safety gear and training.",
  sources:[["O*NET OnLine: Tire Repairers and Changers (49-3093.00)","https://www.onetonline.org/link/summary/49-3093.00"],["BLS OEWS: Tire Repairers and Changers","https://www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes493093.htm"],["Les Schwab tire technician job posting, Yakima WA (May 2026)","https://www.perrytech.edu/alumni/jobs/job_detail.html?id=26036"],["Tire Review: Ensuring your tire dealership stays OSHA compliant","https://www.tirereview.com/tire-dealership-osha-compliant/"],["Tire Review: Automotive technician shortage hits tire dealers","https://www.tirereview.com/automotive-technician-shortage-tire-dealers/"],["Dallas Innovates: A robot is changing tires at a Discount Tire store (2023)","https://dallasinnovates.com/a-robot-is-changing-tires-at-a-discount-tire-store-in-arlington/"],["Indeed: Tire Tech employee reviews","https://www.indeed.com/cmp/Tire-Tech/reviews"]]},
 "b291215":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for family medicine physicians is $244,180 a year (May 2025); OEWS puts the 25th percentile near $162k and the 90th near $429k (the 10th percentile, about $77k, likely reflects part-time work and is uncertain). Physician surveys run higher than BLS: Medscape's 2026 report averages $288,000, Doximity's 2025 report $319,000 (2024 data), and Marit about $280,000 for 0-2 years rising to $315,000 at 11+ years. Residents earn far less during the 3 training years. Family medicine is among the lower-paid physician specialties.",
  growth:"BLS projects about 3% growth for family medicine physicians from 2025 to 2035, with roughly 2,900 openings a year from about 107,500 jobs; physicians overall grow 4% with about 22,100 openings a year. Demand looks stronger than that number: the 2026 Match offered a record 5,491 family medicine residency spots, but the fill rate slipped to 83.6% with 899 unfilled, and NRMP called attracting students a growing challenge. A shortage of primary care doctors means jobs are easy to find almost everywhere.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI is already taking a big bite out of paperwork: ambient AI scribes listen to visits and draft notes. At The Permanente Medical Group, 7,260 physicians using them saved an estimated 15,791 hours of charting over 63 weeks, and most reported better satisfaction and patient conversations. AI also helps sort inbox messages and flag risks. The core stays human: examining patients, judging unclear symptoms, long-term relationships, hard conversations, and legal responsibility for diagnosis and treatment. Doctors must still review AI drafts for errors."},
  sentiment:"Family doctors often say they love their long relationships with patients and the variety of the work, but complain heavily about paperwork, insurance hassles, inbox load, and short visit times. In a 2025 Commonwealth Fund survey, 84% of U.S. primary care doctors called the job extremely or very stressful, 85% were unhappy with time spent on admin work, and 38% were unhappy with their income. Many resent pay that lags specialists and pressure from corporate or private-equity owners. AI scribes are one of the few changes drawing broad praise.",
  path:"The standard route is a bachelor's degree (any major, with pre-med science courses), the MCAT, 4 years of medical school for an MD or DO, then a 3-year family medicine residency, so about 11 years after high school. Career changers often take a post-baccalaureate pre-med program first. Licensing exams (USMLE or COMLEX) are taken during school and residency. After training, options include outpatient clinics, hospital or urgent care work, rural practice, sports or obstetrics fellowships, direct primary care, teaching, or leadership roles such as medical director.",
  notes:"Every state requires a medical license; board certification through the American Board of Family Medicine is technically optional but expected by most employers and must be maintained. Medical school debt is often large, so look at loan forgiveness programs for rural or underserved areas. Physical demands are modest, but hours run long once charting is counted. Pay varies by region and employer, so compare offers (not researched in detail here). Check contracts for productivity (RVU) targets and noncompete clauses.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Physicians and Surgeons","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/physicians-and-surgeons.htm"],["BLS OEWS: Family Medicine Physicians (May 2025)","https://www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes291215.htm"],["Physician on FIRE: Physician salary by specialty 2026 (Medscape, Doximity, Marit)","https://www.physicianonfire.com/physician-salary-by-specialty/"],["Medical Economics: Match Day 2026, record participation but a family medicine warning sign","https://www.medicaleconomics.com/view/match-day-2026-record-participation-but-a-family-medicine-warning-sign"],["AMA: AI scribes save 15,000 hours and restore the human side of medicine","https://ama-assn.org/practice-management/digital-health/ai-scribes-save-15000-hours-and-restore-human-side-medicine"],["ACDIS: 84% of U.S. primary care physicians report extreme stress (Commonwealth Fund 2025 survey)","https://acdis.org/node/69984"]]},
 "b292043":{checked:"2026-10-14",
  pay:"BLS median for paramedics is $60,600 a year (May 2025); OEWS puts the lowest 10% near $44k and the top 10% near $85k. EMTs, the step below, earn a median of $44,470. Pay varies a lot by employer: fire department paramedics and hospital or flight jobs tend to pay more than private ambulance companies, and overtime is a large share of many paychecks. EMS educators and news reports in 2026 still describe pay as low compared with other health jobs, though rising.",
  growth:"BLS projects 6% growth for EMTs and paramedics combined from 2025 to 2035, faster than average, with about 18,200 openings a year across roughly 284,100 jobs (about 102,200 paramedics). Hiring news agrees and suggests a real shortage: the American Ambulance Association reports annual turnover of 20% to 30%, some North Carolina agencies had vacancy rates near 20% in 2026, and hospitals hiring paramedics has pulled more people out of ambulance work.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is starting to help with paperwork: voice dictation and predictive text that draft patient care reports, quality review of charts, and dispatch tools that help prioritize 911 calls. Much of this is still early and pushed by software vendors. The job itself stays human: reaching patients anywhere, assessing them in chaotic scenes, starting IVs, giving drugs, managing airways, lifting and moving people, and calming families. EMS writers warn AI drafts can miss details or add errors, so medics must still review and sign their own reports."},
  sentiment:"Paramedics often describe the work as meaningful, hands-on, and never boring, and value being thanked by patients. The top complaints are low pay for the responsibility, long shifts of 12 to 24 hours, mandatory overtime, call volume, and the physical and emotional toll, including exposure to trauma. Many treat EMS as a stepping stone to nursing, physician assistant school, or fire service, and agencies report that over a third of new hires leave within a year. Burnout and short staffing are widely reported.",
  path:"Most start as an EMT, a course of a few months, and work a year or so on an ambulance. Paramedic programs then take about 1 to 2 years, with roughly 1,000 or more hours of classes, hospital clinicals, and field internship (one CAAHEP-accredited program lists 500 classroom plus 500+ field hours), and can lead to a certificate or associate degree. Then you pass the National Registry (NREMT) exam or your state exam and get licensed. Later options include critical care or flight paramedic, field supervisor, EMS educator, firefighter-paramedic, or moving into nursing or PA programs.",
  notes:"Every state requires a license; most use NREMT certification, but some, like New York, use their own exam. Expect background checks, a driver's license, and ongoing continuing education. The job has one of the highest injury rates of all occupations from lifting, kneeling, and violent scenes, plus disease exposure and mental health strain. Fire department medics are often union members (not verified here). Ask about shift length, overtime rules, and whether the employer helps pay for paramedic school.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: EMTs and Paramedics","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/emts-and-paramedics.htm"],["BLS OEWS: Paramedics (May 2025)","https://www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes292043.htm"],["American Ambulance Association: EMS shortage one-pager (Oct 2025)","https://ambulance.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/10-30-2025-EMS-Shortage-NDAA-One-Pager.pdf"],["WLOS: EMS turnover hits 20% to 30% a year as WNC agencies lean on new graduates (Feb 2026)","https://wlos.com/community/carolina-moment/carolina-moment-wnc-harris-regional-hospital-southwestern-community-college-students-community-workforce-shortage-career-medical"],["EMS1: Charting the future, how AI is rewriting the EMS narrative (Aug 2025)","https://www.ems1.com/artificial-intelligence/charting-the-future-how-ai-is-rewriting-the-ems-narrative"],["EMS1: Flattening the turnover curve (2022)","https://www.ems1.com/recruitment-and-retention/articles/flattening-the-turnover-curve-ERwaFmjlKOvSGKfr"],["SUNY Upstate / OCC: Paramedic program","https://www.upstate.edu/emergency/education/ems/paramedic.php"]]},
 "b319011":{checked:"2026-10-15",
  pay:"BLS median is $58,450 a year, or $28.10 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $33,640 and the top 10% over $100,200. Hourly pay is high but many therapists work part time, because few can do eight hours of massage a day, five days a week, so yearly income depends on how many sessions you can book and sustain. Chains and spas often pay a per-session rate or a commission (Reddit discussions cite about 30-60% of the service price), plus tips; independent therapists keep more per session but pay their own rent, taxes, and benefits.",
  growth:"BLS projects 15% growth from 2025 to 2035, much faster than average, with about 20,400 openings a year in a field of about 155,400 jobs (BLS counts the many self-employed therapists here). Openings are high partly because many people leave the field. Demand looks steady: big chains keep expanding, though some, like Massage Envy (late 2025) and Equinox (2025), are also adding robotic massage tables.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI already handles booking, reminders, intake forms, and some marketing for massage businesses. AI-guided robotic massage tables (Aescape) now run in some Equinox gyms and select Massage Envy locations, offering a clothed, self-guided session, so some quick, routine work may shift to machines. The core stays human: hands-on work that adjusts to how a body responds, judging what a client needs, and the trust and care clients pay for. Robot massage is still new and limited, so its long-term effect on jobs is uncertain."},
  sentiment:"People who stay in the work like helping clients feel better, setting their own schedule, and not needing a degree. Common complaints are low per-session pay at chains, unstable income from cancellations and slow seasons, and pressure to sell memberships or add-ons. Physical wear is a real issue: an older AMTA survey found over half of therapists had shoulder, neck, or back pain, and industry estimates (dated, uncertain) say a large share leave within a few years. Building a loyal client base is what usually makes the career work.",
  path:"Most people enter through a massage therapy program at a school or community college, which mixes classroom study (anatomy, physiology, kinesiology, ethics, business) with hands-on practice, then pass the exam their state requires. Program length and required hours vary by state. Career changers are common since no degree is needed. Many start at a spa, chain, or chiropractic office to build skills and clients, then move to private practice, medical or sports massage, teaching, or running a studio.",
  notes:"Most states require a license, certification, or registration, usually with an approved program, an exam, and sometimes a background check, CPR, insurance, and continuing education; check your state board. The work is physically demanding, with repetitive strain and fatigue the most common injuries, so body mechanics and limited daily sessions matter. About 36% are self-employed. Before taking a chain job, ask how you are paid per session and whether selling memberships is expected.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Massage Therapists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/massage-therapists.htm"],["Advance Study: Is commission-based pay better for massage therapists, according to Reddit (Sep 2025)","https://advancestudy.org/?p=460965"],["ABMP: AI-powered massages and automated massage robots (Mar 2025)","https://ten.abmp.com/updates/news/ai-powered-massages-automated-massage-robots-already-work"],["Massage Envy press release: Introduces Aescape robotic massage (Dec 2025, company release)","https://www.streetinsider.com/Press+Releases/Massage+Envy+Introduces+Aescape+Robotic+Massage+at+Select+Franchised+Locations/25686931.html"],["Spa Business: Massage Envy and AMTA self-care program, therapist strain survey (2017)","https://www.spabusiness.com/wellness-news/Massage-Envy-massage-therapist-spa-wellness-AMTA-franchise-self-care-/331370"]]},
 "b499052":{checked:"2026-10-15",
  pay:"BLS median for telecommunications line installers is $74,330 a year (May 2025), higher than other telecom technicians ($66,200 for the group). The lowest 10% earn under about $45,310 and the top 10% over about $103,680 (BLS OEWS May 2025, via Wagedex). Overtime and on-call pay can add a lot. Fiber splicers with in-demand skills can earn much more in some cities; one ISP CEO cited up to about $200,000 in 2023, but that is an employer claim, not typical pay.",
  growth:"BLS projects a 3% decline from 2025 to 2035, from about 103,400 to 99,900 jobs, as copper lines are retired and networks need less upkeep. Openings still come from retirements: BLS counts about 20,300 a year across all telecom technicians. Hiring news is more upbeat than the projection: fiber builds for the $42.5 billion BEAD program and AI data centers are short of splicers and technicians, AT&T plans to hire about 3,000 more technicians, and industry groups estimate tens of thousands of new broadband workers are needed.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI and software help with network monitoring, finding faults remotely, routing and dispatching crews, mapping, and paperwork, which can reduce some truck rolls. The core work stays human: climbing poles, pulling and burying cable, splicing fiber, working in tight or dangerous spots, and fixing problems on site in all kinds of weather. Robots are not close to doing this varied field work."},
  sentiment:"Workers like the solid pay without a degree, outdoor work, and the variety of each day. Complaints center on long hours, storm and on-call duty, weather, physical wear, and pressure on productivity. Contractor and subcontractor jobs can pay less and feel less secure than union jobs at big carriers, and Pew reports wages at the low end have stagnated. Right now fiber skills are in demand, so people with splicing experience report strong job options, especially near data center and rural broadband builds.",
  path:"A high school diploma is the usual minimum; a certificate or associate degree in electronics or telecom helps. Most learning happens on the job over several months to a few years, sometimes through union or employer apprenticeships, and short fiber courses (some just weeks) are a common way in from unrelated jobs. People often start as installers or ground helpers, move up to splicer or lead technician, then foreman, supervisor, network engineer roles, or their own contracting crew.",
  notes:"BLS says these workers have some of the highest injury rates of any occupation, mostly falls and strains, from climbing poles, ladders, and rooftops. Work involves frequent driving to job sites, so expect to need a driver's license. Vendor and fiber training help, and unions are one source of training; union and contractor jobs can differ a lot in pay and security. Expect night, weekend, and storm work. Rural broadband work may mean travel.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Telecommunications Technicians (includes line installers)","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/telecommunications-equipment-installers-and-repairers-except-line-installers.htm"],["Wagedex: Telecommunications line installers and repairers (BLS OEWS May 2025 data)","https://wagedex.com/jobs/telecommunications-line-installers-and-repairers"],["RCR Wireless: AI data centers and BEAD compete for fiber skills (Aug 2026)","https://rcrwireless.com/20260813/network-infrastructure/ai-data-center-fiber-bead-skills"],["Pew: America's plans to expand broadband could be hindered by workforce shortage (Nov 2025)","https://www.pew.org/de/about/news-room/opinion/2025/11/24/how-americas-plans-to-expand-broadband-could-be-hindered-by-workforce-shortage"],["Fierce Network: Where are all the fiber technicians (May 2023)","https://www.fierce-network.com/broadband/workforce-short-enthusiasm-where-are-all-fiber-technicians"]]},
 "b499098":{checked:"2026-10-15",
  pay:"BLS median is $39,630 a year, or $19.05 an hour (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $29,310 and the top 10% over $55,890. Pay depends a lot on the trade and industry, and overtime is common in construction and emergency repair. The bigger payoff comes later: helpers who move into skilled trades such as HVAC, industrial maintenance, or electrical work often earn far more once trained.",
  growth:"BLS projects about 2% growth from 2024 to 2034 (roughly as fast as average) with about 11,800 openings a year in a field of about 95,600 jobs; O*NET tags it a Bright Outlook occupation. The newest 2025 to 2035 figures in the app (about 5% growth, 13,600 openings) were not checked here. Hiring news for the skilled trades is strong: a 2026 Randstad analysis found job postings up sharply for HVAC and robotics technicians, driven partly by data center construction.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI may help with scheduling, parts ordering, manuals, and diagnostic tips for the technicians helpers support, but it does little of the helper job itself. Fetching and holding tools, carrying parts, cleaning equipment, setting up worksites, and simple maintenance like changing filters are hands-on tasks in messy, changing places that robots do not handle well. Reporting notes that robotics could automate some trade work in time, but likely years away."},
  sentiment:"This is usually seen as a starter job, not a long-term career: people like getting paid to learn a trade without school debt, and dislike the low pay, heavy lifting, dirty conditions, and being given grunt work. Experiences vary with the mentor; a good technician who teaches makes the job worthwhile. With trade worker shortages widely reported in 2026, many employers are eager to train reliable helpers.",
  path:"No experience is needed; a high school diploma, a driver's license, and basic shop or math skills help, and training is short and on the job. It is a common entry point from unrelated work. The usual ladder is helper, then apprentice (often 2 to 5 years of paid training), then journeyman or certified technician, then senior technician, supervisor, or your own contracting business.",
  notes:"Physically demanding: lifting, bending, standing for long periods, often outdoors or in hot, cramped spaces. OSHA safety training helps; trade licenses and certifications (such as EPA 608 for HVAC or electrical licenses) come later and vary by state. Ask whether a job leads to an apprenticeship, since some helper roles stay low-paid with no path up. BLS does not publish a detailed Occupational Outlook Handbook profile for this job.",
  sources:[["O*NET OnLine: National wages for helpers, installation, maintenance, and repair workers (BLS 2025 data)","https://www.onetonline.org/link/localwages/49-9098.00"],["Wagedex: Helpers, installation, maintenance, and repair workers (BLS OEWS and projections)","https://wagedex.com/jobs/helpers-installation-maintenance-and-repair-workers"],["Assessment.com: Helpers, installation, maintenance, and repair workers career profile","https://www.assessment.com/installation-maintenance-and-repair-careers/helpers-installation-maintenance-and-repair-workers"],["Fortune: Skilled trade demand surges, Randstad report (Mar 2026)","https://fortune.com/2026/03/20/skilled-trade-demand-randstand-report-electricans-technicans-construction-workers-six-figure-salaries-data-center-boom"],["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Installation, Maintenance, and Repair Occupations","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/installation-maintenance-and-repair/"]]},
 "b151251":{checked:"2026-10-15",
  pay:"BLS median is $100,390 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $57,710 and the top 10% over $160,460. Pay is good for those with jobs, but the title is shrinking, and many similar roles are now posted as software developer or engineer. Niche legacy skills such as COBOL on mainframes can still be in demand at banks and government agencies.",
  growth:"BLS projects a 7% decline from 2025 to 2035, from about 110,800 to 102,700 jobs, with about 4,400 openings a year, all from replacing people who leave. BLS says automation including AI will take over repetitive programming and more complex work will shift to software developers. News agrees: Fortune reported in 2025 that programmer employment fell to its lowest level since 1980, and a 2025 Stanford study found software developer jobs for ages 22 to 25 down nearly 20% from their 2022 peak.",
  ai:{rating:"High",text:"Writing code from a spec, the core of this job, is what AI coding tools do best: they now draft functions, tests, scripts, documentation, and translate old code, including COBOL, into modern languages. BLS directly cites AI as a reason for the decline. What stays human: understanding messy business needs, designing systems, reviewing and debugging AI output, keeping critical legacy systems running safely, and taking responsibility for what ships. Expect fewer pure coding jobs and more value in judgment and domain knowledge."},
  sentiment:"The mood is anxious, especially among new grads and juniors, who report many applications and few offers as companies hire less at the entry level and lean on AI tools. Experienced programmers in stable industries such as banking, insurance, and government are calmer, and some legacy specialists say their skills are scarce, though others say low pay, not scarcity, is the real problem. Many people in the field see the computer programmer title as dated and aim for software developer roles instead.",
  path:"Most enter with a bachelor degree in computer science or a related field; bootcamps and self-taught portfolios still work but are harder in the current market. Career changers often come in through IT support, QA testing, or analyst roles that involve scripting. Within a few years most move toward software developer, systems analyst, database or DevOps work, or specialize in an industry or legacy platform.",
  notes:"No license is needed; vendor or language certifications can help but experience matters more. About 15% are self-employed and remote work is common. Red flag: the title itself is declining, so build skills that transfer to software development, such as system design, cloud, testing, and working effectively with AI coding tools. Desk work with long screen time; deadlines can mean long hours.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Computer Programmers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-programmers.htm"],["Fortune: Computer programming jobs fall to lowest level since 1980 (Mar 2025)","https://fortune.com/2025/03/17/computer-programming-jobs-lowest-1980-ai"],["Constellation Research: Stanford study finds AI is eating entry-level jobs (Aug 2025)","https://www.constellationr.com/insights/news/stanford-study-ai-eating-entry-level-jobs"],["Slashdot (via Bloomberg): AI tackles aging COBOL systems as legacy expertise dwindles (Apr 2025)","https://developers.slashdot.org/story/25/04/24/1725256/ai-tackles-aging-cobol-systems-as-legacy-code-expertise-dwindles"]]},
 "b292032":{checked:"2026-10-15",
  pay:"BLS median is $96,590 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $67,820 and the top 10% over $129,370. Hospitals are the biggest employers. Pay rises with extra registry credentials (for example adding vascular or cardiac), and travel sonographer contracts often pay more than staff jobs, though we did not find a reliable current figure for travel pay.",
  growth:"BLS projects 14% growth from 2025 to 2035, much faster than average, from about 92,200 to 105,100 jobs, with about 6,000 openings a year. Drivers are an aging population and ultrasound spreading into more specialties. A 2024 study found U.S. ultrasound exams grew 55% from 2011 to 2021 while sonographers grew 44% and new graduates only 23%, and open positions rose 36%, which points to a real shortage.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI is starting to help with the routine parts: auto-measurements, suggesting scan planes, flagging possible abnormalities, and pre-filling reports. FDA-cleared tools such as UltraSight (2023) can coach a novice through a basic heart ultrasound at the bedside, which may let nurses or doctors do simple point-of-care scans. The core stays human: getting good images on hard-to-scan patients, adjusting in real time, spotting subtle findings, and calming anxious patients. Diagnosis is still made by a physician."},
  sentiment:"Sonographers often like the patient contact, the detective work of finding problems, and good pay for a two-year credential. The biggest complaint is the body: a 2024 survey of 127 sonographers presented at UltraCon found about 64% reported a work-related musculoskeletal disorder and about 76% reported burnout, and earlier studies put pain rates at 80 to 90%. Others mention heavy patient loads, short exam times, and call shifts. Shortages mean jobs are usually easy to find.",
  path:"Most people complete a CAAHEP-accredited associate's degree or certificate (about 2 years, including clinical rotations), or a bachelor's program. People who already have a health degree, such as radiologic technologists or nurses, can take a 12 to 18 month certificate. Next you pass the ARDMS SPI exam plus a specialty exam to earn RDMS, RDCS, or RVT; ARRT and CCI offer other credentials. Later steps include more specialties, lead or chief sonographer, education, or applications roles with equipment makers.",
  notes:"Only a few states license sonographers (as of 2026, New Hampshire, New Mexico, North Dakota, and Oregon), but most employers require registry credentials, so pick an accredited program; graduates of non-accredited programs may need about a year of paid work before they can sit for specialty exams. Credentials require continuing education (for ARDMS, 30 credits every three years). Expect long periods standing and reaching, which strain shoulders and wrists, plus some evening, weekend, or on-call work.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Diagnostic Medical Sonographers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/diagnostic-medical-sonographers.htm"],["Won et al., Sound the Alarm: The Sonographer Shortage (J Ultrasound Med, 2024)","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38534218/"],["AuntMinnie: Work-related MSK disorders tied to sonographer burnout (2024)","https://www.auntminnie.com/clinical-news/ultrasound/article/15668381/ultracon-workrelated-msk-disorders-tied-to-sonographer-burnout"],["Cardiovascular Business: FDA clears AI to guide point-of-care cardiac ultrasound (2023)","https://cardiovascularbusiness.com/topics/artificial-intelligence/fda-clears-new-ai-guide-point-care-cardiac-ultrasound-exams"],["Ultrasound Schools Info: Sonography certification guide","https://www.ultrasoundschoolsinfo.com/certification/"],["UNMC: Sonography certification and state licensure (2026)","https://unmc.edu/alliedhealth/academics/programs/dms/admission/certification.html"]]},
 "b492098":{checked:"2026-10-15",
  pay:"BLS OEWS data for May 2025 (from the app's BLS data) put the median near $60,000 a year, with the lowest 10% under about $39,000 and the top 10% over about $81,000. Fire alarm work with NICET certification and state licenses usually pays more than basic home security installs, and union electrical contractors may pay more again, but we did not find a solid current survey figure to confirm the gap.",
  growth:"BLS and O*NET project 7% growth from 2025 to 2035, faster than average, from about 86,300 to 92,100 jobs, with about 8,500 openings a year. Fire codes require regular inspection and testing, which keeps service work steady. Industry groups (ESA and SIA) have called the technician shortage a top business concern for years, though the articles we found are older (2017 to 2019), so current hiring conditions are less certain.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI and cloud tools are mostly changing the office side: remote diagnostics and health checks that flag faulty panels, template programming, and remote updates. Some troubleshooting can now be done without a truck roll. The core work stays human: pulling wire through buildings, mounting devices, wiring panels, testing systems to code, and passing inspections with the fire marshal. Code rules and liability make it hard to automate."},
  sentiment:"Technicians often like the hands-on work, moving between job sites, working with little supervision, and certifications that raise pay. Common complaints in reviews are weak training at some companies, long days in hot or cold attics and ceilings, on-call service calls, pressure from management, and needing NICET levels to move up. Forum and review data for this job is thin and partly old, so treat this as a general picture.",
  path:"Most start as an installer or helper with a high school diploma and learn on the job, often within a few months to a year; electrical or low-voltage classes at a trade school help. Many come from cable, IT cabling, electrical helper, or military jobs. In fire alarm work, NICET certification builds over time: Level I needs about 6 months of experience, Level II 2 years, Level III 5 years, and Level IV 10. Later steps are service lead, inspector, designer, project manager, or starting your own company.",
  notes:"Many states and cities require a low-voltage, alarm, or fire alarm license, and the NICET training center says over 20 states require NICET for fire alarm work; most government contracts also require it. Expect ladders, attics, crawlspaces, lifting, and some driving. Rules vary a lot by state, so check your state licensing board before starting.",
  sources:[["BLS OEWS: Security and Fire Alarm Systems Installers","https://www.bls.gov/oes/currenT/oes492098.htm"],["O*NET: National employment trends, 49-2098 (2025-2035)","https://www.onetonline.org/link/localtrends/49-2098.00"],["National Training Center: Who is NICET? (training provider)","https://nationaltrainingcenter.com/nicet-a-to-z/"],["SDM: ESA and SIA coordinate on workforce development (2019)","https://sdmmag.com/articles/96300-esa-and-sia-announce-coordination-on-workforce-development-strategies"],["Payscale: Fire alarm technician reviews","https://www.payscale.com/research/US/Job=Fire_Alarm_Technician/Reviews/Page-4"],["IFSEC: Texecom Cloud remote maintenance for installers (vendor news, 2018)","https://stgwordpress.ifsecglobal.com/installer-zone/texecom-cloud-launched-remote-maintenance-health-check-feature-simplified-programming/amp/"]]},
 "b291131":{checked:"2026-10-15",
  pay:"BLS median is $130,100 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $73,920 and the top 10% over $215,700. New graduates start fairly high: the AVMA 2025 senior survey reported average starting pay of about $140,000 in companion animal practice, $112,000 mixed, $100,000 food animal, and $95,000 equine. Emergency and specialty vets and practice owners often earn more. The catch is debt: the class of 2025 averaged about $212,500 among those who borrowed.",
  growth:"BLS projects 9% growth from 2025 to 2035, much faster than average, with about 3,100 openings a year. An AAVMC-commissioned study projects a shortfall of about 17,000 vets through 2032, and the AVMA reports vet unemployment of only 0.7%. Shortages are worst in rural and large-animal practice. One soft spot: the AVMA says the share of new grads entering full-time jobs has fallen each year since 2022, and about 7% of the class of 2025 had no offer at graduation.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"AI is spreading fast in clinics: in a 2024 AAHA and Digitail survey of nearly 4,000 vet professionals, 39% already used AI tools, mainly for imaging and X-ray reads, record-keeping, and voice-to-text visit notes. Expect more AI scribes, triage chatbots, and radiology screening. The hands-on and legal core stays human: physical exams, surgery, handling scared animals, judging unclear cases, and talking owners through cost and end-of-life choices. Vets in that survey worried most about AI accuracy."},
  sentiment:"Vets usually say they love the animals, the medicine, and helping families, but mental strain is a major theme: the AVMA reports moderate burnout scores, worst among early-career vets, and a third of those thinking about leaving cite mental health. Full-time vets average about 48 hours a week. Other complaints are debt versus pay, angry or grieving clients, costs owners cannot afford, and corporate consolidation of practices. Many say demand is strong and switching jobs is easy.",
  path:"The standard route is a bachelor's degree with science prerequisites (biology, chemistry), animal and vet clinic experience, then a competitive application to a 4-year DVM program, so about 8 years after high school. You then pass the national NAVLE exam and usually a state exam. Some do a 1-year internship and a 3 to 4 year residency to become board-certified specialists. Career changers often work as vet assistants or techs first. Later options include practice ownership, emergency, public health, research, or government work.",
  notes:"Every state requires a license, and few states accept another state's license directly, so moving can mean extra paperwork. Large-animal and equine vets travel to farms, work outdoors in all weather, and take emergency calls. Risks include bites, kicks, scratches, and diseases passed from animals. Heavy student debt is the big red flag; compare school costs and expected pay carefully, and look at loan repayment programs for rural or shortage areas.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Veterinarians","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/veterinarians.htm"],["Vet Candy: AVMA 2025 graduating senior survey on salaries and debt","https://www.myvetcandy.com/news/2025/10/17/inflation-erodes-veterinary-salaries-as-fewer-new-graduates-enter-full-time-work"],["Digitail (vendor blog): Veterinary stats and findings for 2026, citing AVMA and AAVMC","https://digitail.com/blog/10-veterinary-stats-and-findings-2026/"],["Veterinary Practice News: Digitail and AAHA study on AI adoption (2024)","https://www.veterinarypracticenews.com/digitail-aaha-ai-study/"],["AAVMC: Demand for and supply of veterinarians in the U.S. to 2032","https://www.aavmc.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Demand-for-and-Supply-of-Veterinarians-in-the-U.S.-to-2032-New.pdf"]]},
 "b472081":{checked:"2026-10-15",
  pay:"BLS median for drywall and ceiling tile installers is $58,930 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $38,460 and the top 10% over $102,010. Tapers (finishers) earn a bit more, with a median of $68,270. Much residential work is paid by the piece (per board or square foot), so fast workers can earn well but income swings with speed and the amount of work. Union commercial jobs tend to pay at the higher end. About 19% are self-employed.",
  growth:"BLS projects slow growth of about 2% from 2025 to 2035 for drywall and ceiling tile installers, while tapers shrink about 2%; the combined group still has about 7,600 openings a year, mostly from retirements and people leaving the trade. Contractors say workers are hard to find: in a 2025 AGC survey, 92% of firms struggled to hire and 28% were affected by immigration enforcement. Construction activity was expected to stay about flat over the following year.",
  ai:{rating:"Low",text:"Software already helps with estimating and takeoffs. Robots are arriving slowly: Canvas makes a machine that sprays and sands joint compound on big commercial jobs, and contractors and the painters union have mostly welcomed it to ease labor shortages and sanding injuries rather than cut jobs. Hanging heavy board, taping, odd shapes, patching, and final touch-ups still need people, and residential and repair work is hard to automate. Expect only small changes for years."},
  sentiment:"Drywallers often take pride in fast, visible work and like that you can earn decent money without a degree, especially on piece rate or with your own crew. Common complaints are the toll on the body (shoulders, knees, back, dust), low bids and stagnant piece rates in residential work, slow payment from builders, and swings with the housing market. Many veterans of the trade advise younger workers to aim for union or commercial jobs, estimating, or running a crew.",
  path:"Most people start as a helper or laborer with no formal credential and learn on the job, often within a year. Union and contractor-association apprenticeships last 2 to 4 years, mix paid work with classes, and usually require a high school diploma. People switch in from general labor, painting, or other trades easily. Later steps are foreman, superintendent, estimator, or starting your own drywall business; union members can also become apprenticeship instructors or business agents.",
  notes:"The work is physically demanding: lifting heavy sheets, working overhead, and using ladders, scaffolds, lifts, or stilts, with dust from sanding. Learn OSHA safety rules early. Contractor licensing rules vary by state if you start your own business. Work follows construction cycles and can slow in downturns. Watch for employers who misclassify workers as contractors or pay cash with no benefits or workers' comp.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Drywall and Ceiling Tile Installers, and Tapers","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/construction-and-extraction/drywall-and-ceiling-tile-installers-and-tapers.htm"],["Walls & Ceilings: Labor shortages mount under tariffs, immigration crackdown (2025)","https://www.wconline.com/articles/97610-labor-shortages-mount-under-tariffs-immigration-crackdown"],["ENR: More contractors see benefits of drywall finishing robot (2022)","https://enr.com/articles/53423-more-contractors-see-benefits-of-drywall-finishing-robot"],["Walls & Ceilings: Drywall, a trade to pass down or pass up? (2010 opinion)","https://wconline.com/articles/86968-web-exclusive-drywall-a-trade-to-pass-down-or-pass-up"]]},
 "b291031":{checked:"2026-10-15",
  pay:"BLS median is $76,400 a year (May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $49,090 and the top 10% over $103,720. Outpatient and government jobs tend to pay a bit more than long-term care. The Academy's own compensation survey put the average at about $79,000 in 2024, up from $70,000 in 2021. Location matters a lot: one 2026 example compares an outpatient kidney dietitian at $96,000 in New York with $68,000 in Ohio. Raises are often small, and many dietitians add side work for extra income.",
  growth:"BLS projects 8% growth from 2025 to 2035 (much faster than average), with about 5,700 openings a year in a field of about 86,300 jobs. Demand is tied to an aging population and chronic diseases like diabetes and kidney disease. Recent trade reporting points to a different problem than job supply: experienced dietitians leaving for nursing, physician assistant, sales, or tech roles, which some expect could cause a shortage of experienced dietitians within a decade (uncertain).",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"AI apps already log food from photos, estimate macros, draft meal plans, answer general nutrition questions, and help write clinical notes and reminders. That cuts into simple diet advice and wellness coaching, where many people now ask a chatbot first. The core of the licensed job stays human: full nutrition assessments, medical nutrition therapy for things like kidney disease, tube feeding, or eating disorders, building trust and lasting habit change, and taking legal responsibility for the plan. AI nutrition advice still makes errors, so a professional check is needed."},
  sentiment:"Dietitians often say they love the purpose of the work and helping patients, but pay is the loudest complaint. Many feel underpaid and overworked compared with other health professions that need similar schooling, and raises are often small or missing. Insurance pay for nutrition visits has stalled, which limits salaries. Some build private practices on the side or leave for nursing, PA, or industry jobs. Talk of pay transparency and even unionizing is growing on social media.",
  path:"Since January 1, 2024, you need at least a master's degree to sit for the Registered Dietitian Nutritionist (RDN) exam, plus an accredited program with supervised practice (often a dietetic internship). Realistically that is 5–7 years from the start of college, or 2–3 years of graduate school if you already have an unrelated bachelor's degree. Then pass the national exam and get a state license where needed. Later steps include specialty certifications (kidney, diabetes, sports, pediatrics), clinical nutrition manager, private practice, or food industry and public health roles.",
  notes:"Many states require a license; others only protect certain titles, and a few have no rules, so check your state. The RDN needs ongoing continuing education. The Certified Nutrition Specialist (CNS) is another graduate-level credential many states accept. Watch for the cost of school and internships compared with starting pay, which is the main red flag people raise.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Dietitians and Nutritionists","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/dietitians-and-nutritionists.htm"],["Today's Dietitian: The fight for fair wages (March 2026)","https://www.todaysdietitian.com/the-fight-for-fair-wages/"],["College of Saint Benedict and Saint John's University: New graduate degree requirement for dietetics","https://csbsju.edu/nutrition/curriculum/dpd/new-graduate-degree-requirement-for-dietetics"],["Medesk (vendor blog): Will AI replace dietitians?","https://www.medesk.net/en/blog/ai-to-replace-dietitians/"],["Commission on Dietetic Registration: 2024 compensation and benefits survey","https://www.cdrnet.org/2024-compensation-and-benefits-survey"]]},
 "b319093":{checked:"2026-10-15",
  pay:"BLS median is about $47,700 a year ($22.93 an hour, May 2025); the app's BLS data shows the lowest 10% near $36,000 and the top 10% near $70,000. Hospitals pay more than equipment supply companies. Evening and overnight shifts usually add a pay differential (often around 10–15%, per school and training sites). A 2026 Cincinnati-area labor report showed about $39,000 entry and $52,500 experienced, a typical range for the Midwest. Supervisors and managers earn noticeably more.",
  growth:"The app's BLS data shows about 10.8% growth for 2025–2035 and about 10,700 openings a year, with roughly 77,000 jobs; the prior BLS projection (2024–2034) was 10% and 10,900 openings, so the picture is steady (the newest figure was not confirmed directly on a BLS page). Hospitals report chronic short staffing: a September 2025 consulting report said sterile processing vacancies often stay open for months, mainly because of low pay, which agrees with strong demand.",
  ai:{rating:"Low–Medium",text:"Automation is creeping in: washers and conveyors handle more dirty instruments, computer vision and barcodes or RFID check trays, robots deliver supplies, and software predicts equipment failures. But a late-2025 industry review estimated only about 5–15% of departments worldwide use this, systems cost $20,000 to over $1 million, and most results are from pilots. People still inspect, clean, and assemble hundreds of different instruments by their instructions, run and test sterilizers, and sign off on safety. Expect AI as a checker, not a replacement, for years."},
  sentiment:"Techs often say the job is important but invisible: they keep surgery patients safe yet rarely get credit, and pay does not match the knowledge required. Common complaints are short staffing, constant interruptions, pressure from the operating room to turn trays fast, and keeping up with instructions that can run over 100 steps per device. A 2026 industry article tied this overload to burnout and errors. People who like order, detail, and working behind the scenes tend to enjoy it, and many use it as a fast way into healthcare.",
  path:"Most enter with a high school diploma, either hired as trainees or after a short certificate course (about 3–4 months, roughly $2,900–4,000 at community colleges). Many then earn the CRCST (from HSPA, which requires hands-on work hours) or the CBSPD certification. It is a common entry point from retail, food service, or warehouse jobs. Next steps are lead tech, supervisor, educator, or department manager, or moving into surgical technologist training (1–2 years) or infection prevention.",
  notes:"Certification is required by law to work in sterile processing in Connecticut, Delaware, Minnesota, New Jersey, New York, Pennsylvania, and Tennessee, and Florida, Oregon, and South Carolina have bills or rules in progress (per HSPA). The work is physical: standing all shift, lifting heavy trays, repetitive hand motion, heat, and handling sharp, contaminated instruments and chemicals with protective gear. Departments run around the clock, so nights and weekends are common.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Employment and Wages: Medical Equipment Preparers","https://www.bls.gov/oes/current/oes319093.htm"],["Wagedex: Medical equipment preparers career outlook (BLS projections and wages)","https://wagedex.com/outlook/medical-equipment-preparers"],["HSPA: Legislative certification issues (state requirements)","https://myhspa.org/?p=7477"],["Periop Leader: Report warns sterile processing staffing crisis threatens surgical safety (Sept 2025)","https://www.periopleader.com/briefs/report-warns-sterile-processing-staffing-crisis-threatens-surgical-safety/"],["Infection Control Today: Sterile processing burnout and cognitive overload (2026)","https://www.infectioncontroltoday.com/view/sterile-processing-burnout-cognitive-overload-how-interruptions-ifus-human-factors-affect-patient-safety"],["Medical Technology: Inside the new sterile frontier, AI and robots in hospital hygiene (Oct 2025)","https://medical-technology.nridigital.com/medical_technology_oct25/inside_the_new_sterile_frontier_ai_and_robots_take_over_hospital_hygiene"],["Dreambound (training school blog): Is sterile processing technician a good job?","https://dreambound.com/view/is-sterile-processing-technician-a-good-job-pros-and-cons"]]},
 "b292081":{checked:"2026-10-15",
  pay:"BLS median is $47,260 a year ($22.72 an hour, May 2025); the lowest 10% earn under $35,610 and the top 10% over $76,750. About 43% work in optometrist offices and about 43% in retail stores (general merchandise and health stores), with about 8% in physician offices. Pay tends to be higher with a state license or contact lens fitting skills, and in private or medical practices, though forum posts on this are older and anecdotal. Part-time work is common.",
  growth:"BLS projects 4% growth from 2025 to 2035 (about average), with about 5,600 openings a year, mostly from people retiring or changing jobs, in a field of about 74,300 jobs. More people need glasses as the population ages, but online eyeglass sellers and big chains compete for the simple sales. Industry leaders have long described a need for well-trained opticians, especially since formal programs are scarce; no clear 2026 hiring surge or slump was found.",
  ai:{rating:"Medium",text:"Technology is taking some tasks: apps measure pupil distance from a photo, virtual try-on and face-shape tools suggest frames, ordering systems flag odd prescriptions, and insurance checks and scheduling are automated. Online sellers already handle many simple orders without an optician. What stays human is hands-on fitting and adjusting frames, fixing fit problems, complex or progressive lenses, fitting kids and nervous first-time wearers, low-vision help, and in many states contact lens fitting. Expect fewer routine sales jobs and more value on technical fitting skill."},
  sentiment:"Opticians often enjoy helping people see better, the mix of fashion and technical skill, and steady daytime retail-style hours. The main complaints are modest pay for the skill involved, sales pressure and multitasking in chain stores (front desk, billing, inventory on top of fitting), and burnout in busy retail settings. Many say moving to a private or medical practice improved both pay and job satisfaction. Online eyeglass sales are a frequent worry on industry forums.",
  path:"Most start with a high school diploma and learn on the job, often as an optical sales associate or apprentice, over 1–2 years. Others take a 1–2 year certificate or associate degree in opticianry, though most states have no such program. Earning the national ABO certification (eyeglasses) and NCLE (contact lenses) helps everywhere and is key in states without licenses. Later steps include licensed optician, contact lens specialist, optical manager, lab or frame-sales rep, or going on to optometry school.",
  notes:"About 22 states plus D.C. license opticians, usually requiring exams and an approved program or apprenticeship; fees range from about $110 to $1,250 (2022 data). The rest have no license, so standards and pay vary widely by state. Expect evenings and weekends, especially in retail, and part-time schedules are common. Retail jobs may track sales numbers, so ask how performance is measured before accepting.",
  sources:[["BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: Opticians","https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/opticians-dispensing.htm"],["Institute for Justice: License to Work, optician licensing profile (2022)","https://ij.org/report/license-to-work-3/ltw-occupation-profile/optician"],["20/20 Magazine: The state of opticianry (2021 CE course)","https://www.2020mag.com/ce/the-state-of-opticianry"],["CareerExplorer: AI impact on opticians","https://www.careerexplorer.com/careers/optician/ai-impact/"],["OptiBoard forum: Optician salary and hourly wages thread (older posts)","https://www.optiboard.com/forums/forum/optical-forums/general-optics-and-eyecare-discussion-forum/12576-optician-s-salary-hourly-wages/page9"],["Optician Now: FAQ on ABO-NCLE certification","https://opticiannow.com/faq/"]]},
};
